      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>2 MC DOUGALL'</head>
        <p>2 MC DOUGALL'</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Emmomt (angrily) : "What are you throwing those handbills on the pavement for?" Bill-dis- tributor: "Well, guv'nor, that's what the people does as I gives 'em to. So it's only saving time." "DID you ever go to a military ball ? " asked a lisping maid of an old veteran. "No, my dear," growled the old soldier; "in those days I had a military ball come to me; and what d'ye think? It took my leg off I" MOTHER: "I trust that you are happy with your husband, Maud?" Maud: "Oh, yes as happy as one can expect to be with a man who is talking of himself half the time and of his first wife the other half."</p>
        <p>MASTER: " What ! both want a holiday to- morrow I can't spare you both at once, Mary, you can have to-morrow, and John the next day." John: "Thank you, sir, but we wanted to get married, and I'm afraid, sir, it can't be done that way, but I'll inquire." DREARY DRAGGLES (under the tree) : " Larry ! " Languid Larry (lying in the sun): "Well?" Dreary Draggles (disgustedly) : "Come here, in de shade. Out dere youse is perspirin' like as dough you wuz a common workin' man." "YES," said the lecturer, "I have no doubt your report was correct, but I appeal to you, sir. ; don't you see that it is very humiliating to read in your paper that ten persons in my lecture-room were overcome by gas? It reflects upon my ability to entertain, don't you see? You don't even qualify by saying laughing gas." MICAWBER : "There goes a man who contributes to the support of hundreds of people." Copperfield : "A philanthropist, eh ?" Micawber "No ; a manufacturer of artificial legs." THE stout man wiped his forehead. " Yes ; I was a good deal run down before I got a bicycle," he said. "But now," he added, determinedly gripping the handles and taking aim at an old lady crossing the street, "it is the other people who are run down." They removed the old lady on an ambulance, while the cyclist sped on his way rejoicing. JOHNNY: "I got a book as a prize in school to- day for havin' a good memory." Mamma: "What is the name of the book ? " Johnny: "I can't remember."</p>
        <p>UNCERTAIN.</p>
        <p>A MASSACHUSETTS maiden, appropriately dressed, Spins down the road upon her wheel in bloomers,</p>
        <p>coat and vest; • To reach the town of Wareham she started out to-day, But fears that at some turning she may have missed</p>
        <p>the way. - She hails a burly farmer, who answers to her call, And stares at wheel and costume—the bloomers most of all, "Is this the way to Wareham?" she asks, in voice _</p>
        <p>refined. _ "Blessed if I know !" he answers. wore that kind."</p>
        <p>"I never</p>
        <p>WIFE (one day after marriage) : "No, dear, don't give me any money; I might lose it." Same wife one year after marriage :—" I took a £5 note from your pocket-book last night, John." IN a very thin house an actress spoke very low in her communication with her lover. The aotor, whose benefit it happened to be, exclaimed, with a face of woeful huinour, "My dear, you may speak out—there is nobody to hear us." WiLsos : "Who is that fellow faring sumptu- ously on pheasant and champagne?" Thompson: "That is the author of 'How to Live on Six Shillings a Week '—that is, if you are fool enough to try it." "Win'," shouted the impassioned British orator, "why is it that the wife of the farmer lacks the happy, contented look to be found on the face of the bare-heade t peasant woman of the Continent?" "Because," said the man near the door, "because the bare-headed peasant woman does not have to worry all the time about her hat being on straight," "WHAT'S the matter?" asked the policeman: "haven't you any place to go ?" "Any place ter go ! " replied Meandering Mike, with contempt; "I've got the whole United Kingdom before me. I've got so many places ter go to dat it's worryin' me dizzy makin' up me mind which way ter start."</p>
        <p>DASHAWAY : "Scrooge gave up his seat in the railway-carriage to a lady this morning." Clever- ton: "Oh, come now, don't impose on my credulity." Dashaway : "Its a fa t. He told me afterwards that there was a hole in the window near where he was sitting and he was afraid of the draught." JONESY "I say, Browne, can you let me have two—" Brownie (hastily interrupting) : "I have not a single farthing, Jonesy." Jonesy ; "Two half-sov, for a sovereign?" Brownie (heartily) : "Certainly, old fellow, with pleasure. MRS. S PRATT (vigorously shaking her sleeping spouse) : "John, John! There's a burglar in the house !" Mr. Spratt (protesting) : "See here, Jane, if, instead of shaking the life out of me, you'd go and shake that burglar, you'd be doing some good." THE UscAL PRESENTS:—" James," said the village clockmaker and jeweller, "are there any weddings to take place in tha IL:i4bbourhood. soon ? " "There are two to come off nast week," said his assistant. And the jeweller sat down at once, and wrote an order on a large London firm for two dozen cases of silver teaspoons, with pr vilege of exchanging twenty-two of them for something else within thirty days. MANAGER: "Well, have you the programme all fixed for next Monday's concert?" Assistant: "The programme's all right, but there's another rovr amongst the artistes." Manager: "What are they quarrelling about now ? " Assistant : "About wh)se turn it is to be too ill to appear." A MANCHESTER man found a rough-looking indi vidnal in his cellar. "Who are you?" he deman- ded. "The gasman come to take the meter," was the :eply. "Great Scot ! " cried the householder, "I hoped you were only a burglar." " DILLAWAY : "Talk about your.aristocratic des- cent ! Why, my father often had 10,000 men under him." Caraway : "Ah ! A general ?" Dillaway : "Well, not exactly." Carraway. . A wholesale contractor?" Dillaway : "No ; an aeronaut."</p>
        <p>MoTHER : "Elsie, your sister tells me you took a second helping of pudding at Mrs. Brown's to-day?" Little Elsie: "So I did, mammy." "Do you think that was right, Elsie ? " "Yes. You know you have often told me not to contra- dict anyone, and Mrs. Brown said: I know Elsie will have a second helping of pudding,' and I could'nt contradict her, could I?" Mammy smiled, and said nothing more. JOHNSON : "That man in the corner hasn't spoken a word this evening. Perhaps he is doing all the more thinking." Jackson : "very likely. He tells me he finds it impossible to say a word in company, but he is a clever man, nevertheless. He is an author." Johnson : "Of what, pray ?" Jackson: "Why. of that famous Book, Aids to Conversation.'"</p>
        <p>SYMPATHETIC PLAIN FRIEND (to inconsolable young widow): "The last time I met your dear husband he stopped and spoke to me with such a funny greeting that I was the happier for it all day long." Young Widow (still oblivious to every- thing except her load): " Yes, that was just like dear David. There was no woman so humble, or homely, or unattractive, or dull, but that he could find something pleasant to say to her, and would take pains to say it." FIRST OLD GENTLEMAN: "Let me see. Yes, I met your .nephew five years ago, and, if you must know the truth, I was disgusted with him—such a vain, conceited, insufferable puppy I never saw in my life." Second Old Gentleman: "Oh, he's changed completely now. He's the most modest fellow you could find in a day's journey—he doesn't believe he knows anythir g." "You don't say so! Well, now I think of it, when I met him he was an undergraduate at Cambridge." " Yes ; and now he's a graduate, and trying to earn his own living." THEY were on the beautiful Thames, just above Richmond. The night we. balmy, and for a time the conversation had lagged. Suddenly the young lady remark'-d: "The river seems much lower to- night than lust, night." I did not notice it. How do you tell?" ", ell, can't you see that the watev is no:. so near the top of the boat "But last night there were fourin the boat, and to-night we are all alone." "Well, I don't see how that could make the river higher or lower." He was too dumbfounded to answer, so he changed the conversation.</p>
      </div>
