      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>P ATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR MAKES THE BEST PASTRY</head>
        <p>P ATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR MAKES THE BEST PASTRY</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAREST NELLIE, Although some of the newest millinery is of a really monstrous description, there are smart, pretty, and becoming hats to be had. A very pretty hat is black with a crown of pink miroir velvet, a lovely shade. The brim is draped with cloudy tulle. There are three curled black ostrich feathers, and an osprey at the side, and a " cache- peigne " of gardenias and foliage.</p>
        <p>ier pretty black hat has a quaint arrange- ment of puffed rose pink centre and black satin bows. A very becoming hat is of drawn green lisse, trimmed with cream lace' trails of shaded ivy, and large soft-tinted velvet tulips, while right at the back were two black ostrich tips. For everyday wear in summer, nothing is nicer than a fine serge or twill suiting, which always looks neat and ladylike, and resists the dust wonderfully. Among other specialities is the new lustre, which looks appreciably like alpaca, this being excellent for morning gowns, when washing fabrics are undesirable. "The Duchess" is woven of pure wool, and dyed to most attractive colours, ir.cluding pretty bright and dark greens, fawn, beaver tints, various blue shades, and poppy-red and old-rose, affording a fine variety.</p>
        <p>A most becoming costume for slight women is a costume in chaudron cloth, double-breasted, with fine steel buttons; square revers of white cloth braided. This style of revers is much to be worn during the season. Very dainty and artistic are the new washing costume materials, particularly the grass lawn, which figures in various tints, varying from quite a deep cream tint to the deep string-colour, re- sembling a very dark holland. Cream and 4cru are undoubtedly the most popular shades, and these are shown with openwork embroideries, stitched with white, or with sprays in colour, powdered over the background. The darker grass lawns can be worn an entire season, and when embroidered are dressy enough to be made up on silk.</p>
        <p>A very useful gown is of string coloured grass lawn: with tiny stripe in pink embroidery, which is made up on a silk slip, the skirt quite separate from its lining and finished with little frills of the lawn. The bodice has a V opening, in pinafore style, leaving the silk uncovered, and the ribbons at neck, waist, and sleeves are of the • same colour as the lining. Narrow insertion lace or coloured passementerie may be used round the V opening, and the dress is smart and dressy enough for any function. As a matter of fact, this season, the difference between the blouse and bodice is so minute as to be almost imperceptible, but I may safely assure you that for bettermost occasions it is as well to let the fact be apparent that your bodice is intimately connected with your skirt, the only exception to this rule being in the matter of chiffon bodices, or blouses if you prefer to call them so, which are still well worn, always supposing that they are of the same colour as the skirt; therefore, if you would be economical, you may safely provide yourself with a skirt of striped silk or moire, and wear it will a bodice of chiffon; one for instance, trimmed with jet, another perhaps with an elaborate zouave jacket, of the new gold and jet- worked grass-lawn, and a third trimmed per- chance with a waist band and neck-band of gold jewelled galon. Your loving •</p>
