      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>It would be premature, no doubt, to say that the Government have decided to drop the greater part of the Education Bill. The time has not yet arrived for taking a formal decision on the subject. But it is absolutely certain that in responsible quarters on the Unionist side all hope has virtually been abandoned of carrying the measure in its present shape.</p>
        <p>It may be taken for granted, therefore, that a portion of the Bill will be dropped and the ques- tion is, Which of the proposals are the Government ready to abandon? There is a rumour which, if it proves to be true, points to great simplifica- tion of tee difficulties in the way of the Bill. The Government, it is said, are prepared, if they cannot see their way to carry the measure as a whole, to abandon—or rather to hold over—those provisions of the measure which aim at the new creation of a new educational authority. This course would at one swoop reduce the Bill from a revolutionary measure of first-rate importance to a third-rate bill. The increase of the grant would still be strongly fought by the Opposition as bad in prin- ciple, while on the other hand the Churchmen would no doubt endeavour to increase the amount. But the opposition to an attenuated measure of this sort would be of a very different kind from that which the Li'serals are preparing to offer to a bill which, as it stands, threatens to deal a decided blow at the educational system of the country.</p>
        <p>There remain now only ten weeks till the middle of AuLust, the date which Mr. Balfour, in his fond dream of a short session, had fixed for the raising of the House. Whatever happen&amp; between this and the opening of the grouse shooting,the Government are quite determined not to let their programme be wrecked by the due discussion of their measures, or, as they call it, obstruction. It cannot. be too deeply impressed on the public mind that the wholesale application of the closure is to be resorted to, and that all,these Bills are to be forced uncon- sidered,through Parliament for no other reason than that the landed'gentlemen who sit behind the Trea- sury Bench may get off to the moors early in Au- gust.</p>
        <p>The lovers of sensation are to be denied the luxury of revelling in thrilling newspaper accounts of the executions at Newgate on the 9th and 10th of June. The press is not to be admitted, and its representatives will have to be content with such details as they can pick up from the officials present. It is well that the public should be left in ignorance of the details of the revolting scene. The house of the Muswell Hill victim has been sold by private bargain for .C 1,500 and the family that occupies it, with a knowledge of the circumstances, must hsva anme nerve. .</p>
        <p>The chess tournament between members of the House of Commons continues. There are sixteen players—eight on each side of the house. Each couple is to play three games. Out of twelve games played so far, eleven have been won by members of the Opposition. Mr. F. Wi!son, Mr. C. Shaw, Mr, Allienna, and Mr. Bryn Roberts have won two games each,and Mr. Atherley-Jones, Dr. Clarke, and Mr. Alien one game each. The only Ministerialist who has won a gam. is Mr. George Whiteley. Sir E. Ashmead- Bartlett, Sir H. Maxwell, Mr. Side- bottom, and Mr. Vicary Gibbs have each lost twice, and Mr. Bonsor, Mr. Seton-Karr, and Mr. G. White- ley once each.</p>
        <p>The Bulldog Club's three days' show at the Aqua- rium afforded a further instance of the growing popularity of the national breed, the entries being considerably in excess of former records. As to quality also there was little to be desired. Several of the competitors added appreciably to lists of honours already long. Mr. Pybus Sellon's Donax and Mr. Eumorfopoulo's Forlorn repeated triumphs recently won in Manchester, Mr. G. R. Sims secured fresh awards with his young dog Barney Barnato, and Mr. S. Woodiwiss did likewise with his high- bred Blackberry. The sensation of last year's dis- play, Queer-street, was shown by a justly proud owner, while the famous Cigarette appeared as an ornament to the exhibition—not as a competitor. Mr. Reid's Magic..well known as one of the best bull bitches from the North of England. was prominent among her fellows, and a good example of the Shef- fl,ld strain was furnished by Mr. Olivei's Save Monarch. The puppy classes were exceptionally promising, and a few splendid litters containing sound bargains were strong centres of attraction. Taken as a whole, the exhibition was one of un- usual interest, if only for the reason that nearly every animal on the benches had obtained awards at home and Continental shows. Under such cir- cumstances the task which awaited the judges was one needing the nicest discretion.</p>
        <p>Is thereto be a revolution in the manners of mag- istrates ? Very quietly was it ineuriurated, if it is to be, down in Sussex the other day. A county magnate, bearing a name which belongs to the nation, listening to a long or se, quietly took forth his pipe from his pocket and lit it. There may be a les of dignity—some will say there is. But the accused person upon his trial will be the last, to complain, one imagines, of the soothing effect of the pipe of peace upon the nerves of the great un- paid. • TI ere never was a more ropulnv win of the Blue Riband of the Turf than that of W-dnesday by the Prince of Wales. The Princess of \Vales is known to have pinned her faith to Persimmon's chances throughout, and she is reported to have expressed a strong hope that it would not rain, for then the horse would win. The Princess Maud's marriage dot is likely to be considerably enlarged by the event, and although the Hirsch millions did not come into Marlborough House, to-day's win will bring a splendid sum to the Prince and his house- hold. who followed Persimmon throughout the whole of his training. To show the popular feeling in L,ndon, it may he mentioned that ten minutes after the race was known to have been won there was a large crowd of cheerers outside Marlborough House. There was another crowd at Victoria to see the Prince and Princess return from Epsom,and the comfratulatory applaii we immense.</p>
        <p>According to the Sociali-t.? leaders, the Inter- national Congre-s to be held here shortly will be the most important ever held in the history of the so-called international movement: Every European country with the exception of Turkey will be re- presented, and that, too, by the most distinguished men in the Socialist rnnks. At the Congress three languages will be used—English, Fre!tch, end Ger- man,-7-and this will necessitate the translation of papers and speeches into two. Steps are being taken to prevent any friction arising over the selec- tion of the daily chairman—a matter which has certainly not always been attended with harmoni- ous feelings te, these gatherings—and to obtain the nomination of men who have an international reputation. The results of Wednesday's elections have caused no little concern to the supporters of the Govern- ment. Of course, with the large majority they have behind them, Ministers can well afford •o spare a couple of seats, but the circumstances of the contests give them a significance quite apart from the effect which the Liberal gsins will have on the relative positions of parties in the House. There seems little doubt that Mr. Barlow's victory is tantamount to a vote of censure on the Education Bill. The Bill was kept steadily to the front throughout the contest, and it is freely admitted by Ministers that no other subject had an equal in- terest for the electors. In all there has been a turnover of somethieg like 400 votes since last year. It is said that 200 may be accounted for by the action of the Frome Nonconformist Unionists, who as a body went back to the Liberal colours rather than support a Government responsible for this Bill. But the increass in the 'Liberal poll shows that other forces ba—e been called into ac- tion. There can he now no doubt that there was a large number of Liberal abstentions at the general election, probably on the part of Liberals who did not reel very keenly on the Irish question, which has never aroused the interest in Sornersetshire which it has done in some other puts of the coun- try. The measure at present before the House of Commons has, however, stimulated interest afresh in the conflict of parties, and they have insisted on giving the Government a lesson which is likely be emphasised by a good many Iy-elections. Sir Howorth's letter in the "Times " opportunely throws light on the feeling of the Conservatives in the House of Commons who have not subjected themselves to the discipline of silence which has been accepted by the bulk of the Chureh party, The truth is that the Conservative and Liberal Un- ionist alliance on the education Tiestion has been from this point of the flimsiest cThnrncter. Concern- ing the contest in the Wick district, which has had an equally notable result, there is much more mystery, but one thing may be said with certainty --that the Tory headquarters did not expect such a beating. The Ministerial majority counts four votes less on a division henceforward. Truly a good day's work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>CARD</head>
        <p>A CARD</p>
        <p>NOWN as Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Pre.cription is the only original guaranteed Remedy for all those who suffer from nervous weakress, exhausted vitality- kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak• ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped nvelope to Jos. HOLMES' Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man eons. Bloomsbury Square, London, and get full particu'ars of this great specific free of charge. Mention whether for male or female, Beware of fraudllent imitators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The terrible news from Moscow goes yet again to show that nothing is more difficult than satisfactorily to ar- range for the distribution of souvenirs. Many of us know from experience that even on a gala night in a theatre it leads to complication, but when it is a case of three quarters of a million of people struggling to get at booths on an immense plain without any adequate police arrangements, e cannot wonder that there was a catas- trophe. Yet, that such a terrific crush, and loss of life, should have occurred for the sake of a cotton printed handkerchief, an ornamented tin mug, a sausage, a gingerbread cake, and a bagful of nuts and sweets, is enoueh indeed to make us marvel. It appears the Czar and Czarina are shortly going to make a tour through Europe, storping at the different courts ea route for England. Extensive preparations are already being made at Berlin, also at Darmstadt, the Czarina's old home.</p>
        <p>Princess Maud is to receive,ag wedding gifts,a private omnibus from the borough ef King's Lynn, and a vic- toria from the city of Norwich. Up to this date there is no talk of a motor car, but it is not improbable this particular form of conveyance may be numbered among Her Royal Highness's presents. By the way one of Her Majesty's many wedding gifts to her granddaughter is a very handsome diamond and sapphire tiara and necklace.</p>
        <p>The forthcoming marriage of Viscount Milton and Lady Maud Dundas, which is to take place on the 24th of this month at St. Paul's, is likely to be a very in- teresting occasion, as weddings in the Cathedral have been exceedingly rare during this century. Lady Maui is a daughter of the Marquis and Marchioness of Zet- land, and Viscount Milton is a grandson of Earl Fitz- william. He is moreover the youngest member of the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>It is somewhat difficult to decide just now whether the skirts of our new gowns shall be made to come up over the bodice and be worn with a belt or a satin sash, or whether the bodice shall be finished with a round basque and worn over the skirt. Either mode is correct but short wasted women will do well to choose the latter, on account of the long line it gives. Sleeves continue to grow narrower, and there is very little doubt now,than an end has really come to the huge specimens we have so long indulged in. This fact we cannot any of us regret. At ball, dinner party, or theatre, we shall find it an immense relief not to have the care of our sleeves constantly uppermost in our minds and when donning a coat, the absence of the huge puffs we have solonE had to stow away as best we could, will indeed appear a blessing to be thankful for.</p>
        <p>The rage of the Marie Antoinette fichus still continues —usually they are of lace, or silk muslin edged with frills. The long ends falling in front almost to the feet however, are much more artistic than the shorter ones that pass round the waist and fastm at the back. I would strongly urge my readers to beware of the latter mode, for this particular form of fichu seems to have no connection with anything, and suggests apron strings rather than ornamental drapery. Narrow lace, much of it real and costly, but more of it imitation, is just now being used by the mile to trim the skirts of light summer gowns, which are covered with tiny tucks sir flounces, each of these being edged with narrow Valenciennes, torchon, or euipure. Really the perfection to which the imitation laces have been brought this season is indeed marvellous, and the novice may well be deceived by the beauty of the fabric and the exact reproduction of the varied stitchery. It would appear that the time is coming when the bicycle will be as much a house on wheels as is a ca ra- van. I have just seen one possessed of a canopy, a kind of roof, and blinds, made of linen, and fixel t a the handle bar by mans of a metal rod. The blinds on either side and at the back come down to the rider's ankles, and the front was of green gauz, reaching to the handle bar. Thus the rider was sheltered from the sun and insects. But, whether riding in this sort of shelter is worth the cost of a cycle and the trouble of mounting, is the question. It always seems to me that the rush of air around the cyclist, the freedom of move- ment and of vision constitute one of the greatest charms of cycling. By the way, women cyclists who desire to appear distinctly up to date, must be followed at a respectful distance by a male pethilist in the family livery. The other day I noticed a well-known society leader riding down Bond-st. when the traffic was at its thickest. The lady seemed quite at her ease,as she skil- fully threaded her way amid the throng of vehicles, but her attendant looked anxious, and was obviously over- burdened by a sense of his responsibilities. fn America, matches made of paper are the lltest in- vention. The paper is rolled spill-fasnion.then immersed in wax, stearine, and similar substances, when it burns with a bright,smokeless, and odourless fluid. It is then cut into match lengths and dipped inta phosphorous wax,like the common wax match. As the wood used in the manufacturing of matches grows more scarce and costly year by year,the invention is regarded as a timely one.</p>
        <p>Some of the newest lamp shades are ind,,ed works of art. At a dinner party the other night, I espied one specially lovely creation made to fit a corner. It was in the shape of an oyster shell,of fluted silk, in a perfect shade of apricot pink. It was trimmed with a feathery ruche of frayed silk. Artificial flowers enter largely into the composition of lamp shades this year, and are renewed according to the season. With regard to the new artificial silk,this is only being used for the vreft,in producing workable material. The manufacture is unlikely to be undertaken in England as alcnhol is used in the production,and the duty on alcohol is here,-t heavy one. In Switzerland where artificial silk is now being made,the duty is much lowcr, and the silk itself to he used for weft, can be sold at 44. per pound. The wreat question that now remains to he solved is—will it dear as well as the silk of the silk- worms?</p>
        <p>'Those who are possessed of last season's white straw hats will doubtless be glad just now of a capital re- ceipt, whereby it may be cleaned and made to look as good as new. Take one pennyworth of powdered yellow sulphur, and mix it with a piece of lemon. With a piece of linen rub this mixture well into the hat. Take another piece of linen wrung out of cold water. rub the hat with this, and hang up to dry. Leave for 24 hours then brush the hat well to get out the sulphur powder. This makes the hat a pretty creamy colour.</p>
        <p>An excellent method whereby to get rid of cockroaches is to place a deep tin vessel with perfectly smooth sides in the middle of their run and put some sweet cake at the bottom. 'Then a lath or two from the floor to the top of the tin should be arranged. The roaches milt run up the laths to get to the cake, and dropping over can- not get out again. I have known houses badly infested with them, absolutely cleared by this means. Gelatine Blancmange is delicious and inexpensive. Take a pint of milk, place it in a clean bright saucepan with the thinly-parFd rind of one lemon and two ounces of lump sugar. Take care it does not boil over or burn. Melt half an ounce of gelatine in about half a gill of hot water. Then stew into the milk. Flavour with vanilla or lemon, and strain all into a mould that has been well rinsed out with eolB water. It may be col-aired a pale pink with cochineal if preferred.—" Madge."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
