      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Y JUNE 6. 1896 THE HIDDEN HAND;</head>
        <p>Y JUNE 6. 1896 THE HIDDEN HAND;</p>
        <p>CAPITOLA THE IVIADOA2. BY Mits. E. D. E. N. SOUTIIWO,"1 U.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XL/. aLN UNEXPECTED VISITOR AT MARAII4S (I.I,ST•NIK. "'Friend, wilt thou give me shelter herer The stranger meekly saith ; My life is haunted; evil men Are following on my path."</p>
        <p>Marah Rocke sat by her lonely fireside. The cottage was not changed in any respect since the day upon which we first of all found her there. There was the same bright little wood fire; the same clean hearth, and the identical faded carpet on the floor. There was the dresser with its glis- tening crockery ware on the right, and the sheives with Traverse's old school-books on the left of the tire-place. The widow herself had changed in nothing ex- cept that her clean black dress was threadbare and rusty, and her patient face whiter and thinner than before.</p>
        <p>And now there was no eager restlessness; no frequent listening and looking towards the door. Alas I she could not now expect to hear her boy's light and springing step and cheerful voice as he hurried home at eventide from his daily work. Traverse was far away at St. Louis undergoing the cares and trials of a friendless young physician trying to get into practice. Six months had passed since he took leave of her, and there was as yet no hope of his returning even to pay a visit. Sc. Marah sat very stilt and sad, bending over her needlework, without ever turning her head in the direction of the door. True, he wrote to hr every week. No Wednesday ever passed without bring- ing her a letter written in a strong, buoyant and encouraging strain. Still she missed Traverse very sadly. It was dreary to rise up in the empty house every morning; dreary to sit down to her solitary mea!s, and drearier still to go to bed in her lonely room without having received her boy's kiss anti heard his cheerful good-night. And it was her custcm every night to read over Traverse's last letter before retiring to bed. It was getting on towards ten o'clock when she folded up her work and put it away, and drew her boy's latest epistle from her bosom to read. It ran as follows:</p>
        <p>- "ST. Lours, Dec. 1, 184—. "Mv DEAREST MOTHER,-1 am very glad to hear that you continue in good health, and that you do not work too hard, or miss me too sadly. It is the greatest comfort of my life to hear good news of you, sweet mother. I count the days from one letter to another, and read every last letter over daily until I get a new one. You insist upon my telling you how I am getting on, and whether I am out of money. lam doing quite well, ma'am, and have some funds left! I have quite a considerable practice. It is true that my professional services are in request only among the very poor, who pay me with their thanks and good wishes. But lam very glad to pay cif a small part of the great debt of g.-atitude I owe to the benevolent of this world by doing all that I can in my turn for the needy. And even t I had never myself been the object of a good man's benevolence, I should still have de- sired to serve the indigent; "for whoso giveth to the poor lendeth o the Lord," and I "like the security." Therefore, sweet mother of mine, be at ease, for I am getting on swimmingly—with one exception. Still Ido not hear from our Clara. Six months have now passed, during which, despite of the seeming silence, I have written to her every week ; but not one letter or message have I received from her in return! And now you tell me also that you have not received a single letter from her either. I know not what to think Anxiety upon her account is my one sole trouble. Not tha., I wrong the dear girl by one instant's doubt of her constancy—no; my soul upon her truth! ill could do that I should be most unworthy of her love. No, mother, you and I know that Clara is true. But, ah, we do not know to what sufferings she may be subjected by Le Noir, who I firmly believe has intercepted all our letters. Mother, I am about to ask a great, perhaps an unreasonable, favour of you. It is to go (Hawn into the neighbour- hood of the Hidden House and make inquiries, and try to find out Cldra's real condition. If it be pos- sible, put yourself into communication with her, and tell her that I judge her heart by my own and have the firmest faith in her constancy, even though I have written to her every week for six months, without ever having received an answer. I feel that I am putting you to expense and trouble, but my great anxiety about Clara, which I am sure you must share, mu,t be my excuse. I kiss your dear and honoured hands, and remain ever, " Your loving son and faithful servant, "TRAVERSE ROCKE."</p>
        <p>"I must try to go. It will be an awful expense, because I know no one down there, and I shall have to board at the tavern at Tip Top while I am making inguiries —for I dare not approach the dwelling orGabriel Le Noir I' said Marah Roche, as she folded up her letter, and replaced it in her bosom. Just at that moment she heard the sound of wheels approach, and a vehicle of some sort draw up to the gate, and some one speaking without. She went to the door, and listening, heard a girl- ish voice say: "A dollar?—Yes, certainly; here it is. There you may go now." She reeognised the voice, and with a cry of joy jerked toe door open just as the carriage rolled away. And the next instant Clara Day was in her arms.</p>
        <p>"Oh, my darling my darling! my darling! is this really you ? Re lly, really you, and no dream cried Marah Rocke, all in a flutter of excitement, as she strained Clara to h€ r bosom. "Yes, it is I, sweet friend; come to stay with you a long time, perhaps," said Clara, softly, re- turning her caresses. " Oh, my lamb ! my lamb ! what a joyful sur- prise! rise Ido think I shall go crazy? Where did • 3,0 u come from, my pet? Who came with you? 'When d d you start? l)id Le Noir consent to yo 'r coming? And how did it happen ?—But, - dear child, how worn and weary ye. look. You must be very tired. Have you had supper? Oh, my darling I come and lie down on this soft lounge, while I put away your things and get you some refreshment,' said Matah Rocke in a delirium of joy, as she took oil Clara's hat and. sacque, and laid her down to rest on the lounge, which she wheeled up near the fire.</p>
        <p>"Oh, my sweet, we have been so anxious about you Traverse and myself. Traverse is still at St. Louis, love, getting on slowly, lie has written to you every week, and so Weed have I, but we neither of us have so much as one letter in reply. And yet neither of us ever doubted your true heart, my child. We know h.t the letters must have been lost, miscarried, or intercepted," said Mara", as she busied herself putting on the 'teakettle.</p>
        <p>"They must indeed, since my experience in regard to letters exactly corresponds with yours. I have writien every week o both of you, yet never received one IIAC in reply from •either," said Clara. "We know it; we said so. Oh, those Le Noirs those Le Noirs ! !Int, my darling, .yet are per- leetly exhausted, and though I have aske t 3on a halt a Ii ndred questions, you shall not reply to one of them; .nor talk a bit more until you have rested and hal refreshment. Here, my love, here is Traverse's last letter It will amuse you to lie and read it while I am getting tea," said March, taking the paper from tier bosom and anding it to Clara, and then' placing tlfe stand with the light near the lead of her cou,h, that. she might se to read it without rising. And while Clara, we I pleased, perused and Bridled over her lover's letter, Murals Rocke laid the cloth and spreau a del eate repast of tea, mii k-• toast and poached eggs, of which sue tenderly pressed her visitor to partake. . And when Clara was someltihat refreshed by food and rest, she .said : "Now dear mamma, you will wish to hear how it happens that I am with you to-night." - -</p>
        <p>"Not unless you f el quite rested, dear girl." "I am rested sufficiently for the purpose' be- sides I am anxious to tell you. And oh, dear mamma I I could just now sit in your lap, and lay my head upon your kind, soft bosom so willin,4ly." " Come, then Clara. Come, then my darling," said Marah, tenderly, holding out her arms. " No, no, mamma, you are too little, it would be a sin," said Clara, smiling ; "but I will sit by you and put my hand in yours, amid rest my head against your shoulder while I tell you all about it."</p>
        <p>".Come, then my darling," said Maran Roche.</p>
        <p>Clara took the -otlered -seat, and wool she was nxed to her liking, she commenced and related to her friend a full history of all that occurred to her at the .Hidden House, from the moment that she had first crossed its thresh Ad to the hour in which, through the courage and addres4 of Capitoli, she was delivered from imminent peril. "And now," said Clara, in conclusion, "1 h-vo come hither in order to get Dr. 'Williams to make one more appeal for me to the Orphans' Court. And when it is prove 4 what a traitor pay_ guardian has</p>
        <p>..;een to his trust, I have no doubt that the judge will some one else in his place, or at lez3st father's last wish in regard to Illy inee is carried into effect." Heaven grant it, my child Heaven grant it those Le Isioirs ! those Le Noirs ! were there sr in the world be-ore such ruthless villains and :+4.....kipliBhed hypocrites !" said Marah Roche, clasiAng her hands in the strength of her emotions. A long time yet they talked together, and then they retired to bed, and still talked until they fell r.sleep in each other's arms. The next morning the widow arose early, gazed a little while with delight upon the sleeping daughter of her heart, pressed a kiss upon her cheek so softly as not to disturb her rest, and then, leaving her still in the deep, sweet sleep of wearied youth, she went down stairs to get a nice break- fast.</p>
        <p>Luckily a farmer's cart was just passing the road before the cottage on its way to market. Marah took out her little purse from her pocket, hailed the driver, and expended half her little store in p rchasing two young chickens, some eggs, and some dried peaches, saying to herself: "Dear Clara always had a good appetite, and h cal thy young human nature must live substantially, in spite of all its little heart-aches." Whi e M arah was preparing the chicken, for the gridiron, the door at the foot of the stairs opened, and Clara came in, looking after her night's rest as fresh as a rosebud. _ _</p>
        <p>_ " What ! up with the sun, my darling !" said Marah, going to meet her. "Yes, mamma. Oh! it is so good to be here with you in this nice, quiet place, with no one to make me shudder. But you must let me help you, mamma. See ! I will set the table and make the toast."</p>
        <p>Oh, Miss Clara-" "Yes, I will I have been ill-used and made miserable, and now you must pet me, mamma, and let me have my own way, and htlp you to cook our little meals and to make the house tidy, and after- wards to work those button-hole t in the shirts you were spoiling your gentle eyes over last night. Oh if they will only let me stay here with you and be at peace, we shall be very happy together, you and I!' said Clara, as she drew oat the little table and laid the cloth.</p>
        <p>'My dear child, may the Lord make you as happy as your sweet affection would make me 1" said Marah.</p>
        <p>" We can work for our living together," con- tinued Clara, as she gaily flitted about from the dresser to the table, placing the cups and saucers and plates—" you can sew the seams and do the plain hemming, and I can work the button-holes and stitch the bosoms, collars and wristbands. And if the worst comes to the worst,' we can hang out our little shingle before the eottage gate, in- scribed with :</p>
        <p>MRS. ROCKE AND DAUGHTER,</p>
        <p>SHIRT MAKERS. Orders executed with neatness and despatch.</p>
        <p>"We'd drive a thriving business, mamma, I assure you," said Clara, as she sat down on a low stool at the hearth and began to toast the bread.</p>
        <p>" I trust in Heaven thal it will never come to that with you, my dear." - " Why ? why, mamma? why should I not taste of toil and care as well as others a thousand times better than myself? Why should not I work as well as you and Traverse, mamma? I stand upon the broad plat' orm of human rights, and I say I have just as good a right to work as others," said Clara, with a pretty assumption of obstinacy, a,s she placed the plate of toast upon the board. "Doubtless, dear Clara, you may play at work just as much as you please; but heaven forbid you should ever have to work at work 1" replied Mrs. Rocke, as she placed the coffee-pot and the dish of broiled chicken on the table.</p>
        <p>"Why, mamma? Ido not think that is a good prayer at all. That is a wicked, proud prayer, Mrs. Marah Rocke. Why shouldn't your daughter really toil as well as other people's daughters, I'd like to be informed ?" said Clara, mockingly, as they both took their seats at the table. "1 think, dear Clara, that you must have con- tracted some of your eccentric little friend Capi- tola's wa4s, from putting on her habit. I never saw you in such gay spirits," said Mrs. Rocke, as she poured out the coffee.</p>
        <p>"-Oh, mamma, it is but the glad rebound of the free bird! lam so glad to have escaped from that dark prison of the Hidden House, and to be here with you! But tell me, mamma, is my old home occupied ?" "No, my dear; no tenant has been found for it. The property is in the hands of an a-!ent to let; but the house remains quite vacant and deserted." "Why is that ?" asked Clara. " Why, my love, for the strangest reason. The foolish country people say that since the doctor's death, the place has been haunted."</p>
        <p>"Haunted !" "Yes, my dear, so the foolish people say, and they cot wiser ones to believe them." "What exactly do they say? I hope—l hope they do not trifle with my dear father's honoured name and memory !" "Oh, no my darling—no; but they say that although the house is quite empty and deserted by the living, strange sights and sounds are heard and seen by passers-by at night. Lights appear at the upper windows from which pale faces look out." "How very strange !" said Clara. "1 es, my dear, and these stories have gained such credence that no one can be fouud to take the house."</p>
        <p>"So much the better, dear mamma, for if the new judge of the Orphan's Court should give a de- cision in our favour, as he must, when he hears the evidence, old and new, you and I can move right into it, and need not then enter the shirtmaking line of b-siness."</p>
        <p>"Heaven grant it, my dear. But now, Clara, my love, we must lose no time in seeing Doctor Williams, lest your guardian should pursue you here and give you fresh trouble." Clara assented to this, and they immediately arose from the table, cleared away the service, put the room in order, and went up stairs to put on their bonnets—Mrs. Rocke lending Clara her own best bonnet and shawl. When they were quite ready they locked up the house and set out for the town.</p>
        <p>It was a bright, frosty, invigmating winter's morning, and the two -friends walked rapidly until t. ey rewled Doctor Williams's house. The kind old mill was at home, and was much vrprised and pleased to see his visitors. He in- vited them into his parlour, and when he had heard their story, he said : .</p>
        <p>"This is' a much more serious affair than the other. We must employ counsel. Witnesses must be brought from the neighbourhood of the Hidden House. You are aware that the late judge of the.Orplians' Court has been appointed to a hi:Jt office under the government at Washington. The man that has his place is a person of sound integ- rity who will do his duty. It regains only for us to prove the justice of our cause to his satisfaction and all will be well."</p>
        <p>"Oh. I trust in Heav is that it will be,' said Marah fervently. "You two must stay in my house until the affair is decided. You might possibly be safe from real injury ; but you could not be tree from moles- tationin your unprotezted conditionat the cottage," said Doctor Wil jams.</p>
        <p>Clara warmly expressed her thanks. "fou had better go home now and pack up what you wish to bring, and put out the fire and close up the hose, and come here immediately. "In the meantime I will see your dear father's solicitor and be ready with my report by the time you get back," said Doctor Williams, promptly taking his hat to go. Mrs. Rocke and Clara set out for the cottage which they soon reached. Throwing off her bonnet and shawl, Clara : " Now, ma ; ma, the very first thing I slll do will he to write to Traverse, so that we can sell,' the letter by to-day's mail, and set his "rind at rest. I shall .simnly tell him that our mutual letters have failed to reach their d stination, but that I am now on a visit to you, and that while remain here nothing can interrupt our corn•spon- dence. I shall not sreak of the ecining S.iU uhtil we shall ce how it will end."</p>
        <p>Mr).;. Rocke approved this plan, and plaeed writing materials on the table. And while the in :troll employed lie.k.se!f in closing up the rnoms, pachiog rip what wa,s`needfut to take with them t) he do...:torS, and putt:ng o t the fire, Clara wrote end fwaled the letter. They then put on their bonnets, I,,eked up the ho so and set out. Th,..y /Jailed at the post-office. ;list in time to mail their loiter, and they retiched :he doctor's house j tas lie Ininsed walked up to the door, acconipained by the lawyk.f. The latter greeted the da gliter of his old client, and her friend, and they all went into the i mite together. In the doctor's study the whole subject of Clara's flight and its occasion was talked over, i_oni the lawyer agreed to commence proceedings imme- diately. ( To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
