        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUN.:3I I .</p>
        <p>A tneetl,r -F this Counctl W 39 hi'llt at tit. Town Hall on TUCrly, eVfMitla. the Mayor (Councillor James Fairciough) Lite,sidt-d, and there were also present : Aldermen Sutton, Greenall, Wright, Flancomb. Black- burst, Hutchinson, Bolton ; Councillors Roberts. Shaw Green, Bennett, Stnethurst, Ellison, SAlleock, Duncan, Lee,Furness, Baird, Heaton, Plumpton, Wilkinson,Hep- herd, Monks, Pierpoint, Tinnion, Longton, Skelton, Rogers, Cannel!, J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), J. Fair- hurst (borough treasurer), J. Deas (sanitary engineer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), and Haddock (gas en- gineer).</p>
        <p>THE LONGFORD DEPOT EXTENSION.-ADJOURNED DEBATE. _</p>
        <p>The debate upon the proposed extengions and im- provement in connection with Longford depot, which was adjourned from the previous Council meeting, was renewed.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR asked Councillor Monks if he had anything to say prior to the resumption of the discussion espe- cially with reference to the visit paid to the depot on Friday.</p>
        <p>Councillor MONKS replied that he would reserve what he had to say until the discussion had finished. It would be remembered that at the last meeting of the Council his resolution was that the proposed extension should be approved, and that the committee be authorised to pro- ceed with the vrorkoubject zo the periodical approval of the Council to the different sections of it.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON asked the Mayor if he had under- stood aright that each section of the work would be brought before the Council ? The MAYOR : My impression is that Councillor Monks wants the whole thing carried to-night, but he won't carry out the work without bringing each section before the Council for approval.</p>
        <p>Councillor SriEntuasT : When the sections come up, Mr. Mayor, I suppose—if we know of bater plans—we shall be entitled to alter them.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Oh ! certainly we can omit anything. Councillor SMETHURST Raid that in that case he would reserve his own plans till the sections were laid before the Council.</p>
        <p>Several members of the Council suggested. however, that they should have Mr. Smethurst's alternative plan placed before them that night, and the Mayor remarked that Councillor Monks wanted the whole thing thoroughly debated.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST therefore remarked that they were propcsing to build additional storerooms while they had au existing building which could be used for the purpose. This building was where the tips were placed for the old destructors. He also suggested that the dryers might be and ought to be kept nearer the furnaces,as this would effect a saving in steam. He was further of opinion that it was unnecessary to put down the proposed new chimney. The MAYOR : You are on the committee ; have you mentioned all this to them?</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST said he had mentioned a good deal to the cernmittee, but it seemed to be almost use- less. He was always mentioning something. (Laugh- ter.) The Chairman was very good ; he liked a discus- sion, but it all ended in their having to swallow what was brought before them. (Laughter.) Councillor HEATON said that after his inspection of Longford he was very pleased to agree with the Com- mittee. He believed they had got a very good deatruc- tor, but he belived also that as regards dealing with the excreta they were not one bit " forwarder " than they were 18 years ago. He must congratulate the Commit- tee on having the machinery in better condition, but still there was just as much stench as before. ' Councillor &amp;Frew-GREEN said he paid his first visit to Longford on the Friday previous. He bad heard a great deal about the smell, but it was not until he got into one particular building that he felt it to be anything like what had been described. He also referred to Countillor Smethurst's proposals and suggested that it should have the careful attention of the Committee. Councillor PIERPOINT differed from Councillor Heaton as to the differences in the condition of Longford depot to-day and eighteen years ago. He considered that the depot was now a credit to the town. From a compara- tive point of view it is now absolutely clean, and its machinery teems to be in the best order. Eight or nine years ago the machinery was in a dreadful cendition, and they had repeated rows in that Chamber in refer- ence to alterations and repairs. They did not then even use all their excreta, and he had beard it asseeed in that Chamber, that it was then the practice to run raw excreta down the sewers into the river. (Hear, hear.) Now he understood that all of it was taken to Long- ford and dealt with in a proper manner. (Hear, hear.) He had formed the opinion that the plans of the Committee were excel!ent and such as ought to be adopted in the interests of the town and the manufac- tory itself. But there was another point he should like t) mention. The men at Longford were engaged in one of the most repulsive occupations, yet he was afraid they were not paid the ordinary wages of tanyard labourers. He hoped the Committee would take into immediate consideration the desirability of mending the position of these men, either by giving them a lit tie higher wages or by adopting the eight hours' system by having three shifts instead of two. Councillor PLIMPTON said that as one of the deputa- tion from Longford he could bear out what had been said as to the improvement there upon the state of things that existed a few years ago, and especially with regard to the machinery. He was not going to oppose the plans of the Committee but he should like an ex- planation upon certain points. He understood that from fourteen tons of excreta there was only one ton of dry manure, and he should like to know where the thirteen tons of liquid went to. Was that liquid sent down San- key Brook? If that was so, he considered that it was a cause of part of the nuisance that the people living near Sankey Brook suffered from. Building was going on near the brook, and the complaints which had been made for years would have to be attended to. (Hear, hear.) If Longford depot produced part of the nuisance he should be one to apply for an injunction against the depot.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT said that whilst regarding the scheme as a whole with approval he should abstain from voting for it until be was satisfied that no nuisance would be caused in Mersey-st.</p>
        <p>Alderman GemotALL said,- in reply to Councillor Plumpton, that the pollution of Sankey Brook did not come from Longford but from districts higher up the stream. He hoped the Council would support the Sanitary Works Committee.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS pointed out that every section of the scheme would have to come before the Council for confirmation or otherwise and that it would therefore be competent for the members to oppose any tender. He pointed out that as the Sanitary Works Committee com- prised almost every member of the Council with mechan- ical knowledge its proposals should have considerable weight with the Council. There had been one or two mistakes made with regard to that part of the scheme dealing with Mersey-street. The destructor to be placed in Mersey-street would be simply for the purpose of dealing with refuse ashes, all the excreta would still be treated at Longford. Only the refuse necessary for the furnaces at Longford would be taken there, the rest being dealt with at Mersey-street. There would there- fore be a considerable saving in cartage. With regard to the pollution of Sankey Brook the imp ire water turned into it at Longford was infinitesimal in quantity, in comparison with adjacent authorities. Whatever power they possessed to prevent that pollution they should try to put into operation at the earliest possible moment.</p>
        <p>Councillor LONGTON thought that the proposed boun- dary wall at Longford was not necessary and as for the higher chimney one result would be the sending of the smell further away and perhaps bring the smell into conflict with the Winwick Asylum authorities. Alderman SUTTON thought there was no need for that discussion when they had been told that everything would be brought before them in detail. But perhaps one good purpose had been served as it had given their friend Councillor Pierpoint an opportunity for making an election speech. (Laughter.) He might say that if the workmen at Longford were deservine of more wages he was sure there was not one member- of the Council who would object to their having it. Councillor MONKS, in replying, expressed himself satis- fied with the discussion. With regard to the method of dealing with the excreta within the last month, a new proposal had been made to him to deal with that material on another system altogether. There was a chance of its being made to answer,and if they did they would perhaps be able to reduce the proposed expendi- ture by £2,000. As to the storeroom proposed by Coun- cillor Smethurst it would not be equal in capacity to the presont one which was already too small for the purposn. With reference to Sankey Brook, nothing they cou.la do would make it worse than it was when it came past Longford works. Besides, the amount of condensed water they put into the brook was only one gallon in 35 gallons of clear water, and they might put this into a nice trout stream without doing any damage. With re. gard to Mersey-st., the saving of cartage by placing a destructor there would not be less than £3OO or £4OO a year. The excreta yam would not be kept at Mersey- st.,but at the intercepting depots. Mr. Monks added in conclusion that the larger chimney was !needed at Long- ford because the area of the present chimney was too small to carry off all the gases. The Committee's report was then adopted by the Council without dissent.</p>
        <p>THE SPEED FOR CYCLISTS AND THE LIGHTING OF VEHICLES. The Watch Committee's minutes contained resolutions fixing the speed tor cyclists through the principal streets of the town at six miles an hour, and also that persons driving or having charge of any cart, carriage, or other vehicle shall carry a lighted lamp be- tween one hour after sunset and one hour before sun- rise. -% The MAYOR rnoved,and Alderman GREEIsTALL seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor PtEnporNT said he did not purpose making aR election speech. (Laughter.)elle was exceedingly</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA iS both a stimulating rand refreshin ng drink, and s nutritious food, probably more nourishing ihan any other heverage. It is absolutely pure, containing to foreign admixture.—Advt,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>sorry that Alderman Sutton had trt to make an election speech. (Laughter.) Alluding to the minute with regard to cyclists. he said cycling was becoming very popular, arid a goo.i many of our town councillors were careering a.o,,ut the streets on them, although he did not think they travelled above six miles an hour, as they were getting middle-aged. (Laughter.) But if it was the cor- rect thing that the speed should be limited to six miles an hour in the principal streets, he did not see why it should not apply to the whole of the streets in the town. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LONGTON referring to the proposed new by- law regulating the use of lamps on carriages and other vehicles, said it was a matter which would affect a large number of people, and he hoped it had received consideration, and was thought to be quite necessary. On some vehicles there was no provision made for the carrying of lamps..</p>
        <p>Tie MAYOR : The County Cc uncil is going to enforce the regulation, and I did not see why the Corporation should not. It is quite as necessary for carts to have a lamp as any other vehicle, especially in cases of men re- turning from _market half drunk.</p>
        <p>Councillor LONGTON pointed out that at Wolverhamp- ton they had a similar bylaw and 1,100 summonses were issued against persons for a breach of it. It struck him as being a bylaw that affected a good many people. The minutes were then passed. A DEPUTATION TO BRIGHTON.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW-GREEN in moving the confirmation of the Finance and Estate Committee, said it would be ob- served that a deputation had been appointed to attend the annual meeting of the Borough Treasurers' and Ac- countants' Institute which this year was held at Brigh- ton. Some very important matters would be discussed at the meeting, in which the Warrington Corporation were interested, and upon which they ought to have the best possible information. They would probably have an opportunity of discussing the County Council with those who had bad dealings with that body in other parts of the country. The subjects to be considered in- cluded the Trustees Act, Irredeemable Securities, and Short Term Loans, and all were of the deepest import- ance to them as a borough. The institution had a num- ber of honnrary members, the ex-Lord Mayor of Liver- pool being the first of fly The Corporation had been represented on two previous occasions at the annual gathering,and the institute had invited the Chairman to be an honorary member. (Hear, bear.) The MAYOR seconded,and the motion was agreed to. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of this Committee's minutes, Alderman Surrow said the Council would be pleased to notice that the list of plans for new buildinas included plans for the extension of two important works in the town, viz., Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., and the Hope Foundry Co., Ltd., Dallam-lane. Whilst they were continually passing plans for new houses in the borough, it wae of the highest importance that their works in the town chould be extended. Alderman BLACKHURST seconded the motion, and the minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE STATE OF WARRINGTON ROADS.-TER STRONG RE-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MARES BY THP: M&amp;YOR.</head>
        <p>MARES BY THP: M&amp;YOR.</p>
        <p>Alderman WRIGHT moved the adoption of the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee. He said the Committee had resolved to pave Sankey-st..from Castle- et. to Golborne-st. The Committee had lost an old and faithful servant by the death of Mr. William Wain- wright, and they had granted a small gratuity to his widow. He would like to have given more, but the Cor- poration had a number of old and faithful servants whom they had to consider. A letter had been re- ceived from Mrs. Wainwright thanking the Committee for their generous gift of £5. The Committee had a large amount of work on hand. The previous night they ac- cepted contracts for a considerable amount of work, and the next few days it would be put in hand. There bad been loud complaints about the condition of the macada- mised roads, but the weather had been against them. The MAYOR : How many years? Alderman WRIGHT: What do you mean? The MAYOR: That they have been a disgrace for the last ten years. Alderman WRIGHT I ittll sorry to hear you say that, but we shall nut them right before the next meeting. Councillor HEATON seconded the motion for the pass- ing of the minutes. After what had been said the roads would be improved. The MAYOR: I hope the committee will take the ques- tion of the roads into their serious consideration, as it has been said that the roads in the borough are the worst roads in the kingdom. Alderman WRIGHT : I don't believe that.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: lam determined to speaik. about it. I have spoken about the matter before, and you have not taken the slishteet notice.</p>
        <p>Alderman Rourox : They won't take any notice when you do speak about it. (Laughter.) The minutes were then agreed to. INCREASED SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>Cnuncillor Suew GREEN moved the confirmation of the Schnol Attendance Committee's minutes, and said that there were no fewer than 9,441 children in attend- ance at the differevt schools of the borough. The at- tendance was growing at an enormous rate.</p>
        <p>Councillor Smr.Lcocx seconded, and the motion was agreed to. ISITISETIM, LIBRARY, ARTS AND TECENICAL INSTRUCTION</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Art Gallery Sub-Committee reported that they had secured the hearty co-operation of Mr. Luke Fildes,R.A., and Mr. Henry Woods, R. A., in the promction of a loan exhibition of works of art, and recommended that such an exhibition be held in 1897.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS moved the adoption of the com- mittee's minutes. He referred to the co-operation of the two gentlemen named in the proposed art exhibition. The cnmmittee hoped it would be one of the features of the celebration of the jubilee of the Corporation. Councillor MONKS seconded, and the minutes were passed. THE PROPOSED ALTERATIONS AT THE MERSEY-STREET</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>OAS OFFICES.</head>
        <p>OAS OFFICES.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee recommended that the small room now used as a testing place be converted into an office for the engineer, and that a testing place be provided in A suitable part of the premises in Mersey-street. That the office now used by the as engineer he added to the general office by the removal of the wood partition now separating them, and that the wood partition be re- erected so as to enclose a portion of the committee-room as a private office. Councillor HRPHERD proposed the confirmation of the minutes, and said the structural alterations to be made involved an expenditure of only £lO or £l5. Alderman WRIGHT seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT thought the proposed alterations at the gas office were ill-advised. They had been aiming at centralisation but by the Council's decision with re- ference to the offices af the Corporation, they bad tied their hands behind their backs. Alderman WRIOUT : Are you aware that the alteration is only a_ matter of £7 or £8 ?</p>
        <p>i notinniilor BENNETT : But the cost sometimes gets to i £7O nr £BO.</p>
        <p>Aldprrnan WRIGHT: Not in this Case.</p>
        <p>Cnuncillnr flion.nrrT : These sma-if items have a w"- derful capacity for growing. (Hear. hear, and laugh- ter). The bill for the alterations at Bank House will be so considerable that we shall live to regret the day WA--</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : Bank House is not on the minutes. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: You are out of order. Councillor Ben- nett. The sum is so small that I don't think it is worth di.cussing.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then passed. THE PROPOSED NEW WHARF AGREED TO. Counnillor ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the Rivers' Committee, and the joint resolutions of the Street Improvement Committee and the Rivers' Com- mittee with reference to the construction of the new wharf for Messrs. Bishop and Co.. 10Ort. in length and to extend from the east end of the old Packet Stage. He stated that the terms which had boen arrived at with reference to the wharf were such as would commend themselves to the large majority if not to the whole of the members of the council. Objections were made to the previous proposals with reference to the wharf on what were deemed to be equit- able and reasonable grounds. These objections had now been fairly met,and the security which was then thought necessary had been obtained. The present proposals were entirely diffrent from the last, and were more conclusively to the advantage of the Corporation. He did not see any objection to them whatever. The other nortions of the minutes related to the inclusion in the Bill of the Ship Canal Company of certain clauses for the Dr,,teetion of Warrington. It was extremely important in the interests of the town, especially in the interests of those who had to residl near that portion of the old river which was not in that condition in which they would like to see it, that the proposed clauses should become Without alteration a Dart of the Ship Canal Company's Bill. Mr. R. T. Fairclough had submitted a report as their representative on the Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners, and the committee had also received papers or importance from him. Councillor HEATON seconded. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said the resolution with regard to the wharf absolved both the Street Improvement Com- mittee and the Rivers Committee from any charge of dealing unfairly with Mesers.Bishop and Company or of wishing to torass them in their business. (Hear, hear.) All they desired wal to see that the public roads were kept at the proper width. He believed the decision they had come to was not only for the benefit of Messrs. Bis- hop and Company but also for the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
