        <p>1 I Lark% $ • , _ _ •</p>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL TEETH for all who use daily on the tooth brush a few drops of SOZODONT, the pleasantest dentifrice in the</p>
        <p>world. Cleanses the teeth and spaces between them as nothing else will. Sound .and pearly white teeth, rosy lips, and fragrant breath ensured. +ft 6.sk forrSOZODONT: 2, 2.</p>
        <p>Councillor LEE said he could not agree with what Councillor Roberts and Alderman Sutton had said with regard to the wharf. Instead of the company roving a rent of five per cent, on the cost,and £5 per annum with respect to the foreshore, they should pay a wharfage rent of 31. per ton. There was nothing in the agreement to prevent Messrs. Bishop and Company from becoming common carriers.ln case they carted Mr him,they might charge him a shilling a ton, or whatever sum thy thought proper.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON: There will be a public wharf as well.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS replied that the term for agree- ment was so short that it was desirable the terms sub- mitted to the Council should be ratified. The experience of seven years would enable them to see whether it was desirable to make an alteration. (Hear, hear.) The com- mittee did not think they would be justified in spending Corporation money without security for interest on their capital. The agreement was the result of an interch6nge of opinion and more in the shape of a compromise. The minutes were da-ried. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee the TOWN CLERK reported that the differential rating clause submitted by the London and North-Western Railway Company as amended by him had been accepted by the Local Government Board and inserted in the Warrington Ex- tension Order, and that the London and North-Western Railway Company had withdrawn their petition against the sill for confirming the order. He also submitted the clauses inserted in the London and North-Western Bill for the protection of the Corporation, and reported that upon the instructions of the Parliamentary Com- mittee the petition of the Corporation against the Bill had been withdrawn. It was resolved that a memorial be presented to the Vice-President of the Committee of ,Council on Education. The committee have resolved,in the event of the memorial not being granted, that a re- presentation be made to the Local Government Board "That it is desirable to constitute the borough into a county borough." The Town Clerk reported the pro- ceedings between the Ship Canal Company and the War- rington Highway Board with respect to the repair and maintenance of the new road across Arpley Meadows, when it was resolved that the Corneara.tion take over the repair of: the road :on receiving £250, and relieve the Canal Company and the Highway Board of all future liability in respect to the same. The Parliamentary Committee has been revested to consider and report upon the arrangements to be made for celebrating the jubilee of the C Irporation. The minutes were passed without comment, on the motion of the MAYOR. seconded by Alderman GREENALL.</p>
        <p>VIE SHIP CANAL BILL.</p>
        <p>Councillor RO2ERT3 moved " That a petition be pre- sented to the House of Lords against alterations in the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, 1886, and that the common seal of the borough be affixed thereto." He said the Mersey Docks and Harbour Board had petitioned against the passing of the Bill which was now before the House of Lords, and the main object of their petition was to obtain joint power with them in regard to the flow of the water over the Weir at Howley, Butcher's Field and the control of the outfall of the water at the various sluices of the Manchester Ship Canal. In the Act, Warrington stood alone with regard to the right of con- trol, and it was of the utmost importance that Warring- ton should have that sole right of control. The Mersey Docks and Harbour Bo3rd up to now had had no joint authority with them, and it was a matter of the gravest and most urgent importance that they should not have the interference of any outside authority whatever with regard to powers which belonged absolutely to Warring- ton. He was sorry it was necessary to spend anothei penny in regard to the Bill. The House of Commons had seen the reasonableness of their proposals and had accepted them, and he had a strong belief that the House of Lords would not upset the decision of the com- mittee of the House of Commons. At the same time it was of the utmost importance that they should be repre- sented in the House of Lords as supporting the Bill as it now stood, intact. Councillor FIRATON seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>REPAIR OF MAIN ROADS.</head>
        <p>REPAIR OF MAIN ROADS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved "That the common seal of the borough be affixed to an agreement with the County Council for the repair of main roads for the year 1896-97."</p>
        <p>This was seconded by Alderman HUTCHINSON and agreed to. The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CHECKMATE TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>CHECKMATE TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>RHEUMATIC FEVER THREE TIMES.</p>
        <p>A local correspondent had an interview with Mrs. Owen Firmager, of" The Chart," Limpsfield common, Surrey, who told the story of her plucky stand against death.</p>
        <p>"You must understand," she said, "that for years I have not been strong. I have had rheumeic fever three times. The first attack occurred sixteen years ago,and until this last year, when I learned the cure, I did not know what it was to be really free from rheuma- tism. It is five years since the second attack occurred, and that left me much worse than the first. I lost the use of my arm—the left arm, same side as the heart— and was constantly subject to acute rheumatic pains. But the third and worst attack was two years ago. I was in bed for months and endured terrible agony." "Yes," added a neighbour, "she could not bear any- one oven to walk across the room, and doctors said that, even when the fever left her, she would be a life-leng Invalid."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Firmager, continuing said, "When I Was at last able to come downstairs, my joints were swollen and the pains were such that I could do nothing. I gave up medicine, ecause I could not see that it did me any good, and the doctor said there was nothing that would do me good."</p>
        <p>" What was it then that brought about your cure ? " The good woman rose from her seat, and stepping nimbly across the room, reached down a box. " Those," she said ; " Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. They cured me ! I read in a newspaper several cases similar to my own in which they had wrought wonder- ful cures, and I made up my mind to try them as a last resource. I found relief before the second box, and in three months I was able to walk to the adjoining vill- age of Westerham, to the astonishment of all my friends. I am quite well now and have not the slightest sign of relapse." Mrs. Firrnager's story was borne out by two neighbours, one of where gave information in regard to a second wonderful cure that had taken place in the same village. It was that of a young woman in her teens, living in the same village, whose friends believed she was in " a galloping consumption," but who, since taking the Pills ha r• become quite well and hearty. In place of their former bloodless appearance, her cheeks have the glow of health, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People have, indeed, a reputation amongst the highest and lowest in the land, owing to the wonder- ful cures they have effected in rheumatism, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, sciatica, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anemia, pale and sallow complexion, weakness, loss of a ppetite, palpita- tion, pains in the back, female weakness, aid hysteria. These Pills are rtenuine only with full name Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pale Pecple, and are sold by chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s. 91 a box, or six for 131. 93. They are a tonic—the only tonic pill—not a purgative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>– TERRIBLE CRIME IN LONDON.</head>
        <p>- TERRIBLE CRIME IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>A GIRL SHOT DEAD BY HER FATHER.</p>
        <p>A terrible murder occurred at 8, Colestone-street, Battersea Park-road, London, soon after nine o'clock on Monday evening', when William Sutton, a prison warder, murdered his daughter, and committed suicide. Sutton, who is separated from his wife, called at the house to see her on Monday. As she was not in he asked to see his daughter. The poor girl came down, and was greeting her father cheerfully %hen suddenly he raised a revolver, and placing it close to the girl's body fired. He then fired another bullet into his heart and fell dead. The girl, who was taken to St. Thomas's Hospital, d'ed in that institution on Thesday morning. Sutton was formerly a sergeant-major in the army. Ten years ago Sutton and his wife separated by mutual consent. For four years Sutton contriouted to her maintenance, but for the last six years he had done nothin4; for her. She had recently been to the War Office to attempt to get his pension attached.</p>
        <p>FITS OR EPILEPSY, GIDDINESS, SENSATIONS, FAINTS.</p>
        <p>I will demonstrate t) the whole world how that most dreadful of all complaints, " Epilepsy," which has been hitherto considered incurable, can be permanently cured (without the chance of failure). Write to the " SECRETARY," Burwooti House. Burwood Place, Hyde Park, London. He will send you, .• rßtis," full instructions for cure.</p>
        <p>RUPTURES.—REYNOLDS' Patent Imperceptible Indiarubber Stem Pad TRUSS. Suitable in all cises. perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— REYNOLDS, 10, Slate -treet (a f.w doors Bold-street), Liverpool. Open 0 Saturday until? adv.`,</p>
        <p>AN EXPERIENCE IN SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>George Reinhard is one of the msny young men who have gone out from England to South Africa to seek their fortune. These travellers have interesting expe- riences, not the least interesting of which are the hard- ships they have to undergo in order to trade with the natives. Mr. Reinhard has written a letter, dated at Cape Town, May 3, 1895, in which he says :</p>
        <p>"Three years ago I went into the interior of Africa. I was then in perfect health, weighing nearly thirteen stone. While there I caught a fever which hung about me for many months. After about four months of this fever I began to feel pains in my lungs. I did not pay very much attention to these pains at first, until they grew more troublesome and I began to cough. My coughing became intense ; it wrenched and strained my lungs, and soon I was raising quantities of blood. The pains in my lungs continued to grow worse, and I be- came so emaciated in weight that my friends thought I could not possibly live. "In February last, when I was in Smithfield, in the Orange Free State, a friend advised me to try Scott's Emulsion. I began to take the Emulsion as directed, and felt relief at once. My cough became easier from the outset, I did not wrench and strain as I had been doing, and it did not hurt my lungs so much, and I found also that the pain in my lungs was relieved. I could not believe that any remedy would have such quick and satisfactory results. I kept raising less and less quantities of blood, until I did not raise any at all, and at the same time I began to feel stronger and better in every way. I rested more quietly at night, and steadily gained in weight. It seems most extraordinary, but nevertheles it is a fact,that during February,March, and April, the time that I took Scott's Emulsion, I gained three stone in weight and completely recovered my health. lam now as well as I ever was, thanks to Scott's Emulsion, and I cannot say too nruch in its favour."</p>
        <p>Mr. Reinhard's experience with Scott's Emulsien is just as :important to sufferers in Great Britain as in South Africa. This climate encourages throat and lung affections, as is clearly proved by the large percentage of deaths cauved by these affections every year. You will profit by this South &amp;frican experience if it induces you to try this remedy. Scott's Emulsion is a palatable and eany form of Cod-liver Oil cosibinod with the hypo- phoophites of lime and soda. These important nutrients are just what the consumptive needs to relieve coughing allay the irritation of the lungs and give flesh, strength and vitality. On the wrapper of every genuine bottle of Scott's Emulsion is the trade-mark of a fisherman carrying a big fish on his back. (21)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the committee was held on Tuesday night at the Spread Eagle Fhtel,Manchester ; Mr. H. H. Waller (Brighouse) in the chair. Mr. Leonard (Hudders- field) and Mr. Buckley (Oldham) were elected auditors, representing Yorkshire and Lancashire respectively.— The Hull Club applied for permission to present testi- monials to certain officials and old players.—The Chair- man said that the committee had no jurisdiction in re- gard to the officials.—Mr. Warren (Warrington) thought it would be creating a dangerous precedent to allow grants either in money or kind to old players. A club might promise its players a handsome testimonial when they gave up playing.—Mr. Hutchinson (Wakefield) thought that players should be placed in the same posi- tion as officials.—The Chairman said the rule provided that no player should receive any monetary considera- tion.—Mr. Wright (Hunslet) proposed ,and Mr. Rothwell (Rochdale) seconded, that Hull should receive the per- mission asked for. Only four voted for this resolution, and it was therefore lost.—The following clubs were unanimously elected members of the Union :—Rad- cliffe, Waterhead Hornets, Warrington Saint Mary's, Morecambe, Dukinfield, Warrington Rangers, Warring- ton Locomotives, and from Yorkshire—East Moor, Hull and District Rugby Football Union (comprising 23 clubs), Castleford, Holbeck, Bramley, and Leeds Parish Church.—The Chairman remarked tbat every club in Hull except one was now in the Union.—The following dates were fixed for the county matches :—Lancashire v. Cheshire (in Lancashire), October 17 ; :Yorkshire v. Cheshire (in Yorkshire), November 7 ; Lancashire v. Yorkshire (in Lancashire), November 21.—The cup ties were fixed for March 20 and 27 and April 3, 10, 17, and 24.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LANCASIIIRE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LANCASIIIRE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire section of the Northern Rugby Football Union held on Tuesday night in the Spread Eagle Hotel, applications for membership of the Lancashire League were received from Swinton, Sal- ford, Crompton, Walden, Morecambe, Rochdale St. Clement's. and St. Helens Recreation. It was resolved that the Lancashire League should be composed of 14 clubs, and that the vacancies sh3uld be filled with Swin- ton, Salford, and Morecambe.—A resolution was passed that the clubs elected at the first League be asked to send in their registers of players at once, such registers to be made up of last year's regular players, and that players from other clubs would require to be transferred in the u way.—Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornets were re-elected members of the competition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>VISIT OF TOWN COUNCILLORS TO LONGFOR,D DEPOT.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF TOWN COUNCILLORS TO LONGFOR,D DEPOT.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon at the invitation of Mr.F.W. Monks,chairman of the Sanitary Works Committee, a number of town councillors and officials of the Corporation made an inspection of Longford Depot. The members of the Town Council assembled at the Town Hall at three o'cleck, and proceeded in one of Mr. Maginn's conveyances to Longford There were present :—Aldermen Oreenall and Bolton, Council- lors F. W. Monks, Furness, Bennett, Evans, Shaw, Shaw-Green, Plumnton, Duncan, Shillcock, Ellison, Rogers, Heaton, Smethurst, Longton, Wilkinson, Baird, Mr. J. L. Whittle (town clerk), Mr. James Deas (water and sanitary engineer), Mr.J.Fairhurst (borough treasurer). Councillor Roberts followed afterwards on his bicycle. On arrival, Councillor Monks who conducted the party through the works, explained the various processes for dealing with the material taken to Longford, and pointed out the necessity for the alterations and improvements which the Sanitary Works Committee have deeided to carry out at the depot, and which they asked the Council to con- firm. Proceeding to the two Low "Beaman and Deas " destructors which are used for the destruc- tion of ashes and the town's refuse only, these were shown in working order. The refuse just as it is taken from the tubs is here burnt to a clinker, the amount of clinker left being about thirteen per cent. The process of transformation is carried on with hardly any perceptible smell whatever and enough steam is generated to. drive the machinery at the works.</p>
        <p>On entering the evaporating and drying depart- ments where the excreta is dealt with as received from the intercepting depots, there is no denying that a smell—an awful smell—does exist. This was admitted by the Councillors,and some preferred the purer air outside to an inspection within. The smell is most pronounced in that portion where the raw material goes through what is known as the " dollying "process. Mr: Monks explained that the smell from this portion of the works escaped—not up the chimney as was generally supposed—but through the ventilators. The committee were seeking to minimise the smell by the introduction of exhaust fans, which would draw off the air to the furnaces where it would be consumed. The need for the proposed improvements in this depart- ment were admitted, as was also the necessity for increased storage accommodation. Mr. Monks also referred to the larger and higher chimney which it was proposed to erect, but several Councillors said they were not convinced of the necessity of it. Mr. Monks stated that the first require- ments were a boundary wall, new store and offices, then the laying down of additional plant, and the committee had placed the new chimney amongst their last requirements. In answer to questions, Mr. Monks said it took 19.4 tons of excreta to make one ton of their saleabl'e' monure. The water was turned into Sankey Brook. As to the workmen engaged, Mr. Monks said they had no trouble in getting men. They were paid from 225. to 245.. per week. Several mem- bers thought this was an insufficient wage consid- ering the conditions under which the men worked. The works, allowing for the material dealt with,are i neatly kept, and by those n a .position to know, are stated to be in a better condition than at any time previously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
