        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>A RECORD NIONTFI</p>
        <p>The trada of the p.irc of Manchester, as measured by the number and tonnage of the seagoing vessels using the docks, continues to increase rapidly. April was a recordsmonth in regard to the number and tonnage of the arrivals and departures. rnerchadise handled, and gross revenue,but although official information in regard to these two latter particulars has not yet been mach public, there is every probability that last month's trade and receipts will prove to have been appreciably larger. The tonnage using the port during May was the largest ever recorded, and exceeded the April returns by over 11,000 net registered tons. C)astwise traffic increased by nearly 22,000 tons register, whilst foreign traffic showed a tallins,r•off of Close upon 11,000 tons. The c.astwise tonnage was equal to 54 per cent, of the whole as compared with 46 per cent. in April, and the foreign tonnage to 46 per cent., again.t 54 percent., the propor- tions having been exactly reversed. Toe average net tonnage of vessels using the canal was 589 tons in the foreign trade, against 608 ton.; in April, and 184 tons in the coasting trade, agairst 172 tons. During the month new steamship services weno inaugurated between Man- chester and Cork, Waterford,Jersey, floulogne,Montreal, Melbourne, &amp;c., and theManehester Corporation lairags at Old Trafford received the first consignment of foreign cattle ever landed at this port. LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the week ended Friday, May 29, the number of seagoing vessels arriving at and sailing from the port of Manchester was 156, representing an aggregate net registered tonnage of 41,953, as compared with 132 vzssela of 36,250 tons in the preceding seven (lass. 126 vessels of 26 202 tons were engaged in the coastwise traffic, and 30 vesels of 15,751 tons in the foreign trade. The foreign imports mcml d logwood from Laguna. fruit from Burrian-i. Messina, anr4 Palermo; ice, wood pulp, timber, &amp;c., from Christiania and the Baltic, flints from St. Valery-en-Caux, cotton and onions from Alex- andria, and general cargoes from Antwerp, Boulogne, Calais, Copenhagen, Ghent, Hamburg,Paris, and Rotter- dam. Steamers cleared outwards with miscellanlous cargoes for the same ports, al,o for Barcelona and Valencia. Large consignments of piece goods, &amp;c., were shipped to China and Japan by the China Mutual liner Ningchow, and to Persia by the Zdlu. A very large passenger raffic was carried en every day during the week. This is not inehided in the appended approxi- mate statement of traffic for the period under notice :</p>
        <p>Inward:;.</p>
