        <p>THE CANAL AND THE RAILWAY COMPANIES. The Ship Canal Company en Tuesday brought a case before the Railway Commissioners to compel the Mid- land Railway Company to grant through rates from Manchester to Birmingham and the Midlands generally, and thus give Manchester, as far as those districts are concerned,the benefits of a seaport,which,it is contended have hitherto been withheld. A point was raised before the case came into court that the Canal Company are not a railway company within the meaning of the Acts, and consequently that they are not entitled to a through rate. This position was, however, abandoned in court, counsel for the Midland Railway Company stating that they bad no desire to rely upon a merely technical point. The questions remaining then to be decided were the amount of the rates and their apportionment. The Canal Company asked that Manchester should have the same advantages of sea competition as Liverpool, and a terminal allowance of is. 6d. per ton, instead of Is., was proposed, an intimation being given that further ser- vices would be rendered for the higher sum. The Rail- way Company,on tke other hand,contended that is. was quite suffieient. Mr. Turner,the general manager,stated that the Midland Company had always been anxious to facilitate the business of the Ship Canal, and he sub- mitted that the terms they offered were reasonable. The hearing of the case was not concluded. Judgment was given on We.lnesday in the cao brought by the Manchester Ship Canal Company before the Railway Commissioners to secure the benefit of thorough rates over the Midland Railway to Birming- ham. The chief questions at issue were whether the railway company should be allowed to - charge 7d. per ton more from the docks than from the Central Station, and whether the Canal Company should have Is. 61, in accordance with their claim, or only is. per ton for their terminal services. The railway company contended that they had already placed Manchester on the same footing as Liverpool, the rate from both places to Birmingham being 13s. id. Mr. Justice Collins delivered judgment, finding that 12s. 6d was the proper rate from Corn- brook to Birmingham, and that the Canal Company were entitled to 14 61. per ton out of the charges for their terminal services. The Canal Company also suc- ceeded in their application as regards Bristol rates.</p>
