      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>1 NOTES_ AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>1 NOTES_ AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A curinus instance of the red-tapeisin of the P t comes from Norfolk. q A letter-carrier, with a long coun- try round, recently purchased a tricycle, which enabled him to get over his district with greater ease to himself and a more convenient promptness to the public. In some way or other the department became aware of this fact. What does it do? Thank the man for going to pe sonal expense in order to serve the public with greater dispatch ? Not at all. It sends down an official censure to Lim for having dared to buy his machine without having received permission from head- quarters. It is this sort of conduct in the small details of its work that makes one almost hopeless of ever seeing common-sense displayed at St. Martin's-le- Grand.</p>
        <p>Marriages, if made in heaven, are sometimes cele- brated in singular places on earth. The Americans have racked their brains to provide singular circumstances for the tying of the nuptial knot, and weddings in bal- loons have become too commonplace to call for much remark. Our American cousins must, however, look to their laurels. By the last Cape mail conies news of a wedding in a lions' den, an event which a local paper unkindly called "A Chance for the Lions." The marriage was to take place in the "den" oflis' Circus,at Johan- nesburg. and as no cable has since been received as to any tragic results, it may be presumbd that the lion tamer Winschermen and Miss Malmaison were happily united to the accompaniement of roars and growls in- stead of the traditional chiming of sweet-voiced bells.</p>
        <p>A very interesting communication appears in the pages of this week's " Nature " from the pen of Mr. Alexander Graham Bell on the subject of Professor S. P. Langley's aerodrome, or flying machine. The machine is built chiefly of steel, and is not supported in the air by any gas, or by any means but by its own steam en- gine, which is betwixt one and two-horse power, and weighs, including fire-grate, boilers, and every moving part, less than 71b. This engine is employed in turning aerial propellers, which move the aerodrome forward. In the machine at present built, owing to its small size, the steam is not condensed, so that its flights are short. The total weight of the machine, including engines, is about 251 b., avd the distance, from tip to tip, of the supporting surface, some 12ft. to 14ft. In the flights witnessed by Professor Ball the machine rose with re- marl:able steadiness in the face of the wind, afterwards swinging round in large curves of about 100 yards in diameter,until it reached a height of some 80ft. in about a minute and a half, when the steam being exhausted the machine settled quietly down. The velocity of flight was about twenty-five miles an hour, the machine tra- versing during its flight some 3,000 ft., or over half a mile.</p>
        <p>At Blackburn on Tuesday Tom Branden, the Black- burn Rovers' full back, who in April olayed fn the Scot- tish international team, was charged by his wife with persistent cruelty. The complainant, a good-looking young woman, who appeared in the box with a black iye,stated that her husband had oommitted half-a-dozen assaults upon her in one week. Flis wages as a pro- fessional footballer were £4 per week. The complainant further stated that Brandon once drove her into the street in her nightdress. Another time he chased her with a carving knife, and on yet another occasion he produced a revolver and threatened to shoot her. He also kicked and struck her. The defence was that Mrs. Brandon was addicted to drink, and that the injuries complained of had been caused by falls while in that condition. The Bench made a separation order, the de- fendant to pay his wife 12s. 6d. per week.</p>
        <p>The Greater New York Bill, which Governor Morton has now signed, will make the New York municipality enormous. The population will be just three millions, and the area will be 300 square miles,or about twice the sunerficial area of London. This territory will contain 130,000 dwellings, 37,000 business houses, 6,000 acres of parks, 1,100 miles of surface and elevated railways, 900 miles of paved streets, 1,100 hotels, ninety post-offices, and 350 schools. The consolidated debt of the city will be 170.000,000 dols., and the taxable property will be over 2,b00,000,000 dols. The consolidation comes into effect on January lst,lB9B,and the first mayor of Greater New York will be elected in November 1897. A State Commission has been appointed to draw up a charter for the immense city thus constituted, though some doubts are expressed as to whether this Commission will be able to agree on 1, report.</p>
        <p>Many English writers take a delight in drawing gloomy pictures of the decline of English manufactures, which, they say, are being replaced in the markets of the world by goods "made in Germany." But some in- telligent Germans, at any rate, take a very different view of the situation. According to a writer in the " Allgemeine Zeitung," this country leads the way in the production of furniture, glass, carpets, wall-papers, and all the other articles which go to make up the artis- tic and comfortable home, and nowhere are these goods more popular than in Germany. "From month to month we hear and see more frequently that English and American furniture, carpets, and stuffs are imported wholesale and sold,that rich people fit up their houses in the English style—in short, that something like an Ang- lo-American fashion threatens us, in the same way as a French fashion long ruled us in the past." There are "English shops" in all the great towns, and our best and most costly manufactures for household use find a ready sale, because they are well made, because they are useful and convenient, and, finally, because they are artistic in design. The "Arts and Crofts" movement has created an extraordinary impression in Germany, and to the labours of Mr. William Morris, Mr. Walter Crane, and other able designer the present popularity of English furniture is in no small measure due.</p>
        <p>Friends may fall off, and Theosophy may not interest the multitute, as in other days but that ardent theoso- phist, Countess Constance Wachtmeister, the pupil and friend of Mme. Blavatsky, keeps as zealously as ever on her lecturing tours in the Old World and in the New. She has " done " Australia, Tasmania, New Zealand,and Honolulu, and still seeks fresh lecturing-worlds to con- quer. She is fifty-eight. She was born in Florence, but her family, that of De Bourbel, was one of the most ancient in France. She lived in Linden Hall, Berkshire, with her aunt for several years, and was married there- from in 1863 to her cousin, Count Wachtmeister, the Swedish and Norwegian Minister in London. Her husband died in 1871; and a decade later she joined the Theosophical Society. Mme. Blavatsky gave her the post of corraspsnding secretary,and for many years she conducted the work of the Theosophical Publication Society in London. As a worker for theosophy she has no equal apart from Mrs. Besant.</p>
        <p>One result of the attempt to capture the Board schools has probably not been foreseen by the new propaganda. It is statcd that the letter which we eive below is only a specimen of those which the head master of a school in East London is receiving. The letter is instructive : To the Head Master.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! am desirous of withurawing my three boys from religious instruction in your school. This unjust and dishonest Tory Education Bill, which is intended to cripple the Board schools, has settled my convictions. Sectarian teaching has always been obnoxious to me. more especially when taught at the rate or taxpayers' expense; and I am convinced that no permanent settle- ment of the question can be arrived at until the Bible is abolished from the Board schools altogether. Therefore you will understand my objeeon not to be a personal one ; ,indeed,l have much to th-llik you for, I am sure at all times von have given the greatest attention to all the lads under your charge. A-- B--, Fatber.</p>
        <p>Last year our guest the Shabzada attenqed the Derby and was rather bored by it. The unexpected victory of Lord Rosebery's horse was not in the least unexpected to him, 'for," his Flighness is reported to have saicl,"what is the geod of being Primp Minis'er if you cannot ensure your horse's coming in firit ?" If the result of Wednes- day's Derby should penetrate to Cabul it is to he feared that the Shahnda's ideas a. to the purity of English horse-racing will become more fixed than ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"OLD CROW" PUBLIC-HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE "OLD CROW" PUBLIC-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>INTENDED ALTERATIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday morn- ing before Mr. F. Monks (chairman),Alderman Harrison, Alderman Frannomh, Mr. T. Birchall,and Mr. S. Wright. Mr. W. Owen, on behalf of Messrs. Greenell, Whitley. and Co. submitted elan% of al'orations it was proposed to make to the "Old Crow Vennell-street. Mr. Owen said the plans hld been submitted to and approved by the Watch Committee and also by tbe Street Improvement Committee and the Chief Constable, but Messrs. Greenall. Whitley. and Co., desired out of courtesy to show to the Bench what was proposed to be done. The chiof object of the alteration of the house was that supervision by the tenant and the police should be better in every way. It would cover less area than it had before.</p>
        <p>Mr. Monks It : It is really a conversion of a public- house into a vaults P Mr. Owen : You may call a railway refreshment room a vaults then. Only in tha'. sense can the alteration be called a vaults. The Chief Constable said he had examined the plans himself. It appeared that there were a number of rooms which could not be properly 9upervised by the person in charge. The proposed altereions would bring nearly all the company into one room and so supervision by the tenant and the pollee was made much easier. Formerly they would have to make four visits. Mr. Owen : There were five rooms before and now there will be three. The Chief Constable said there were rooms that women were in the habit of occupying with the knowledge and sometimes without the knowledrie of the tenant but now they could all be seen, if the plans were passed. The plane wet, passed.</p>
      </div>
