      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SATURDAY, JUNE 6th, 1896.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY, JUNE 6th, 1896.</p>
        <p>Pr is not not often that, in con- ,o Tv, Liberal ictories nection with Parliamentary bye- elections, two victories can be Net.rded on one day. Yet that was the case on 4ay. On that day the Liberal Party suc- Zed in wresting two seats from the Govern- In both Les the contests were watched kik exceptional interest, and in neither case id'aci it be said that the Liberals fought with any • tLltautages in their favour apart from the cause Of represented. Probably the most important h- the two elections was that in the Frome divi- ai"lll of Somerset, which last year returned a sup- ter of the Government in the person of Lord , tPxourir. The Tories entered upon this elec- „-"I With a full assurance of success, and even the close of the poll on Tuesday the sporting eleh, t 'gent in the constituency was offering odds of to one on the success of their candidate. No 'utibt from their own point of view there was enable ground for this confidence. The con- is largely agricultural, and is not the yerument pushing through the House of Corn- %llls by means of the closure: a Rating Bill which ,tit;dY saY is designed to " relieve" agriculture? "tt,then,again, there are many voluntary schools at constituency, and in not the Education 14,a,ocially intended to " muzzle " the School are-icis and to further endow the schools which of tniller the control of the clergy of the Church t -gtarld P These are arguments which they f ar-Orally expected would tell with irressistible tiso_ee J is reports Promo division. It is apparent from .7e re- its :hese argil of the progress of the contest that ments were used for all they were lorth. In the more rural parts of the division Ige farmers were told the exact amount of the kites that would be taken off the land, and the 'l!lhourers were told that this reduction of rates ItoUld be followed by an increase of wages. In the tic rban districts the electors were diligently in- 'cltrflecl of the exact additions that would be made thrants of the various voluntary schools, and be --;:eY Were all asked to admire and support a suint—eLeat Government. The disappointing re- of „ Ir°l3l the Tory point of view—is the return vo:ue Liberal candidate by the largest number of ...an:trees:ler:corded in the Promo division. The as that of the Somersetshire con- atituency Was returned by the Wick Burghs away in the extreme north of Scotland. The vacancy at Wick was caused by the death Of Si,' JOHN th PEN/I)ER a Unionist supporter of e (iovernment, and it has been filled by the :turn of a Liberal who also received the itirgest number of votes ever recorded in 01‘.1e constituency. These two defeats, one Which at all events was entirely un- ;lPected, has naturally caused a good deal of con- _ternation amongst the supporters of the Govern- meat and various reasons are assigned for the re- But as as a matter of fact the explanation simple. The policy of the present ZWernment is not what the country wants and i s loct what was expected. Both in home and teign affairs the Government has entered upon ,eobtful and dangerous courses. The opposition `° the Education Bill increases almost hourly, 4 (1111 this opposition is by no means confined to ham2,llarY political opponents. The more the pro- effects of the Bill are considered and 4ralised the more pronounced is the antagonism it .:laos amongst men who are interested ie the 41-"eelleational welfare of the country. It is plainly tetioll that this is not the time to cripple the urees of the most advanced at.d elfi,ient idit taa'.eational authorities merely for the sake of taining clercial predominance. Some other tvo'intries are already spending a great deal more ksli3 the education of their children than we in itltiaaud, and the results are beginning to he seen that Ci_race for commercial supremacy. It is said .tne Education Bill has had more to do with it 0..7' of the Frome election than any other cause. suitl'tglat.therefore,to be a warning to the Govern- tsp, a it will certainly be a timely encourage- et;poiltsto these who are determined to strenuously fise,„ e tike Bill. The Rating Bill has also had in- great in these elections, and probably a very tarp, 'nfluence. The Tories have been telling the ttpect ers of /creme the exact amounts they should t.he f t° benefit by the passing of the Bill, but in ace_l of audiences who are not directly con- teOsaill' With the land they have been practically tiks,e to defend the Bill. There are many indica- • a art from these elections, of the intense in- -4413 I°n Which has been aroused by this scanda- eks Atete... ure. We have before referred to the a , kaitlat Inacie by the Town Council of Crewe 4(1444 the tax of a shilling a head for the benefit koth °4l'ners, and this week the Town Council of . er 4111 a JaCent toVr,t—Stockport—has made a ePru -vst. As both Crewe and Stockport are 24ted • te Parliament by Tory members these •••1 t . w very clearly the effect that the Rat- -41(1 t having upon borough constituencies, op, here oi"erttip;, eau be little doubt that, given the MI6 '4') the results of the two bye-elections e(1411t- Wtek q. Could be repeated in every part of the</p>
        <p>SPEAKING at Leeds on Wednesday, The Soudan Expedition. Mr. JOHN MORLEY in a powerful speech dealt with the very serious questions that have been raised by the Soudan expedition. When, a few months ago, the expe- dition was first announced the country was startled and there was a general desire to know the cause of this unexpected departure. The Government were promptly questioned in Parlia- ment but even yet there has been no plain and authoritative reason given for the expedition The Government, relying no doubt on their large majority, have refused information in a manner which is quite new to the English Parliament. Papers and documents which have been pubtished in Italy have been refused to the House of Com- mons, members of which have actually had to re- sort to the Green-books of a foreign government for the information they wanted. As Mr. MORLEY says the "youthful Parliament in Rome is to have Information but the mother of Parliaments at Westminster is to have none." So far as can now be gathered the movement up the Nile was originated for the purpose of relieving the [ Italians from the difficulties they had got into in their -war against Abyssinia. But now when the Italians have practically been re- lieved what is to be done,? The expedition is still pushing forward and troops have actually been brought from India to Suakin. This seems to show a fixed though.unavowed intention to re- conquer the Soudan. Mr. MORLEY warns the country against any such enttrprise. It is one which may before it is completed involve the whole of the military resources of the Empire and lead to some awkward and certainly undesirable complications with France and Russia. Mr. MORLEY is particularly opposed to the use, with- out previous Parliamentary sanction, of Indian troops in these foreign expeditions,and he quotes in support of his views no less a person than Lord SALISBURY himself. At the time of the Abyssin- ian expedition in 1869 Lord SALISBURY—then Lord CRANBORNE—declared that he did nct like India to be looked upon as "an English barrack in the Oriential Seas "from which any number of troops might be drawn without paying for them. Lord SALISBURY then considered that this was a temptation to engage in small wars, and it is a curious coincidence that a Government of which he is himself the head should do the very thing against which he warned the country.</p>
