      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>EX A MINER SAT UR DA Y, JUNE 6. 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER SAT UR DA Y, JUNE 6. 1896.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>AN OLD OFFENDER AT RUNCORN. On Tuesday morning, before Messrs. Speakman and Taylor, at the Runcorn Police Court, Ellen Moran, of Mersey-street, was charged under a warrant with failing to answer to a summons on the previous day for using obscene language. As there were a number of previous convictions recorded against the prisoner she was sent to gaol for 14 days. P.C. Butler proved the case. RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The Saturday section of this club travelled to Beeston Castle. There was a fair muster and the weather tended to make the outing enjoyable. On Wednesday the run was fixed for Mold, but the thunderstorm prevented the completion of the journey. A stop was made at Chester, where the afternoon was spent on the river and in the park. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN SCHOOL, RUNCORN. • On Sunday, the anniversary services in connection with the above school were preached in St. John's Church, Victoria-road, by the Rev. R. Williams (Ethos.) In the afternoon the choir and scho,ars gave a service of song entitled "The footprints of the Bing." There was a good attendance at each service. THUNDERSTORMS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wedaesday a long spell of good weather termin- ated by a thunderstorm and a short but heavy fall of rain. Thursday again commenced hot and oppressive, and after mid-day ominous clouds gathered over the town breaking about half-past two. Rain fell heavily during tke afternoon while there was several vivid flashes of lightning and heavy thunder claps. No dam- age has been done, and the rain should prove an un- mixedlblessing. RUNCORN TRADES COUNCIL, RUNCORN. . .</p>
        <p>On Monday the sub-committee appointed at the meet- ing held to discuss the question of the bad condition of trade in the town, met at the Runcorn TOWR Hall. A general conversaticn took place about the introduction of new industries, and it was suggested that the Run- corn people would assist the town by purchasing as much as possible in the town by giving a preference to commodities made in the neighbourhood. It was also decided to act upon the suggestion of Sit. J. T. Brunner, and ascertain the area and sites ot vacant land in the district and what buildings were unoccupied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>This organisation held its usual meeting on Saturday in the Greenway-road School under the chairmanship of Mr.T.Heesom. After the routine business reports were read from the delegates at the National Division meet- ing held at Darlington on Whit Monday. From the re- ports it was gathered that the number of adult mem- bers was 35,000 or an increase of 2,043 on the year, of which 41 per cent., were from cadet or juvenile orders. There were 15,000 cadets, and during the year 16 new branches had been opened. After the reports had been read, a discussion took place in which Messrs. T. Cooke, James Johnson, Thomas Clarke, Thomas J. Holt, T. Whyte, and D. Bisbrown took part. SERMONS AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary sermon-Fs took place in the Wesleyan Chapel, Moore, on Sunday, when at both the afternoon and evening services the chapel was filled. The Rev. E. H. Jackson, a former minister of the circuit, had been selected for the preacher. and excellent discourses were given by him. Special hymns and anthems were very well rendered by the choir ; indeed, in every respect the services were a great sticcess. At the close of each service a 'collection was made in aid of the Sunday school, which amounted to the creditable sum of £lB fis. 81., an increase on the previous year. PUNERAL OF THE LATE MRS. HANDLEY, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon the remains of Mrs. _Handley,</p>
        <p>of 32, Greenway-road, were placed:to rest in the family grave in Runcorn. She was the widow of Mr. Thomas Handley, son., who died nearly 25 years ago, but to whose place and influence in the religious and public life of Runcorn we made reference to on the death of his:youngest son ,Mr. Thomas Haadley,J.P.,nine:months since. The lady under notice was of exceptional force of character in her day, but since the death of her hus- band she has lived in comparative retirement, and for the past two years has been an invalid. She was taken suddenly worse last Saturday evening and passed pain- lessly away at the advanced age of 83 years. The funeral procession started from her late re: idence shortly after two o'clock,and in advance walked Dr.Robinson,Messrs. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C.. W. Stainway. M. Dobson, S. Watson, J. J.Speakmaa, Joseph Slack, J. Corry, William Ackerley, W. Lacey, John Bate, H. W. Jinks,R. Rawlin- son,J. R. Frith, William Bate, and W.Sutton. After the hearse came two coaches, in which rode the mourners, namely :—The Rev. S. B. Handley (Hertford), Messrs. W. Handley,Joßeph Handley (Garston),sons ; Horace W. Handley, Clement Handley, T. Brookes Handley, and Arthur Handley, grandsons. These were followed by the Rev. Thomas Cole (Garston), Messrs. S. B. Stringer (Warrington),Joshua Midwood (Widnes). F. Beck, J. Bennett (Bootle),W. Rushton, R.ltushton,John Rushton, Joseph Litler, Joseph Kirkham (Manchester), R. H. Bell (Cressington), William Richardson, F. L. Scott, A.Batli- gate (Liverpool). and C. Hodgkinson (Garston). At the Cemetery the party were met by Mr. J. Simpson and Mr. E. Imison. The funeral service was conducted by the Rev. J. S. Simpson. On the coffin were wreaths from each family of grand-children, Mrs. Cameron's family, Mr. W.Handley's familT,Mrs.Thomas Handley's family, Mrs. Raw, and Mr. J. J. Speakman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>SAD FATALITY- AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SAD FATALITY- AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>INQUEST AND VERDICT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, at the Runcorn Police Court, Mr. J. E. Worelev investif,ated the circumstances con- nected with the death of Robert Hendry Farrell,aged ten years, son of Mr. William Farrell, of 12, Wat'rloo-road, who died on Thursday night from the result of injuries sustained on the previous Sunday evening. Mr. A. L,nalzer represented the parents of the deceased,and Mr. Burton appeared for the owner of the property, Mr. Thomas Andrews, of Peterborou4h.</p>
        <p>_ William Farrell, jun.,builder,s-aid he was father of the deceased. He saw his son half a minute after the acci- dent being carried by Thomas Ashcroft. He heard from him that he had been injured by a stone falling from the wall surrounding his yard. Afterwards he went to te,., wall, which was six feet high, built of brick, with a store coping measuring one foot 14, inches by 12 inches, fix inches deep. The stone which fell,according to a measurement was 56J)a. weight. He had noticed the dangerous condition of the wall, the stonework being hr night out so that the least it of touch with the hand lifted it. He wrote to the owner four months ago, drawing his attention to its being unsafe. Be received a reply asking what would be the cost of the repair, and witness answered that it would be 355., including other matters. Witness had previously vet itten to his landlord saying if anything happened to his children he would hold him responsible. He expected the accident to hap- pen at the particular spot where the stone fell. Another pneion of the wall was loose. It was 30 years old at the least. As soon as he heard of toe accident he sent for Drs. Robinson and McL,moan, and the o latte,trl at- tended him ure his death, on Toursday night. at ten o'clock.—By Mr. Burton : The boy had been to church, farnott be we insideey )t t ( o r:ilittencbleodseto, vaerrn.l aanfdt e r mustfaste haveng thep door his hand on the stone, which caused the accident. It was a fact he had been on the wall. William Lea, brieksetter's apprentice, residing with his uncle, Mr. Farrell, said he had come from church on Sunday night about 8.20. He and deceased wanted to go to the closet. The Yard door was fastened and wit- ness climbed over the door into the vard. He unfastened the door and admitted deceased. Afterwards witness went back through the doorway, leaving deceased to climb over after fastening the door. He did this, and immediately afterwards witness heard something drop. Deceased touched the wall with one hand in getting over, and then fell. Witness went to him and found him lying in the entry with the stone on his head. He lifted it off him and then called to Ashcroft, who carried him home.—The Coroner : Have you made a aractine of going over the wall in that way ?—Witness : No— The Coroner : How often have you climbed the wall ? —Witness : Twice. Continuing, he said deceased had not to his knowledge climbed over the wall except on the two occasions in question. He had not seen anyone el,e, go over the wall. Witness in climbing the door did not touch the stone. Hie uncle regularly used the doorway, and would pass throuelh six times daily. The other members of the family also used the doorway.—By Mr. Linaker : It was the custom, if the house was locked up to climb over the door. This was the only way they could get in.—By Mr. Burton : Witness repeated the same statement. He added, in answer to the Coroner, that altogether they had only been over the door twice. He could not get over the deor without assist- ance. Dr. McLennan deposed to being called in to see the deceased at 8.30 p.m. on Sunday. He found him bleed- ing copiously from the ears and nostrils, and from a lacerated wound on the rieht side of the top of the head. He was told the boy had been struck with a stone, and the injuries were consistent with the account given. Ele the skull. him injury t ot o tthe hbt irma ien , obfyhfi rs a cdteuarteh fwt hhei cbha swe ao sf ca at tuesnedde bd The jury, after a few minutes' deliberation in private, brought in a verdict of" accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LYII3I.</head>
        <p>LYII3I.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED. SUICIDE OF A WOMAN. —Late On Mnruis y slight an oh i woman named Percival, who resides on Eagle-brow,attempted t 3 commit suicide by cutting her throat. Luckily, however, she was observed by some neighbours,who took her immediately to Dr. Williamson. On Tuesday morning Mrs. Pervival was charged before Dr. Brabszon with having attempted to commit suicide by cutting her throat.. Prisoner, who pleaded guilty, was conveyed to the Knutsford workhouse.</p>
        <p>TIIE Pusuo ARE WARNED against the use of Foreign cocoas conta ning alkali, producing highly coloured prepara- tions offered as " pure cocoa." Cadbury's Cocoa is absolutely pure and unadulterated, and is therefore the best, The " Lancet" describes it as " the standard of highest purity at present attainable,"—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTION AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Friday afteraoon last was the day appointed for re- suming nominations for the vacant seat on the Cheshire County Council vacated by Mr. P. Speakman, J.P., who represented the South electoral division of Runcorn. The papers handed in to Mr. Ashton were on behalf of Mr. Edwin Imison, Heatherlea, Weston-road, Runcorn, jaweller ; and Mr. Alfred Robert Norman,Linden Lodge, Runcorn, potter. The former gentleman was proposed by John Brundrit, J.P., seconded by Mr. John Jones, and assented to by Messrs. James Littler, Henry Branch, J. W. Lightburn, Alexander Kennon ,John Littler, George Henry Grounds,Henry Bill ,and Thomas Clarke ; also by Mr. Samuel Pope, seconded by Mr. William Whittaker, with assentors Messrs. Richard Abel, Samuel Yarwood, Thomas C. Price, Charles Leach, William Mellor, John Coxhill, William E. Dudley, and George Goldsmith. Mr. Norman was proposed by Mr. Samuel Walton, seconded by Mr. Samuel Chadwick Beckett, with assen- tors Messrs. Thomas Bramall.William Handley, Charles Littler,S. D. Matthews,Alfred Ashbrooke,Edward Aston, John Stafford, James Bate; also proposed by Mr. R. H. Posnett, seconded by Mr. M. L. Davies, and assentel to by Messrs.Thomasßiley,James Knowles,Elward Taylo r J. W. Temperley, R. Norman,D. Bisbrown,B. Kniveton, and William Rusnton ; also proposed by Mr. P. Speak- man,seconded by Mr. Richard Morris,and assented to by Messrs. John Andrews,Thomas Moores, William C Dyer, William Carroll,William Massey, Wiliam Upton, George 1 E. Woodland, and William Stanway. MR. NORMAN'S CANDIDAIURE. ADDRESS BY SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a well attended meeting was held in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Green way-road, in support of the candidature of Mr. Alfred R. Norman, Aid. P. Speakman, IP., occupied the chair, and there were also on the platform. Sir J. T. Brunner M.P., the Revs. J. Stafford, and T. Bramall, Messrs. A. R. Nor- man, R. H. Posnett. and W. Ockleston.</p>
        <p>The Chairman explained that they were called together to hear a candidate who had been chosen by the Liberal Council for the South Electoral Division of Runcorn, in the vacancy which had been occasioned by his own elec- tion to a seat as county alderman. He trusted that the meeting would approve of the candidate, in the person of Mr. A. R. Norman, who was well known to them in Runcorn. He had a good pedigree, nad done goad work in the past and he (the speaker) believed that Mr. Nor- man would be able to render a good account of himself in the future if he was elected by them. (Hear, hear.) The position which he (Mr. Speakman) had held on the County Council for over 74 years, had come to an end, and he took that opportunity of thanking them for the kindness he had received at their bands;dering that time. It was a pleasure to him to do everything he could for Runcorn' and it would continue to be so. He had simply changed his position and he would be pleased to see Mr. Norman put in his old place on the County Council for that division. As they were aware burdens were being put upon that Council which made it more important every year. They had heard rumours about no politics en the c nrncil; as far as the actual working of the Coun- cil, there were no politics at all, but he felt bound to point out to them that his appointment as alderman, was because he was a Liberal. They had an honourable understanding between Liberals and Conservatives on that question, and when the vacancy arose his name was selected because of his connection with the Liberal party. The committees ,were elected on the same lines, and after that the work of the Council was before them. and there was no Council that possessed a better record than the County Council of Cheshire. (Applause.) Mr. A. R. Norman, who was well received, said that it bad been stated that he was a nominee of Mr. Speak- man, but at the outset he wished to deny that state- ment. He was where he was four years ago—on the Liberal platform, and the chosen candidate of the Liberal Council in Runcorn. Four years ago he took the view of the County Council which he took to-lay, and his view was that the County Council had been in- augurated to relieve the House of Commons of a deal of County work. That being the case it would be im- possible to argue that politics did not take a place in the working of that body. He supported the Liberal candidate on the last occasion because though he held Mr. Heztehurst in the highest esteem, he felt it was his duty to support the Liberal candidate on that occasion. With refeience to the present contest, he had the seine feeling as to personalities. Mr. Imison was well res- pected and deservedly so. He was a gentleman for whom he had the highest esteem, but there were times when they had to drop their personal and private affec- tions and stand by their true principles. It had been said that there were no politics in the County. Council, but what had caused the vacancy ? Had not the chair- man been appointed an aldermai because he was a Liberal, and what further proof could they want than that? In the choice of aldermen and committee,politics were considered, and therefore it would be ridiculous to speak of no politics on the Council. He hoped that fact would tell on the Liberals of the South Division of Run- corn,and that they would not be misled by the red her- rings drawn across the path. One of the things which attracted him to take up the invitation of the associa- tion was the Education Bill. Education was one of the principal bones of contention in the political arena, and it had to be handed almost entirely to the County Coun- cil. In his opinion there were politics in that. The speaker then went on to criticise the new Bill dealing with the authority as it would likely be composed, the question of grants and the religious phases of the question. It accrued to him that when elections were brought round, the education question would be forced to the front, and there would be a great fight over it, while matters of sanitation and other !work which was the work of the County Council would be left in the background. Elucation—he considered, was a matter important enough to demand a specific beard.and ought not to be Oven over to a body which had other things to do. He was very aexieue that the Liberals of the outh Division would not be blinded. lie thought there was an endeavour to tamper with their sight and he hoped that it would not be successful but that they would meet the occasion and do their duty manfully. (epplause.) He contended that independence meant practically nothing. He came before them that evening in his true colours. They knew well that he was a Liberal, and if they returned hiin they would be able to rest assured that ne was not on the -County Council to talk politics. He came before them as a Liberal,and in matters of politics they would know what he was. He had the greatest respect for Mr. Imison who held several important offices in the town. Without going into personalties he did net believe in the plurality of nffiCi4i as unless a man holding so many offices had his full time at his disposal one or other of the offices would suffer. If he (Mr. Norman) was elected by them he would go as a Liberal and take liberal views of the matters which came before him. Me felt that the Liberals and ratepayers had an opportunity of malting themselves heard. He should be glad of their support and he felt that they would be willing to give it, but if he was not returned lie would have the satisfaction of knowing that he had acted straightforwardly. (ep- plauSP. The Rev.J.Stafford proposed a resolution as follows : " That this meeting of electors having heard Mr. Alfred R. Norman's expression of his views, heartily enoorses his candidature for the vacancy on the County Council, and hereby pledges itself to secure his election for Ron- corn South Division by personal influence and by record- ing their votes in his favour on Saturday next," and those present to give their support to Mr. Norman urged who would serve them well. Mr. Posnett seconded the resolution and briefly referred to the education question which, he pointed out would make the Church and the Nonconformists antag- onistic. Mr. Imison was one of the hottest Conserva- tives in the toe n and as a churchman would be a faithful son of the church and opposed to the liberty of the children. On these grounds he seconded the resolution. Sir John Brunner supported the resolution. In open- ing, he congratulated the Chairmae on the dignity bestowed upon him. They all knew he (the speaker) was a politician. He was a business man as long as it was necessary and after that he was a politician because pelitice meant one's duty to one's neighbour. He was there because he desired them to work and to persuade their neighbours to look upon the contest as a political one. Over and over again there came up questions on the County Council whenimembers had:toldecide whether to vote with the interests of the few or the many, and they would always find the Liberal on any question tak- ing the side of the many, while the Conservative was tempted by his associations to place the interests of the rich at the top and the great masses of people below. He counselled the meeting to vote Liberal on Saturday. They were told that politics did not touch the work of the •Conneil.but why was that so ? When it was formed the people sent in an equal number of Liberals and Con- servatives and the members decided to avoid the bitter- ness of contests and entered an arrangement by which the honours and half the work was allotted to each party equally. On this ground he asked them to let Liberal succeed a Liberal on Saturday to keep up that arrange- ment. If Runcorn South sent Mr. Norman they would avoid a political struggle,and show their approval of the friendly arranzements which had existed since the for- mation of the Council. In sending a Liberal they would be on the side of leace, friendly feeling and fair play. The result of the past neutral arrangements had been an absence of friction in the administrative work on the County Council,and the work had been carried out with a smoothness and celerity that had made the body a model to the whole country. Mr.Norinan had said that befere long the County Council would have to appoint an Education Committee ; nowithe fact that the election was the first since the Government proposals with re- gard to education bad been made known rendered it an extremely important one.as that important constituency decided what was likely to he the complexion of the next County Council. It meant that in addition to the honours being equally divided, the new committee on Education woulci consist of half Liberals and half Conservatives. The new Education Committee would touch the residents in every township. It would mean that if they had an impartial committee and not division between Chute and Nonconformity they would have such a committee that every parent could appeal to it with a certainty of getting fair play. I</p>
        <p>many parishes in Cheshire there was only one school— the Church school,and the people who sent their child- ren there were generally dependent for food upon the rich men in the neighbourhood who were generally Church men and if the committee had a majority of Churchmen and Tories, even if they were fair-minded men, the Dissenters would not have that confidence in them which was desirable. If, however, it was half and half, the committee would hold a fair and equal balance between thel two' sides. In Runcorn they perhaps knew nothing of these difficulties experienced in small townships for they were perfectly independent and could not declare themselves to be of any religious sect, whereas those in small villages had to depend upon a few rich men for their existence. He therefore asked them to vote Liberal on behalf of those who were less independent than themselves, as those who were strong and able to hold up the knees of the weak. (Ap- planse). He had no doubt that the people of Runcorn did not wish to detach the church people from the faith of their fathers,but they claimed that no public measure should be allowed to influence Non-conformists to de- tach children from their faith. Much had been said of the personality of the two candidates and praise had teen given to Mr.lmison. That had not been overdone. He had served the town well but that was no reason why he should want all the honours. Mr. Norman had not had too much honour shown him while Mr. Imison had been placed in the most honourable position at the disposal of the townsmen—the Chairmanship of the District Council and an " ex-officio " magistrate. Let him be content for a little —(applause) for he had as much to do as he could do well. He was pleased to see the Runcorn Board of Guardians had recently decided to send the children or a portion of them in the house to a neigh- bouring school where they might mix with other chil- dren dressed alike and not in uniform. He had asked Mr. Norman if he had voted for the change, and Mr. Norman had said "certainly, and I never experienced greater pleasure than in doing so." He(Sir John)had per- suaded the Northwich Union to adopt that system some years ago, and the regular attendance of the children combined with the careful preparation of their lessons found them at the top of the school. They might depend upon it that those who were at the top of their classes would never go back to the house of the inmates. Sir John in concluding said it was said that Mr. Imison had done a great deal for Runcorn. He, personally, was not fond of beasting of what he had done but he claimed th,tt he had done something for Runcorn. It was because he had made sacrifices for Runcorn that he loved i the more. It was because he desired Runcorn to prosper, and desired passionately the welfare of every man, woman and child in the town, that he begged of them to vote for the Liberal candidate on Saturday. (LJud applause.) Mr. William Ockle,ston supported the resolution and thought, knowing Mr. Norman's principles, he would be the right man in the right place and would do his duty. He hoped that by a •majority on Saturday Runcorn South would show themselves true to the party. The motion was then put and declared by th 3 Chair- man to be carried, amid loud applause. The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to Sir J. T. Brunner and the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CANDIDATURE OF MR. IMISON.</head>
        <p>THE CANDIDATURE OF MR. IMISON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Imison held his first meeting in support of his aandidature in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Greenway--oad on Wednesday evening. A good attend- ance assembled, over which Mr. S. Taylor, LP., C.C., presided, and there were also present, the eandidate, Mr. E. Imison, Messrs. J.W. Lightburn,J.P., W. Collier, J. R. Frith and Dr. McKenzie. Mr. Taylor, in opening the meeting, expressed the pleasure it gave him to support Mr. Imison as a candi- date for the County Council. Mr. Imison had come out as an independent and had he not done so he (the speaker) would not have been on that platform that night. It was pretty well known in Runcorn that for many years he had taken that view on all matters con- nected with local bodies. Be had opposed politics being introduced because he had never seen what good it could do. He concluded in his own mind years ago that politics in local matters did no good but a deal of harm. When politics were introduced into local matters, feeling ran high and they knew perfectly well who were the sufferers. He referred quite as much to the working ef the County Council as to the Urban Council,the Board of Guardians 1 or the Rural Council,and although he had now served 18 ' months on the County Council he had not seen a single instance that reminded him that there wore politics , either on the Council or in committee. Their friends I seemed to have two questions which they were drumm- indat—pelities and the Education Bill. There had been a great deal made out of the Education Bill but he was quite sure, and he had gone into the matter very cart- fully and had heard opinions on the question.that in the Bill so far as it had gene there was nothing which he as a Nonconformist felt alarmed at. If there was he could assure them that he and his friend Mr. Frith who was els() supporting Mr. Imison. would not have been there. They knew their friend Mr. Imison. There was no broader-minded man in Runcorn,and no man in Runcorn who would take hroader views on all matters than Mr. Imison. If Mr. Imison succeeded in carrying that seat they would have a most valuable representative—(ap- plause)—unbiassed in opinion,with the full object of do- ing everything that lay in his power for the benefit of the peeple in the town. (Applawe.) Mr. Imison said the first word he would like to say to that meting was one of acknowledgement of the hind allusions made to him at the meeting held on Mneday. Mr. Norman spelt° in a very kind manner of his esteem for him,(Mr. Imison) and he would like to acknowledge and reciprocate those sentiments. Every speaker referred inest kindly to him, until they got to the educa- tion question which seemed to have the same effect upon them as a red rag on a bull. Mr. Norman told them that a great deal of trouble had been taken, and a great deal of pressure brought to bear upon him to stand. He wished to enlarge upon that a few mo- ments, and show them how strong the party feeling went in Runcorn. He was right in saying that Mr. Norman was the first gentleman named for the vacancy. Failing him another local gentleman was ap- proached, who was said to be more in harmony with voluntary schools. The matter was laid before him in an indirect manner as to whether he would be opposed, and he expressed the opinion which he expressed to them that night, that if a man aspired to represent them on any public body, he ought to be pre- pared to face the electors and let them know what he would do when he got there to represeet them.</p>
        <p>The prospect of that arrangement not tieing satisfactnrilv considered they approached a gentleman who lived 50 _ _</p>
        <p>Miles away from. litincorn and asked to know whether he would represent them en the County Council. For a party talking about Home Rule.local options and other measures it showed how far .party feeling would carry them when they were satisfied to have representing them a gentle- man who lived at London, who beyond politics could have no other interest in the district. That gentleman declined and the party came back to the candidate who now appeared before them. He was sorry to see him :Wear in such a position, and for this reason. He told them that he was not the nominee of a certain gentle- man. This might technically be correct but as A matter of f set he thought he was iedispatahiv the nnmio„ of a'certain gentleman and his only reeret was that the same gentleman was not the candidate for the constitu- ency that night. The most satisfactory way to put his views before them that night was to deal with the argu- ments used against him on Monday ni,g`st. • With refer- ence to Mr. Norman's statement expressing that the 4s. per head grant tn voluntary aehnels was untair as it only included 125 out of 2,452 Board schools, the reason was that the Board schools had another source of income, while the voluntary schools were limited simply to the amount they received in grants and the money received per subscription. If the Run- corn School Board wanted an additional 4s. per head, they had only to issue a precept. He (Ncir. Imison) also thought that the schools could ,be as well governed under the County Council act they were at present. &amp; good deal had been said ebnirt he (Mr. Imisen) holding too many offices. He himself considered that matter Wal a question more for him than them. (Applause.) It could he said on reflection that his friend Mr. Taylor held more offices than he did. In addition to the offices which he (Mr. Imison) held, Mr. Taylor was a member of the Board of Guardians and also of the County Coen- and there had been no suggestion made that Mr. Taylor's business was suffering in coneequenee, and if it did the loss would fall upon Mr. Taylor. They did not surely think that he was so anxious for honour that he would take an office when he knew that he could not possibly represent them. Although he held these offices his attendances compared favourably with many of the gentlemen who had only one office Mr. Poenett had said of him that he was one of the hot- test Conservatives in the town, and one of the strongest churchmen. As far as that went he agreed with Mr. Posnett, hut he cnule not agree with him when he said that if he (Mr. Imison) went to the County Cluncil he would be a faithful eon of the church and opposed to the interests of the children. It was an that point he crossed swords with Mr. Poenett. It was not wise to prnnhece unless one knew, and when Mr. Pocnett pro- phesied that he would lose his independently end that he would fro simnly in the interests of the chureh and he opposed to the infereats of the children, he did not know. Ha had been a member of the School Board for 13 years and his aetions on that body would point to the fact that such a statement was not correct. Sir John,Brunner had said of him." If Mr. imiaen wont to the eenncil aa an independent neither the Tories nor the Liberals cell(' depend upon him, and that was per- fectly true. (Loud applause.) A man ought net to go to the County Council representing parties but ought to go to guard the general interests of the whole place and that was what he proposed to do. (Applause.) Mr. Imison also challenged Sir John Brunner's statement to the effect that if a Tory was sent to the Counts" Council on Saturday there would be less money spent in County Council scholarships. Mr. Imison pointed out that he had always been in favour of the scholarships, Instead of their being 20 he would like to see 50 free scholarships and he would never be the man under any circumstances te vote for or support any proposal to withdraw any money from technical education. It had been said that he was op.</p>
        <p>posed to the School Board system, but though that was not so he was in favour of the preservation of the voluntary schools. The preservation of voluntary schools was an important matter to them in Runcorn where the number attending the school was over 3000 made up by 1,300 at the board schools and 2,100 at the volun- tary schools. Supposing for a moment they admitted that the voluntary school system was entirely wrong, and their opponents were absolutely right, and volun- tary schools were closed and board schools took their place. They would be in the position of having to raise £30,000 to provide buil.iings only estimated on the cost of building the Victoria-road schools. In addition to that they would have the maintenance expense of £750 per year. He asked them as practical people and ratepayers, were they prepared for that. Assuming that the system wts wrong be did not think it was the time to abolish the system or the right time to bring it about. They were trying to draw new works to the town and if they put before them a double school board rate did they think it likely that new manu- facturers would come ? He maintained that the volun- tary schools had done good work, their teaching staffs were efficient, and he did not think they were without the necessary appliances. In conclusion he wished to say that he was not going as a mere partizan. He , would like to impress upon them that he went as an in- dependent and if elected he would not take instructions from any party. He would go willing to receive sug- gestions from the vicarage, from the Wesleyans, and from St. Edwards, or from the men in the street if he thought they would do any good, and he believed that was what they wanted. (Applause.) Mr. Frith moved the following resolution, "That this meeting considers Mr. unison a fit and proper person to represent the Runcorn Siuth Electoral Division on the County Council and pledges itself to use its utmost en- deavour to secure his triumphant return on Saturday next." Mr. Walter Collier seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. W.Lightburn also addressed the meeting which closed with the usual votes of thanks to the Chairman proposed by Mr.lmison and seconded by Mr. Li'Aler. OPEN AIR MEE LING IN SUPPORT OF MR. NORMAN. _ _</p>
        <p>In furtherance of the candidature of Mr. Norman a meeting was held on Wednesday night on waste land near the station. A good crowd assembled, and the proceedings were of an interesting character. Mr. Speakman, J.P., C.C., occupied the chair. Mr. Norman, who was warmly received, dwelt particularly upon the political aspect of the matter, and gave as instances of the political feeling of the County Council the manner in which the aldermen of the standing committees were appointed, and pointed out that the vacancy caused by the election of Mr. Speakman to the vacant aldermanic seat was due to political feeling. On the Runcorn School Board they knew from experience there was a strong political feeling, and a considerable amount of diplomacy bad to be used to avoid contests. If it was so on a local School Board Mr. Norman claimed that it would be so if the County Council had entire power in the administration of education.</p>
        <p>Mr. King, of Northwicn, moved a resolution to the effect that Mr. Norman was a fit and proper person to sit as a county councillor for the South division and Pledging the meeting to use all legitimate efforts to ensure his return.—Mr. W. Millington seconded and the Rev. Mr. Stafford supported. The meeting concluded with the usual votes of thanks. IHE CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of the week more interest has been evinced in the contest and the supporters of the two can- didates are indefatigable in their endeavours to have their candidate returned on Saturday. A considerable quantity of literature has been displayed by both candidates. Canvassing parties are out in all pr ions of the district, and the " canvasqed " are mainly occupied in discuss'ng the prospects of the respective candidates. Mr. Norman with a band of willing workers has established himself at the Liberal Club,while Mr. Imison and his supporters have made their committee room in Victoria-road.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the polling which takes place to- morrow (Saturday) have been completed. Mr.J. Ashton will officiate as the returning officer, and be has decided to have the votes counted immediately after the closing of the poll, and it is anticipated that the result will be made known abut nine o'clock. Polling commences at eight o'clock a. m.and will go on until eight p.m. The poll- ing booths being at the Victoria-road Board School. The regiater contains the names of 977 electors; through re- movals t,";e number is considerably reduced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn,on Tueslay night. present. Messra. Pritchard (chairman), F. J. Norman, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, G. Moss (clerk) —The School Attendance Committee reported that 28 parents appeared before them for breaches of the Educational Act, and nroceeditms were ordered e.:,0 be taken in five cases.—Dr. Gastaldi proposed and Mr. Norman seconded the adoption of the minutes. —A letter was received from Mr. F. Wiswall. asking the Chairman to accept on behalf of the Board 41 samples of wood be had prepared at the request of Mr. Gilburn.— The Cterk explained that the samples were polished and wer intended to facilitate the instruction of the chi!- ' then —On the motion of Mr. Norman, seconded by the Chairman Mr. Wiswall was cordially thanked for his memorandum from the Liverpool School Board I with reference to the Education Bill was referred to the I School Management Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF A TEACHER,</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF A TEACHER,</p>
        <p>For the appointment to- the position of asiistant macter at the Victoria-road Blys'Board School at a salary of £75 ,qixteen applications were:received.The applicants were reduced to four, who were summoned to meet the hoard, and ultimately William Joynson of Widnes, who at pre-Pot is receivina his education at Chester Train- ing College, was appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before His Honour, Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. A FAMILY MONEY LENDING TRANSACTION.—Mrs. Bertha Trim')le sued George Williams. of Ashridge-st., for £25 3s. 4d. principal and interest on money lent--Mr. F. A. Greer in- structed by Mr. Burton appeared for plaintiff and Mr. A. Browne for d•-fendant.—Mrs. Trimble said she was the wife of Thomas Trimble. Defendant was her brother. In July, 1888, she was a schoolmistress and had beets for six yeus,and had saved £lO5. In July of that year she lent her brother £ES at the rate of 5 per Cent per annum for building pur- • oses. Defendant was to begin to pay Interest when the houses were tenanted. .The houses became tenanted in November and she spoke to him about the money and in- terest, and it was agreed that she should have the money that came in from rents after other payments had been made. At that time defen ant gave her an 1.0. U. He did net dis- pute nor had he since disputed the amount of ihterest. In December, sth. 18;9, she went to live in one of the houses,the ' rentof which was 4s. 3d. per weak. She paid the rent for some time, and after that in November. 1890. she ceased to pay, and made an arrangement with her brother to credit him with the payment of the money be owed her in lieu of the rent she did not pay. They went on that system until ‘leptember 1891. She then saw the defendant about the £lO she owed him for rent. Her mother was present. She reckoned 4s 31. per week for 47 weeks, which was £9 19s. 9d., and she told her brother that. Her mother gave them 3d. to make up the £lo.—Cross-examined : The £lO5 had not been savel out of money earned by her brother.—Mrs. Williams, mother of the two parties, said she was preSent when the ar- rangement was made to lend the £lO5 at 5 per cent. per annum which was to be paid out of the rent of houses her son had intended building. At this stage a settlement was ar- rived at by the parties, the defendant agreeing to the pay- ment of £22,—Mr. Browne on behalf of the defendant, ex- pained that he thought the money was on bank interest, and had never read the documents 111 had signed. He did not to go into the box to contradict his mother.—His Honour said he was pleased to see the case settled out of Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>CHURCH MISSIONARY SOCIETY.—The annual sermons on behalf of the Missionary Society were preached in the Parish Church. Frodsham, on Sunday, by the Rey. H. O. Squires. of Rlchmond, formerly a missionary in India. The collections taken amounted to £l3. The annual public meeting was held in the Town Hall on Monday evening, presided over by the Vicar, the Rev. H. B. Blogg.. M.A.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF SCHOOL MANAGERS.-011 Monday evening, a meeting was held in the National School, Flelshy, for the purpose of appointing schrol managers. The Vicar, the Rev. A. Spaffird, occupied the chair. After the subject had been fully explained the election was conducted and Mr. Joseph Taylor was appointed treasurer and Messrs. G. Crosland Taylor, James White and Joseph Jeff, managers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CHESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL SCHOLARSHIPS.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL SCHOLAR- SHIPS.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF THREE RUNCORN STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>The report respecting the recent examination of chil- dren attendina elementary schools in Cheshire for the scholarships offered by the County Council has just come to hand, and Runcornians will be pleased to learn that out of the twenty-fou scholarships offered, three have been secured by Runcorn students. The names of the successful candidates are Walter Retler,ll3.Leinster Gar- dens, educated at the National Schools; George Jones, Weaver Cottage,Vitest on Point,educated at Victoria-road Board Schools; ani Harold B. Rogers, Weston-road, Weston, educated at Brunswick-road Boari Schools. Twentythree candidites presented themselves from Run- e.orosthe total numb.rs sitting at the examination being 343, or 120 girls and 223 boys.</p>
