      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>TILL-ROBBING BY LIVERPOOL BOYS. On Friday, at the Warrington Borough Court, before Aldermen Harrison and Wright, and Councillore Pier• point and Roberts, Christopher Robinson, 25, Soho-st., Liverpool, James Webb, 91, Rose Place, Liverpool, and Joseph Sutton, 40, Jenkinson-st., Liverpool, youths of about 17, were charged with stealing on the 22nd May from the shop of Ernest Miller, 19, Greenall-st.. some scones and 10a. 01 in cash.—The Chief Constable said that at 2.30 on the day mentioned _Mrs. Miller went upstairs for some domestic purpose and remained there . for some time leaving the shop door open, and the shop unoccupied. A neighbour named 'Rachel "Simister was I -ale() upstairs in her own house and w le there, through the window saw the three defendant e I rking about Mrs. Miller's shop. She saw Sutton walk away and keep watch. The other two then went into the shop, and came out hurriedly and ran away. Rachel Simister then told Mrs. Miller, who looked round the shop and missed i the scones and 103. in cash. Amongst the coins was a ' threepenny piece of peculiar appearance. Information was given to the police, and in the afternoon P.C. Wood met the three youths in Orford-lane and recognising them from a description, he. arrested them. When searched scones were found upon them and also money, and .. Sutton had in his possession the identical threepenny ,- — piece. Last Saturday the lads had been before the Court and remanded.—Rohinson,speaking for the others, candidly admitted the theft, and said that the money ~, was staring them in the face from the,open till and they took it. They also took the scones because they were hungry.—Mrs. Miller denied that the till was open, ani . the Chief Constable said the lads were,- he thought, regular till-thieves as two of them wore rubber goloshes —a regular thing in the profession.—Defendants were all committed to goal for one month's hard lab Our. AMERICANS IN TROUBLE. Before the Mayor and Messrs. Sutton and Ridgway at the Warrington Borough Court on Thursday, James Bayliss and John Meisenpach, lately from America,were charged with being found in possession of two gold bracelets and other articles, supposed to have been stelen.—The Chief Constable said that at 3 o'clock p.m. on Wednesday, P.C. Newey was called to Jackson's pawnshop in Winwick-street, and saw the prisoner Bay- liss trying to dispose of a gold bracelet of peculiar work- manship. It had upon it a monogram—apparently " 8.E." Newey questioned Bayliss and he said the article was his and that be found it in New York, 18 mouths ago. He was brought to the Police Station where he varied his statement. He said the bracelet belonged to another man who was at Daly's lodging- house. At the latter place Meisenpach was found. He claimed the bracelet as his and said that he found it in London three weeks ago. Bayliss said he was with him when it was found. In Meisenpach's possession was the sum of £3 10s. sd. almost all in shillings. They had also a glass-cutter, another bracelet.and a gold ring. Bayliss too, had a razor and knife. On suspicion the prisoners were detained and the Chief Constable now asked for a -week's remand.—The remand was granted. HAD NE COME TO ELECTROCUTE ANYBODY? On Thursday morning at the Warrington Police Court. a young man named Robert Fairhurst, of 17, Bradley- street, Manchester, was charged with being found in possession of a magneto-electric machine, supposed to have been stolen.—The Chief Constable informed the bench that he scarcely knew what to do with the case. On Wednesday night, the defendant who was appar- ently not quite " right " was found wandering about the town with a new electric battery sander his arm. He made several statements as to how he got it but they nretty much amounted to the same thing—that he had bought it at a shop in Manchester. He (the Chief Constable) had telephoned to the Manchester police about the defendant, but for some reason or other best known to themselves th-y refused to hold communica- tion with anybody outside Manchester.—A. remand of 24 hours was granted for further enquiries. DISHONESTY AT MESSRS. CROSPIELD AND SONS' WORM. The magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Thursday morning dealt with a lad of 14, named Fred White, Snutsford-road, who was charged with stealing from the works of Messrs.Crosfield and Sons' two lbs. of soap value 81—White admitted the theft.—The Chief Constable said that the boy was up to Wednesday in the employ of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons', and for some time past quantities of soap had been missed. The firm suspected that it was taken by the boys, and as they were put to considerable loss, watch was kept and on Wednesday the defendant's basket was examined as he was leaving and 21b. of soap, the property ot the firm, found in it. They felt that it wag a case that should be made an example of. Detectives Barlow and Roper took the lad into custody, and searched the house of his parents,who are respectable people. Mts. White handed to them two tablets of soap which she said the boy had brought home with him. The Chief Constable added that he was informed that the firm had refrained from prosecuting in a great many cues, but to .such an ex- tent had the robberies been going on—eight or nine boxes were taken in a day—that they asked that the lad might be punished.—Mrs. White begged for lenient treatment of the defendant, who she said had done nothing wrong before.—The Bench sentenced the boy to three days' imprisonment in the cells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MINOR CASES. '</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. '</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alderman Wright, Councillor Pierpoint, and Councillor Roberts. Druns AT SEVENTEEN.—A youth of 17, named Philip Thompson, 10, Buckley-street. was charged with being drunk and disor +e-ly in Scotland-road. at 10.15 on Saturday night —He pleaded guilty and P.C. Corbett said he found defend- and drunk land fighting with another youth on Saturday night. He brought him to the Police *Station to prevent further disturbance.—Mr. Talbot said the lad had been locked up for Sunday night. He had been out on bail since.—The bench cautioned defendant and discharged him. ALLEGED CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Charles Simonds, 15, Arpley-st., was charged with having on the 25th Mar illtreat• .ed a horse by working it in an unfit state.—P.C. Beacham stated that on the day in question he saw defendant with a waggonette and two horses. The near horse seemed dis- tressed, It had wounds on the shoulder which were bieed- ing. Defendant at his request took the horse in the stable.— P.C. Dela.hay eorroborated.—Defendant said he was asked by Mr. Maginn to take the brake to Frodsham. He went out and found the horses already harnessed. He himself did net know that one of the horses had sore shoulders.—Mr. Maginn who was sent for by the Bench, said the horse had no wounds on its shoulders when it went out. He did not know who had harnessed the horses.—The magistrates said they thought the defendant was Ignorant of haying broken the law and they dismissed the case. They further expressed the opinion that they did not agree with. drivers being summoned for the lameness of their horses. as an many eases they were not res- porrable.—lnspector Btlson, R.S.P.C.A., prosecuted —A similar charge was brought against John Dutton and Sarah Dutton, 62, Lovelv-lane,the former for working the horse and the latter tor causing it to be worked.—As Mrs. Dutton. the owner of the horse, was in poor health and circumstances and -the Inspector thought the horse would recoverwer from its lame- ness with a fortnight's reg. the bench adjourned the case for 14 days to see if the animal was trdatel as required. HE GOT HIS Elms BOXED. —A boy named John Robinson, 4 Matthew-et., summoned a married man, Herbert Thoup- so.n, 6, Matthew-st., for assaulting him. Defendant pleaded</p>
        <p>guilty, but added that he had only boxed the complainant's ears for using" cheek" towards him and also for striking his little son. He detailed the cause of the quarrel and com- plainant's behayiour.—The Chief Constable said the boy's mother had herself admittel to him that there was not a more mischleyious by in the town than her son, John Robinson. Ths case was dism'ssed.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. John Burgess. THE THEFT OF WIRE ROPE.—Alfred Lea who was remanded from We inesdav on a charge of stealing a quantify of wire-rope was again brought before the B ench. —The Chief Constable said inquiries had resulted in the wire-rope being found to have been stolen from Messrs. B. Evans and Co., in the borough of St. Helens. Defendant would now have to be handed over to the St. Helens police.— Joseph High's , a Constable in the St. Helens police force, said the theft had been committed at Parr Flat farm, and was the property of Messrs. R. Uvans and Co. —Lea was halide 1 over to the St. Helens pollee, an ion Monday morning at the St. Helens police Court was sent to gaol for 7 days' ha-d labour. A SEARED HILISBAYD. —ATM Peen WAS summoned by her husband, John Peers, for tying threats towards him on the 27th of May. whereby he was afrail.- -BAh parti-s are ad- vanced in years and had been married a long time. Peers said his wi'e had threatened "to have his life, to murder him, to make him stiff," and she had also prophesied 'that he would be a corpqe before morning." He wanted A seroara- tionl from her.—The Bench dismissed the charge as de- fendant was willing to live with a married daughter on beiag allowed maintenance. Peers WAS advised to grant her Sc. a week, the sum she asked for. MONDAY.—Before Mr. Monks (in the chair). Aldermen Francomb and Harrison, and Messrs. r. Birchall and S.</p>
        <p>Wright. INrunratmENT OF THE MARGARINE ACT.—Robert Whit- field, shopkeeper 38 and 40 Chorley-street, was summoned for selling margarine in paper without the word " Margarine" upon it. —Mr. Ralph Norman, sanitary inspector, said under his direction a girl was sent into the defendant's shop to purchase a pound cif butter. A woman, wit) was behind the cownter at the time, said teat she hid bmter at la, and 15,35. a pound. She gave her the biter in paper which had not the name ofl the article Oh it --Mr. J. Longlan I explained ore behalf of the defendant that Mrs. Whitfield had used la,r last margarine paper,and unfortunately this took place before anybody came into the shop whom she cauld send tor more papers. Carelessly, she wrapped it in a plain paper and delivered it to the purchaser. He hoped their worships would not look with serious intent on the offence. It was done out of sheer carelessness. It had greatly upset the poor win' an. It was a mere technical offence. —Their wor- ships decided to dismiss the case on payment of costs. A CAB-DRIVER FINED. —For being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cab on the 23rd May, in Horsemarket-street. Ernest Wead was fined 55. OBSTRUCTION. —A broker named John Gandy of Napier-st , , was fined a similar sum for obstructing Bewsey-street by leaving a cart there 40 minutes on the 28th May. CHIMNEY ON FlRE.—Chtrles Fox. Heath Side, was fined ss. for setting his chimney on fir J on the 24th May and John M'Gann for allowing his chimney to be on fire was find is. ALLEGED CRUELTY —Samuel Allen, a youth of about 17, was charged with illtreating a horse t v beating it.—lnspector Bilson. U.S P.O A.. said that the wife of the Rev. E. U. Reed had complained to him about d Jendant beating his horse in Museum-street on the 17th May. He had since examined the horsesand found weals on his legs. Its mouth was also core with being "jagged."— Defendant denied striking the horse and told a story which was believed by the batten, who clisnaisled the case. VuEsnA.Y.—Before Mr.R, Garnett (chairman) and Alderman</p>
        <p>Wright. DRUNK AND DI§ORDF:RL/.—Thomas Kenny was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Marsh House-lane early 'that morning. P.C. Clough and P.C. Turner proved the Case. -The defeneant stated that he had come to join the 3rd South Lances'eire Militia. -The Chief Coestable said he had received complaints feem the inhabitants aboet disorderly conduct in that neighbourhood. H i had had to put extra polcemen on clety round there. The prisoner was com- mitted to not for fourteen days hard labour. AN UtfetteeY MARRIA.GR.—WiIIiam Buckley Taylor. slater, of 22. Dallem-line, was ehereed under awarraut with assault- ing his wife. Alice, on the 30th Mey.—Mr. P. Davies. solici- tor, proseented. and Mr. Jenkins, solicitor, defended.—ln opening the case. Mr. Davies remarked that it w is one of the saddest he had had to do with. The defendant's wife was a yonng woman. 21 yeirs of age, and she was married to the defendant three years ago. They had three children, Florence aged three veers. Ada two, and William, nine months. On Sales day night, between five and six o'cleck, the defendant came home. One of the children was sitting on the doorstep and simply said " Daddy." This seemed to infuriate him Slid he said to the child in a rude way, 'Go home to your own Daddy,' ma wife then interposed ani said to the defendent that fOiil did not know how he could use such language. The defendant was always making those insinuations. He afterwards struek his wife on the face, stating that if it was not for the law he would hang her up and cut her to pieces. lie also knocked the child from her arms, but the complainant succeeded in catching it by the stains. After- waeda he deliberatelv kicked her. The complainant was a wernen the defendant should be proud of. There WAS no occasion for jeeleusy. She saw the Chief Cmstable and he advised her what to do. A full warrant was granted the pre. rioes day for the arresc of the defendaat who also kicked the complainant's sister.—Mr.W.H.J.Jenkins sail he was oily in- structed at five minutes to ten, and that was insufficient time to meet such a charge. The defendant was a member of the Fire Brigade and had never been brought to that police court before. He would ask the magistrates to adjourn the case.— Mr. Davies objected to such a course, and said it would be unfair to the witnesses. The magistrates decided to proceed with the case,—Alice Taylor, the complainant, corroborated her solicitor's statement. In cross-examinatioa,she said they had not got on well during their married life. She denied that she threatened to throw the defendant's fireman's clothes on the fire, or that she used most obscene language to her husband in the street on the night in question. She did not say to her husband that the children were not his.—Elizabeth Turner, 22, Dallem-lane, Sarah Newell, sister to the com- plainant, Ellen Atherton. 46 Dellam-lane. Margaret Smlth,lB Dallana-lane, and Mary Ann Mee also gave evidence for the prosecution.—The Chief Consteble stated that the complain- ant came to him a little after 6 o'clock on Saturday night.and was in a very nervous and excit d state. She as trembling In every limb. She told him what hl occurred, and he ad- vised her what to do.—For the defence, Mr. Jenkins said the case was a most unhappy one. The tempers of the complain- ant and defendant were not suited to one another, and an- other feature which had had the effect of marring their lives was that they were mixed up with their relatives. The moment any little difficulty arose, sides were taken and a row ensued. When he heard the opening statements he thought the charge was on the verge of manslaughter, but it had now filtered down to a row, the result of the temper of the d fendent's wife. He proceeded to refer to the meddliag of the relatives, and the defendant's previous good character. By her conduct the complainant irritated him, and on the seer of the moment he struck her in the face. He did not kick her stall, and no witnesses had said so. He asked the meeistretes te, dismiss the case.— After evidence by Mrs. O'Brien. 24. Dallane-lane, Clara Lee, 18, Dallam-lane, Mrs. White, and Mrs. Buckley, the magistrates said they had decided that an assault had been committed. The defendant would be bound over to keep the peace for six months, him- self in a surety of EU) and another of £lO. He must also pay the costs —Mr. Davies applied for the payment of the advo- eate's fee and this was granted. The costs amounted to £2 is—The hearing of the case lasted almost two hours, WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Feirclough. DRAFATION —A man named George Walsh was charged with being a deserter from the Royal North Lancashire Regi- ment stationed at Freston, since June Ist. He pleaded guilty.—P.O. Wood said that at 8-45 p.m. on the day in ques- tion he was stationed et Market Gate when defendant passed Witness recognised him from a description which was cir- cuisted from Preston and accosted defendant. The latter at first deniei the offence bet at the Police Station admitted that he was the man wante I. He said he was a colout- sergeant and orderly room clerk.—The Chief Constable said this was true and that defendeet 'cad been 13 years in the army. He had got almoit as high as he could le rank. He had kept his books carelessly and was ashamed to meet his Colonel—Defendant was handed over to the military authorites and the constable Was recommended for the usual reward. Tatman AY.—Before the' Mayor (chairman), Alderman Sutton, and Colonel RiciftWAy. DRUNIERNNRAR AND BRITTALITY.—SaMOCI Dagnall. Silver- street, a man of most disreputable appearance, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Silver-square on Wednes- day night he was further charged with assaulting Mary Dunn at th • same time and place.—Sergeant Cheney said that at lip m. on Wednesday night he saw the prisoner drunk and disorderly in Silver-square. He was using bad language and before witness could get to him he kicked a woman twice. She gave him no provocation.—Prisoner said he was too drunk to walk never mini kick anybody.—He was sent to gaol for 14 days with her i labour.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.-- Before Mr. John Crofiehl (chairman), Mr</p>
        <p>John White and Mr. W Houghton. LICENSE TRANSFER.—MIZIbeaI Taylor, widow o! Samuel Taylor, was granted the transfer of " The Boathouse," Rixton to herself. DRUNK AND DisnithEßLY.—For being guilty of drunken- ness and disorderly conduct, the following persons were fined : -James Woods Croft, George Welsbv, Croft and Peter Haves, Croft, were fined ss.fand costs, and Mary Cunning- ham Orford, fined ss. ; Peter G4rzitty, Sankey ss. including costs SLEEPY DRIVERS.—Fm• belga asleep in charge of lorries, in Sankey on the 23rd May, Patrice O'Donnell and Robert McMullen were fined Irls. inclu ling costs each.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs</p>
        <p>lig-. J. Norman and J. W.</p>
        <p>Li ghtburn.</p>
        <p>PROFANITY.—EIIen Moran failed to answer to a charge against her of using obscene language in Mersey-st. on April 25th.—A warrant was granted.--John Ki!grass. Weston, was charged with using ob.cene language on My 16th in High-st. P.C. Gregory proved the case. and said defendant was in company with a man present in court who was also using bad language, end would have ben summoned only he got away. Defend tnt who ple npled not vullty called as a witness the man singled out by P.C. Gregory. The witness stated that no bad language was used in fact they were only talking politics.—Superintendent Okell : You did not use any had language ?—Witness: I could not. -Superintendent Okell : Did you give evidence for the defendant the last time he was here.—Witness: No air. I've never been here before Superintendent Okell You will be here next time. Defend- ant who had been previously convicted fora similar offence was fined ss. awl Ils. 6d. costs. DRUNK AND DISIRnF.RLY. —Thomas and Willtam Inzles- field, labourers of Mill Brow, were charged with being drunk and disorderly on May 25h, in Stringer's Court.—P.C, Hughes proved the case.— Defendant Thomas was fined ss. and costs or 14 days. as he had been previously convicted and the other defendant was fined 29. 6d. and costs or seven days. LicxxamGßUSlNESS.—Luke Holmes applied for permission to sell at the Anchor Inn, Brunswick-street. lat?.ly occupied by Henry Arnold and permission was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The cases at the se;sions on Saturday were dealt with in two courts. The magistrates pres.nt were :—Mr. T. J. Gillespie, Colonel Wood, 11r, C. B. F. Borron and Mr. It. Stone.</p>
