      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 6, 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 6, 1896</p>
        <p>reference to the Railway Hotel, Earlestown, occupied by Mr. George Apoleton.lt was proposed to make certain structural alterations but these would not affect the portions of the hotel used for trade purposes. It was intended to erect three bedrooms and a bathroom, and the enlargement of the kitchen and washheuse.—The magistrates gave their assent to the enlargement. There were no objections. DRUNK ENNEsB.—The following were fined for being drunk: —Hugh Cain. Earlestown, 55.; Patrick Donnelly, Earles- town, 53. and Costs; Thomas Herren. ss. and costs ; Thomas Ball, Haydock, 2s, 63. Ana costs ; Thomas Fannon, Haydock, be. and costs. For being drunk and disor deny the following were dealt with: —F. Lord, If, aslestown. 2s. 6d. and costs; Thomas Pi•kington, Hay, tock, 2s. 61. and costs ; Aaron Morris, Haydock, 2s. 6d. and costs; James Farrington and Michael Sc illighan. Hay lock, 23. 63. and costs. ASSAULTS AID CETARGE3 OF ASSAULTS—James Thompson, Galborne, was surnan )ned on a charge of assaulting William Hill at Golborne, on the 9th ult.—Mr. Stuart, .solicitor, apoeared for the defence. The evidence showed that the defendant went to the complainant's house to . uire about some furniture beloneine to a Mrs. Wood. mg defends:at ns ed cam—Thomas Craven was summenr:oll of t blows were struck.—The magistrates decided to di miss the ca f wanted to fetch the articles away and a row e The in which e tance o Henry Simm, of Haydoek, on a cha— the ins He was fined 23. 6,1 and costs. A cross-su rumens assault. hSi Grace in w hichmm was charged with assaulting ary ann , son of yornas tha ', Craven was ptayiug with a nuinee Craven had also been issued. The evid; „ was to the effect 0 oys when shuns assaulted him The magistr ates said an assault had been proved bay, they thought that Simla sn,„ia have had more pay the c)sts.—Patrick Charles was sun;m med for assaulting sense than pay with a number of boys He was ordered to of Ann Stanton on the 25th May complainant stated that defendant struc her at Golf:game. The tha in her own h m se. Tins e witnesses. The defendant was corroborated by two tla at the complainant WAS fighting Was the magistrates that lit aveut to separate them. In with another woman and doing so he was assaulted. Corroburcive evidence haring been given, the magistrates odnilnitheslithth '-'ayVaesTsa-u\llitc'el(ll 'Paid I s'le; I.7l•Yanwe'Los liarlestown. Tee defend tut did , suntannedvr3 r.s t John ifiour -street, thavingro et. that he had gone to South Afri not gone the previous night, not appear and it was stated ca._uss Cooper sail he had , the case. She deposed that ou Jane Cooper gave corroboratiand she decided to go on with r the date named the del endant struck her on the face and theatene3 to kill her.—Miss Mary and casts or 14 days' imp i FIGHTING vs evidence. and a fine of 10s. -sonment was impnsed. IN THE STREe.rs.—Fialph. Rudd was charged with committing 3 breach of the peace by fighting at Newton named he saw the on the lath May--P.C. Turner stated that at 2.30 on the date fighting together.—P.C. defend ant and another man named Holden Williamson gave co-roborative evi- dence. Defendant was bound (neer to keep the peace for six months in his own recognisances of £s.—Thomas Hickson and James Mitchenson were similarly dealt with for coma- tin a breach of the A CA,E T Peace at Newton on the 9th ult. UNDER HE FIRST OFF ,NDZRS' Ace.—May Tun- stall, a girl, 17 years of age, re siding at Earl °stover', was charged with having on the 18th May at Newton stolen four pairs of boots, value 303., the property of Jolla Lambert, shoe-dealer. Earlestown. Mr. Knowles, solicitor, represented the defendant, and pleaded guilty. He stated that he had had a conversation with Mr. Taylor (Police Court Missionary) and he would, if the magistrates agreed, take her to some home. The girl was seventeen years of age and on becoming twenty-one she would came int) possession of some money. It would be a pity if she were branded as a criminal. He had arranged that Mr. Lambert should be repaid out of the money the girl would receive. —The Chairman said he was pleased it had been settled in that way. The case would be de alt with underthe First Offenders' Act, and she would be bound over tocome up when cane I upon. CRUELTY TO A HORSE—.Charles Blanthorne, of St. Helens, was charged with having on the 18th May cruelly illtreated a horse by causing it to he worked in an unfit state, and Dennis Dolan, a youth, was summoned for working the same.—ln- snector Bilson, R.S P.C.A.. stated that at 5.3 ) on the 18th May he was at Newton Fair when he saw Dolan riding a horse about the place. die saw that it was very lame and stopped it. On examination ha found that the animal was suffering from rineborte and sidebone o 4 the off-foreleg. The tendons were contracted and they were heated and inflamed. The horse was suffering pain and should net have been worked.—ln- speetor Luff gave corroborative evidense.—The defendant Blanthorne said the horse Was not lame. —The magistratea said they were satisfied that it was, and he would be fined 20s. and costs. Dolan was fined be. and costs.</p>
        <p>DAMAGE TO PROPERTY BY Boys —lo3eoh Sandeman, a youth, residing with his parents at Newton.was charged with having between the 30th Ilarch and the 3rd May, wilfully damaged certain property at Newton to the amount of 26. Mr. Stuart, solicitor, said ha was instructed on behalf of Lord Newton to pro?ecute. The house at which the damage had been committed was an unoccupied one and was previously In the occupation of Mrs. Martin, He believed it was to be acquired by the postal authorities. It had been discovered that the whole of the windows at the back of the house had been broken, the wire attached to the bell hal been cut and the bell removed. The defeadant was only one of five or six boys. all younger than himself, who had done the damage, but by his action he had encouraged the others to do it. Sandeman was seen to throw stones and pieces of brick through the windows with the ohject of cutting the wire at- tached to the bell. In doing so the wall paper and the ceil- ing had beers cut and on the floor there was a heap of stones which had been thrown by the defendant and the other lads In his company. P.C. Williamson found the bell in the pos- session of the defendant, and that was all that had been put down as damage done.— Samuel O'Neil and George Vero, two boys,spoke to seeing the defendant throw through the windows at the tiell.—James Howard, in the employ of Lord Newton, said he had visited the house,and the wall and ceiling had been very much damaged by stone throwing. The bell produced would be worth about s. About twenty-five panes of glass had been broken at the back of the house.— P.C. Williamson stated that when he found the belt in the poscession of the defendant he told witness that he had picked it up in the road —The defendant said he never threw at the bell or knocked it off.—The Chairman said stone-throwing was becoming an insufferable nuisance. This Was the first case that had come before them,and the defend- ant would be fined 108. includiug costs, and pay 2s. damage. If stone-throwing continued the magistrates would deal with the defendants more severely. ILLEGAL FtsatNG.—James Atherton was summoned for having on the Bth May unlawfully and wilfully attempted to take fish frem Newton Lake, the property of Lord Newton, and of which he had private fishing rights. The defendant pleaded guilty—Mr. Stuart, solicitor. prosecuted. He stated that on tbe date named a man named Barrow in the employ of Lord Newton, saw the defendant putting into the lake a fishing line which had been baited. When the defendant saw the keeper he packed up his tackle. He had been previously warned alamit fishing in the lake.—A fine of is. and costs was imposed. CHARGE oF WIFFE DESERTION. —Samuel Buckley, formerly of Earlestown. but now living at Preston Brook, was charged with having on the 19th February, deserted his wile.—The defendant who was represented by Mr. Stuart. solicitor, pleaded not guilty.—Mary Buckley, wife of the defendant stated that her husband Ipft her on the 19th February. He had never been to see her since, and had not offered her a horns or asked her to come back. She had written asking him for money but he had not sent any. He had been employed as a carter for Mr. France in Crow-lane and earned a 21 per week. In cross examination she said she was sure the de- fendant left her on the 19th February. About three weeks afterwards she went to the Old Bull Hotel. She stopped there for one month. She did not agree to separate. They were married on Christmas Day, last year. Her husband suffer.d from a disease soon afterwards but she was not the cause of it. She remembered writing a letter, saying she was going to service again. She denied that she had been away to Preston with some rm.—Mr. Stuart said there was no desertion in the matter. By the letter produced written by the complainant, it was clear that both husband and wife had agreed on a temporary separation while they made a bit of money. The defendent had been out of work in conse- Tienteof 11 ness and had been living with his parents. If the defendant had deserted her, why did she not immedi- ately take out a summons, instead of going to service ? Samuel Buckley and Mrs. Buckley having given evidence, the Chairman said the magistrates were not at all satisfied wth the evidence and would dismiss the ease. If the com- plainant had anything further to bring against the defendant she could do it through the Poor Law guardians. AN UNMUZZLED UoG.—Willianit Lloyd, Newton, was fined 3s. 64. ineluding costs for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled.</p>
        <p>and to ensure such perfection as is attained by this firm the fruit is purchased some eighteen months before the last process can be said to be accomplished. The purity of all preserves is guaranteed by the firm, but perhaps the most substantial guarantee of the excel- lence of Messrs. Walter Robertson's productions is the fact that their Jellies, Jams, Cakes and Confections are in greater demand than evsr after the multiplied testa of nfty years.</p>
