      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CONFECTIONS A LA MODE.</head>
        <p>CONFECTIONS A LA MODE.</p>
        <p>I was dining with the Countess of her sub- urban seat at Syclenham, when my attention was ar- rested by the effective set ' of her table. As lam on intimate terms with her ladyship I remarked " How delightful your table appears ?" She smiled and said graciously: " Yes, thanks to Walter Robertson's Chel- sea Table Jellies ; I scarcely know hew we should man- age without them and I am sure they are as nice as they look." No more was said upon the subject at that time, but happening not long afterwards to find myself in the quaint old town of Chelsea, and walking down the Queen's Road West, on my way to a certain club in the vicinity, I noticed a handsome new building bearing the legend :—" Messrs. Walter Robertson and Co., manufacturers of Table Jellies, and Wholesale and E. port Confectioners." It has always been one of my idiosyncrasies o neep into busy places and watch busy hand's at work. True ta its leading I entered and re- questei permission to Tour.' Mr. Walter Robertson, the originator of the famous Jellies and the kind-hearted proprietor of the factory, was out, but his manager was in and immediately acceded to my. Tequest. I was allowed to inspect, hate, taste, and freely criticise everything I wished to, and before I quitted the premises bad grasped the truth that Chelsea Jellies don't "come famous of themselves," but are so because tho most scrupulous care and attention are bestowed upon them, and only the choicest materials are used in the production. But lam anticipating ! Following my guide into the " Jelly-Boiling" Depart- ment I observed several rows of capacious copper pans, heated by steam, and, standing before these, men with muscular arms and determined faces. Their work con- sisted in stirring the contents of the boiler. These were fine white loaf sugar, glucose and water, into which was weighed a quantity of first-class gelatine. The gelatine is brought into the factory in huge sacks, and soaked all , the night previous to its use, in ta...ks filled with pure water. When the t- ugar, water, glucose, and gelatine, and essence of " flavouring" have boiled together for about half an hour in the copper pans, the liquid is poured out from these vessels, which work in sockets, through muslin into " Coolers," i.e. large shallow pans. From thence it is baled into tb tins, resembling cake tins. Etch tin is moulded with the familiar "W. R. and Co., Chelsea " ; as each is filled it is placed on the scales and weighed. When the boil has all been " tinned " the tins are stacked for coolint purposes upon slate slabs, and the effect of these tiers of tins, raised one above another to the height of a wall, is quite imposing.</p>
        <p>After standing:all night to cool, the jellies'are passed en in their tins to the workers at the cutting te.bles. These are girls,and many of them are very nice girls too; well conducted,,, ell dressed,and well paid. The workers then turn the jellies fromthe first set of tins into others divided on each of the four &amp;des into sections, down which a powerful guillotine-knife moves, separating the jelly into the desired eise—half pint,pint or quart,as the case may be. The divisions;are then tossed on to large trays,for the convenience of distributing to the different tables where the wrappers and packers are engaged in securing them in the neat coverings with which every housewife is familiar. Standing in the centre of the packing room, with the bright light from the windows in the roof falling alike upon the gaily-coloured trans- parent jellies, the light tissue packing papers and the pretty little card-board boxes, the sight presented is most unique. _ _</p>
        <p>In another Department I came upon groups of workers in "Oriental Delight." This delicious and tempting sweet- meat is made in a very similar way to the "Table Jelly." I watched its manipulation with interest. At small square tabl,,s, scrupulously elean, and fitted with backs and narrow side-pieces, I saw the confectioners cutting it into small squares, after having powdered it freely with sifted loaf sugar. Such a demand is there at the 'present time for both ' Jellies 'and • Oriental Delight ' that the firm is kept constantly busy with increasing orders from all parts of the country, and is not accumulating stock at all. Every year the output is larger, nor is this to be wondered at when it is remembered that the Walter Robertson Table Jellies' are far superior to any Table Jellies upon the market, both as regards purity, flavour, nutriment, appearance and the cleanliness of sur- roundings and atmosphere amid which they are manu- factured.</p>
        <p>Passing from the "Jelly " and " Oriental " Depart- ments, I entered those set apart for the manufacture of ice cream drinks. I have frequently tasted the cooling and refreshing beverage, known as the " Chelsea Con- centrated Ice Cream," which I have always considered a special boon, reducing,as it does, the making of ices to a perfect and reliable method. Possessed of a small packet of vanilla or raspberry concentrated ice cream, what could be simpler than to pour upon the contents half a pint of hot water, and, this coding, an additional pint of either water, :nilk, or cream, freezing the whole in the usual manner? A boon indeed to thirsty souls during the hot months of Summer and Autumn ii this cream ; nothing could be more delicious, less expensive Or more quickly made. On my way to the Fruit Preserving Premises, I wan- dered through labyrinths of sugar sacks handsomely filled ; sacks of gelatine and barrels of glucose until I paused for a short time before a number of young box- makers at work. The dexterity with which the wood Is sawn, put together and nailed, affords an illustration of what continual practice in any one industry can accomplish. Passing tempting trays of iced citron, and candied peel, huge cases of bon-bons; long benches of Devonshire Toffee, similar in taste to the 'Broken Scotlh 'of the North, but much lighter in colour; I arrived in due time in the fruit preserving portion of the manufactory. Oae of Messrs. Walter Robertson's noted specialities is "Apricot Jam." The finest apricots are imported from France, and this firm imports them direct in large quantities. The fruit arrives in perfect condi- tion, and is prepared in a way that allows it to retain its full flavour, while the colour, is vivid but natural, no- thing being added to the fruit to enhance its hue and great care being exenised not to destroy or impair it. Nothing but the purest loaf sugar is used. When one con- siders the high standard of comfort now attained by the Enflish working-classes, compared with what was pow sible to the wage-earning population of two or three generations ago, certainly there is no encouragement for the pessimists of to-day, so far as their views and com- ments upon the condition of the "lower" classes is con- cerned. Delicacies which a lady in well-to-do circum- stances could scarcely have afforded to place upon her table some twenty years ago or less, are now within the reach of quite the poorest people: thus anyone may buy three pounds of Apricot Jam for 10Id. Who then would prefer so-called " butter " ? It is not alone for " Apricot" preserve that Messrs. Walter Robertson are justly celebrated, they are quite as much appreciated for their Strawberry, Raspberry, Currant, and Plum Jams, which are sent out in large quantities North, South. East, and West, -and stocked extensively by the high-class Groce'd, Confectioners, and Provision Dealers.</p>
        <p>" You should ply Ili a visit a f:•w weeks latter on " Mid the manager, "When the New Season's fruit will becoming in"</p>
        <p>I said I should be very pleased to do so and mean- time he has plac.d in my hands a neat little description, written by an eye-witness who'spent a day in the Firm's Fruit Preserving works last Summer. The writer says: ")Literal walls of preserves, encased in shining glass jars of all shapes and sizes, towered above me. and sparkled around me in the brilliant sunshine, awaiting my inspection. "Now this jam," said the Manager of the Department holding up a pot of whole-fruit straw- berry " was actually growing on the fruit.farms early this morning and before tea-time this evening it will likely enough be on somebody's tea-table." The writer then records how he entered" Another large depart- ment" where I saw many wsrk people hard at work ; some were engaged in throwing the fresh fragrant fruits after weighing it, into great copper-pans heated by steam coils ; others were testing the boiling preserves ; while others again were busy weighing-in sugar, and a fourth lot were engaged in emptying out the pulp. It was a pleasing seene,and one not easily to he forgotton." "These pans "said my conductor," cook about 1201 b at a time, three boilings in the hour, the fourteen of them producing about thirty tons of jam per day." " Do you cook all the fruit as fast as it comes in ? " "Yes, that is to say we 'pulp' it—boil it without sugar! Th jam-making is continued throughout the winter. as Mr. Robertson is very considerate for his ' hands 'and secures therm continuous employment."</p>
        <p>The pulped 'fruit, stored for winter use, is stacked In huge casks and remains in perfect condition,retaining its delicacy of flavour and its refreshing fragrance. Per- haps the speciality of the whole place is Cake. Here may be seen men turning out in the most astonishing manner from long narrow boxes eggs to the tune of thou- sande. Each of these is tested and finally beaten up, if perfectly sound. Butter, fine white sugar and flour, the best fruit and well regulated ovens are the order of the day. No care is grudged, the greatest attention being bestowed upon details. Cakes to right of me, cakes te left of me, cakes behind and before me thoroughly ac- counted for the provincial fame of this Chelsea fin. I . observed with satisfacti,n, that not only is the utmost care taken in measuring the ingredients, mixing and baking these delicious cakes, but cleanliness of person and place is insisted upon throughout the factory. I was exceedingly interested in noticing the various processes undergone by the citron, orange, and lemon Peel. These are imported in large casks of brine from Italy, Sicily, Spain, lee. The peel is brought from the store. yards to the boiling pans, and after " freshening " with fresh water, boilcd until tender. A knowledge of the time requisite for this can only be gained by experience. It is then plunged into cold water, and when cool pulped—Le. the inside or pulp is taken out—again it is "freshened" and subsequently thrown into a weak solution of syrup, where it throws out the water and absorbs the sugar. It passes from strength to strength of syrup, remaining in the last, which is quite thick, for a period of twenty-one days. It is then withdrawn, placed on large wires to drain,and subsequently upon other wires in the drying-room. Eventually the completed article is packed in cases and stored away until rrquired for candying purposes. The candying process nececsitates immersion in boiling syrup the peel being forked out on to wires and placed in racks. When quite cool it is packed in boxes as seen in the grocers' shops. These processes call for great detail,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>GEO. G. BU3SEY. &amp; CO., the leading; Sports Manufac- thrers of London, S.E., have published a most com- plete catalogue. It is bound to interest Cricketers, Golfers, Tennisers, Croquet Players, an I thelike. If you cannot ob- tain a copy from the local Dealer. send a post card to G. G. B. Co.,_ as above.</p>
