      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Total IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>Total IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons met again on Monday after the Whitsuntide holidays. Mr. MORLEY asked the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs whether he could lay upon the table of the House any correspondence between the Government and the Governments ef Germany and Italy relating to recent affairs in the Soudan. He also asked if the right hon. gentleman was aware that the Italian Government had laid before the Italian Parlia- ment a Green•book containing despatches and commu- nications bearing upon this very important subject. Mr. CURZON replied that he was aware that the Green-book had been laid before the Italian Parliament,but the des- patches and communications could hardly be described as relating to affairs in the Soudan. In reply to other questions, there had been no correspondence answering te the description with Germany. The correspondence with Italy had referred almost entirely to military °per- ations,and could not therefore be produced at the pre- sent time.</p>
        <p>Mr. HANEURY, in reply to Sir Charles Mike, said a Bill would be at once introduced authorising the necessary payment out of the Consolidated Fund to meet the addi- tional outlay of £500,000 in the present financial year on the Uganda railway. Mr. RITCHIE. replying to Mr. Massey-Mainwaring,said the engineers thought that the accident on the Snowdon tailway was caused by the locomotive mounting the rack,probably in consequence of a subsidence of the per- manent way. As the line was not constructed under any statutory authority,the Railway Acts did not apply. Nevertheless, no passengers would be carried on until precautions had been taken to prevent another accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the Howe of Commons on Tuesday Mr. T. MORLEY asked the Under Foreign Secretary whether he would lay upon the table any despatches from Her Majesty's Government relating to the East African affairs which had recently been laid before the Italian Government. Mr. CURZON said he would be happy to lay any des- patches which answered the description given by Mr. Morley, but he did not think there were more than one or two.</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Labouchere, Mr. Cl:razor; said that snme time before the receipt of the communication from the Italian Ambassador asking if the Government, in view of the presence of dervishes around Kassala, would decide to make a diversion by an advance on the Nile, the question of an advance against the dervishes tad been under discussion with the Egyptian Govern- ment, whe were anxious that such operations should be undertaken in order to ensure the security of Egypt. With respect to the time and circumstances of the ac- tion which the Government sanctioned, they were un- doubtedly influenced by the representations received from the Italian Government of the danger to which the Italian position at Kassala was exposed, the fall of which place at that time would have involved a serious menace to the security of Egyptian territory. A motion made by Mr. P. A. Muyrs, to adjourn over Derby-day was afterwards discussed, and rejected by 199 votes to 58.</p>
        <p>The House then went into Committee on the Diseases of Animals Bill. Mr. Warns proposed an amendment to the first clause to the effect that the bill, if it became an Act, should only remain in force until Her Majesty should, by an Order in Council, otherwise direct. Mr. G. Whiteley (Stockport) supported the amendment, de- claring that to his mind the measure was nothing but thinly veiled protection. The amendment was rejected by 202 votes to 90. Mr. S. Buxton moved an amend- ment the object of which was to exempt from the opera- tion of the bill any colony where it was clear that no disease existed. The amendment was defeated by 140 votes to 57. Air. Bryce proposed that Iceland sheep should be exempted from the operation of the bill, but this was defeated by 105 votes to 42. A number of pro- posed new clauses were ruled out of order, and the bill passed through Committee and was reported to the House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the FinnFe of Commens on - Wednesday. on the motion that the Benefices Bill as amended by the Stand- ing Committee to be considered, Mr. H. Foster moved the rejection of the measure. Mr. Warner seconded the amendment, which was negatived without a division. Mr. Macdona moved a new clause with the object of vesting the right of presentation, in the case of a rural parish in the parish council, and where there was no parish council in the overseers of the parish, and ia the ease of a borough urban district in the borough council or district council in which the parish is situated. The clause was negatived. Mr. Warner then moved that in all cases where a right of presentation lapsed or became vacant under the Act the right of presentation should go to the Crown. This was defeated by ne votes to 49. Mr. Foster, on behalf of Mr. J. Lowther, moved the omission of the first sub-section of the first clause, on the ground that it infringed the rights of private patrons. The amendment was defeated by 202 votes to 22. Mr. Foster proposed the omission of the second sub-section, but the amendment was negatived without either discussion or division. Very little progress ha&amp; been made when the debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>%O,Porfics,</head>
