      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>WARRINGTON FLORAL AND HORTICUL TURAL SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WINDING UP MEETING.</head>
        <p>WINDING UP MEETING.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Committee of this Society was held in the offices Scotland-road when there w(re present Messrs. Frank °Hinshaw (presiding) Joseph Gr6unds, T. Cowdell, S. Go3dier (head gardener Cemetery) George Astell (gardener at Massey nab) D-tvid Guider (gardener at the Elms, Moore), George Wright (gardener at Mr. Fletcher's), James Clarke (gardener at Mr. Navl)r's;' and others. Apologies for absence were received from Alderman Bolton, W. Lockhart, (head gardener to Sir G. Greenall Bart.,) and Mr. P. ulton, gardener at Mr. Long's. The minutes of the previous meeting having been read and approved, the Chairman read a letter from Mr. Ryan tendering his resignation as secretary of the society. This communicatio,n was re- ceived with much regret by all present, as it was felt that the succe,s of the Society in the past was almost entirely due to Mr. Ryans indefatigable labours. Mr. Grounds in a few words d:precated the want of symp oily which had been shown by the general publlc in not supporting an undertaking which was entirely for their benefit,and-which gave them high class exhibitions and entertainments for so little money: _</p>
        <p>After further discussion it was decided on the motion of Mr. Grirnshaw, seconded by Mr. Calder, that Mr. Ryan's resignation be accepted, although, the Chairman remarked it gave him souse anxiety as to the future of the society, seeing so much had depended on the seer( - tary. He felt sure it would be impossible to readily lino another to take up the position. On being pressed fol his reasons for resigning. Mr. Ryan called upon the treasurer to read the statement of accounts to date, which showed that it was imposzible tg carry on the imiety and make arrangements for a summer show. The amount collected bp the Committee was only £8 14s. 6. aid by the secretary £34 13s. 6.1. making a tor4i £43 Bs. It would necessitate a further sum of £4O to £5O to meet the expenditure, and as Ole Committee Dad not evinced sufficient enthusiasm to secure this, lie thought it was now the right time to take a stand and show exactly what was being done and who did it. Tiff • was his reason for giving up the secretaryship and his decision was final. Mr. J. Fulton Smith (treasurer), stated that in view of the remarks which had fallen from Mr. Ryan to the effect that the committee had not succeeded in suriplc- menting his efforts to obtain sefficient subscriptions, there was milv one course left for him (the treasure.) t follow, and that was to repay all subPcriptions received by him up to date fJr this yeai's show. It was their duty as custodians oh public money to refund the same in full when the object for which it was given could not be fulfilled. He accordingly moved that the money be re:lald as suggested. The motion was sPconded by Mr. Ryan, and carried. Mr. Calder asked how the expence of re-payment was to be met, when it was elicited that all accounts owing by the society had already been paid and any further expense in winding up the society would be provided tor. A vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meet- ing to a cloie. 1</p>
        <p>WEEKLY ILEPOI{T QN THE HEA.LTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During last week, which was ths 21st, of the current year, the following births and deaths were re4istered Births. D63.ths. Warrin,cron Latchford 1 2</p>
        <p>Total 3O</p>
        <p>• The excess of births over deaths was 14. The death rate from all causes was equal to 14.3 per I,oo3,a'zailst 12 2 the avera,Ye of the previous four weeks, and against 127 !la corresponding week of last year. The death rat" from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 1.7 per .1,000, against 0.4 the avci-age rate of the four previous weeks, and against Ni the corresrnding week of 1895. The ages of m,rtality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 1 5 4 3 3 16</p>
        <p>The deaths inc:ulll-1 from scarlet fever, 1 fro-n vriire•min,! c ,ugh, 1 from old ag..,1 from pntliisis, 1 fro n cii,thete, 2 from brain disease, 3 'rum heart disease, 1 from lung disease,</p>
        <p>The tollowin2 cases of infectious diseam have been reported dunng the week :—Scarlet fever 2 cases. Total number of patients-in hospital week ending May 23rd-8.</p>
        <p>METROROLOGY. —The mean temperature was 52.3 Fah., and therefore 5.7 degrees lower than the precedint: week. The maximum temperature 73F., was recorded on the 19th inst., and the minimum temperature 31V., on the 21th inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall '33 measured inch. The general directio, waq N.W. the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1410 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health</p>
      </div>
