      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>lettrro to tin 00ttor.</head>
        <p>lettrro to tin 00ttor.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB. Sir,—ln your report of the annual meeting of the Warrington Football Club, it was stated by one of the members, that the prizes for the last Walking Day sports were marked "made in Germany." This statement, as also one by the Chairman, that they were "made in Vienna" is entirely incorrect and misleading. There were in all twenty prizes given. Two only were of foreign manufacture. The remaining 18 were by the best English makers. The twa exceptions were (from the character of the articles themselves) unobtainable in England as their manufacture is exclusively confined to the one country producing them, and do not in any way enter into competition with English manufactures. We feel it is due to the Selection Committee and to ourselves that the same publicity should be given to the actual facts as was accorded to the misleading statements at the meeting. Thanking you in anticipation for your insertion of the above, we are, yours taithfully,</p>
        <p>E. &amp; A. EUSTANCE.</p>
        <p>43, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
