      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>BYE-ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE BYE-ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL VICTORIES AT FROME AND WICK.</p>
        <p>The votes recorded on Tuesday in the election at Frome to fill the vacancy caused by the accession of Lord Weymouth to the peerage were counted on Wed- nesday, with the fcllowing result : J. E. Barlow (L) Lord A. Thynne (C) 4763</p>
        <p>Majority The following are the figures of the two previous contests :- 1892. 1895. J. E. Barlow (L) ......... 4747 Via. Weymouth (C)...5043 Vis. Weymouth (C) ....4260 J. E. Barlow (L) 4660 -- Majority 487 Majority After the declaration of the poll. Lord A.Thynne,who, with his opponent, had been present at the counting, proceeded to the Frome Conservative Club and ad. dressed his supporters. The Liberals had assembled in great force in the market square. manifesting signs of great excitement and enthusiasm, and the successful candidate had the greatest difficulty in getting to the hotel. When at length he made his appearance at the balcony there was a remarkable scene.and it was fully five minutes before the cheering subsided. The market square was literally a sea of heads and faces,and the enthusiasm was unbounded. Mr. Barlow thanked his supporters for their magnificent victory—a victory which would strike a great blow at the majority of the Government. Their meddling and muddling foreign 'Policy, and unjust and iniquitous so-called Education Bili,as well as that measure for the relief of landowners, or,in other words.for the oppression and taxation of the vest masses ef the people—these were the isques on which the election had been fought and won.—Mr. Bar- low afterwards left by coach for Bath, and at Radstock stated that the Liberal whips had telegraphed that the victory would resound throughout the country. In the evening the new member left Bath for London. Mr. John Emmott Barlow, the newly-elected member, is the eldest son of Mr. Thomas Barlow, of Torkington Lodge' near Stockport, and Bryn Elrias, Colwyn Bay, North Wales. He was born in 1857, and was called to the bar at the Inner Temple in 1884, but does not prac- tice. He is well known in the Frome Division, having within the last four years gone through two contested elections there. Last year he had the somewhat unique experience of fighting the division during his honeymoon as the election had begun at the time of his marriage in July, with a sister of the present Lord Denman. He is a member of the well-known firm of Oriental merchants, Messrs. Barlow and Co., of Manchester, London ,Calcutta, 'Shanghai and Singapore. He is a magistrate for both Cheshire and Somerset, having qualified in the latter county only a few weeks ago. Mr. Barlow unsuccess- fully contested the Knutsford Division m 1885, and the 'Denbigh Burghs in 1886, but he won the Frome Division for the Liberals in 1892. The votes recorded on Tuesday for the election of a member for Wick Burghs in the place of Sir J. Fender (U), retired, were countel on Wednesday. The candi- dates were Mr. W. C. Smith (U) an 3 Mr. T. C. H. lied- derwiek (L). The result was declared as follows : Hedderwick (L) .................................... 1054 Smith (111) .......................................... 842</p>
        <p>Maj0rity.............................. 212 The mutts of the two previous elections were :- 1892 1895. Sir 3' Pender (11) 952 Sir J render (U) 1 X Cameron (L) ...... ... 82b T C Hedderwick (L)</p>
        <p>_ 24 Majority</p>
        <p>LADIES' CYCLING COSTUME'S, Gaiters. Divided Skirts, !cc Special Department now open. WILLIAM HODGKIN - soli, 46 and 48, Bridge-st., Warrington,- Ifiv</p>
        <p>FEARFUL ACCIDENT TO A SAILOR AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday aftnnoon a serious accident happened on the Ship Canal near Runcorn resulting in frightful injuries to a seaman named Ernest Pritchard,a native of Parr,and one of the crew of the schooner Lady Jane, of Preston. The vessel is moored on the canal and Prit- chard was engaged scraping the masts with a knife when he fell to the deck a distance of 29 feet. So heavy was the impact that the knife which the unfortunate man held in his hand tore up the deck striking down into the woodwork a depth of several inches. Mr.lose, the dock master, attended to the man and owing to fus ambulance experience he was able to render valuable service until Dr. McKenzie,who was summonetharrived. On examination the doctor found that one leg and one arm were fractured,together with other serious injuries. Alter attending to him the doctor ordered the man's re- moval to the Liverpool hospital where he now lies in a serious condition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>RUNCORN RIFLE CLUB GOLD MEDAL COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RIFLE CLUB GOLD MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The third meeting of the above for the current year took place on Saturday at Runcorn Heath. Under Bic ley rules seven rounds were fired at 200, 500, and 600 yards. Nineteen members put in appearance, the details of the scoring being as follows :- 200 500 6)0 To-</p>
        <p>Rank and Names yds. yds. yds. tal. Sergeant W H Harvey Private S D Mat thews Sergeant J A Rushton 3O ... 27 ... 27 .. 84 Private J Houghton 3l ... 23 ... 27 ... 81 Private C Bradbury 33 ... 24 ... 24 ... 78 Sergeant D Edwards Private J Mort Private James Sadler Sergeant W Cooper Private C Garratt Band-Sergeant J Gittins 2B ... 20 ... 21 ... 69 Privatf, J Edwards Private S Joinsou 26 ... 12 ... 25 ... 63 Private J Pie Couteur Private W Gregory Private T Chamberlain Sergeant J W Woodland 25 ... 18 ... 13 ... 56 Private W H Warburton 2B ... 11 ... 13 ... 52 Private S Stubbs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RUNCORN GHOST STORY.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN GHOST STORY.</p>
        <p>During the week a curious rumour has gone the rounds of Runcorn, and as its subject consists of all the original features (in these days)of a ghost story it is fast gaining ground and credence in the town. During the last three weeks a family new to Runcorn has been oc- cupying a :mall house in a side street off one of the chief thoroughfares in the town and for a few days their resi- dence there went on uninterrupted. Later a son was left asleep on the sofa and in the middle of the night he ran screaming up to his mother's room complaining that the pillow had been taken from under his had. From th.t time onward weird and uncanny noises have been heard at night time in the house, generally taking the form of shuffling footsteps on the stairs together with the rustling of garments. Nor have these mysteri- ous manifestations been !the only incidents, as the lady of the house has twice seen the phantom figure of a mysterous old woman who entering through doors in different rooms has been closely followed and found to have disappeared in a very unsatisfactory manner. , Ihe elderly " spirit's " last appearance occurred last Saturday, when she was seen by the mother and one of the son to glide across the kitchen., This seems to have been too much for the whole family as I.lley have moved their quarters a few streets away from the house favoured by the alleged nocturnal visitor. The affair has caused no little excitement and the street has been visited by many people,while the house is becoming an interest- ing Ibuilding in the eyes of the superstitiously inclined neighbours. In a matter of fact age like the present, this paragraph will no doubt cause many of our readers to furtively smile on reading it,but our representative on interviewing the late occupants was bound to admit that they at least were unpleasantly impressed with their recent experiences. Moreover the ghost's appearance during its nightly walk has been vividly described by Mrs. , who goes into minute particulars in speaking of the dress, gesture and deportment. of the Runcorn perturbed spirit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Runcorn 'lowa Hall on Thursday morning, present: Mr. J. W. Lightburn in the chair Rev. D. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor J.P. C.C., H. J. Grice, E. Imison, J. H. Kirkham, J. Pritchard, G. Hewitt, M. Sweetman, F. Speakman J.P., C.C., J. Wilson, T. Dan, D. Bisbrowne, Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall secretary, and Mr. J. Wilding (surveynr). MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT._ -</p>
        <p>_ This report showed that the number of eleaths regis- tered during the past month amounted to 24. Of these 6 were inquest cases, and of the remaining 18, 2 each were due to congested tabes, semle decay, convulsions and heart affection, one each due to pharyngeal ulcera- tion, scarlatina peritoniti adenitis, (probably tuber- cular),croup, atheroma, congenital debility, tubercular meningitis, pneumonia and phthisis. Tie death rate for the 12 months was 15-56, and exclusive of inquests 11-67. The number of births in the month was 37. •</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE. This committee recommended that a resolution passed at a meeting held on the sth March last, relating to the hire of stables at the Boat House Inn from Messrs. Greenall. Whitley and Co., he rescinded, and that the following resolution be adopted :—That the stables and yard at the" Boat Rouse" Inn he hired from the occu- pier of such inn at a rental of £lO 10s. per annum, the tenancy to be a yearly one, with the right of the Urban District Council of Runcorn to erect sheds, such sheds to be the property of the Council ,and to be removed at any time at their discretion. The Sanitary Inspector having reported that a nuisance exists at premises in Granville-street and Fryer-street, it was recom- mended that notices be served on the owner or owners to abate the nuisance within 28 days of the service of the notices, otherwise that legal proceedings be taken. The. Committee also gave notice that at the meeting of the Council to be held on the 2nd day of July next, the following resolu- tion would he proposed :—That Mr. James Percival be appointed Inspector of Nuisances at a salary at the rate of £125 a year, sullect to the appointment being deter- mined on one month's notice being given with the sanc- tion of the Local Government Board, as required by their Order of the Bth March, 1880; that application he made to the Local Government Board for sanction to such appointment, and that application be made to the Cheshire County Council for the payment of half of such salary. Mr. Dean moved the confirmation of the minutes, which was seconded by Mr. Hewitt and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes contained a resolution recommending that Hugh Ackerley be appointed caretaker of the Tech- nical Institute at a weekly wage of 153., were approved on the motion of Mr. Wilson,seconded by Mr.Bisbrowne.</p>
        <p>THE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes were that 228 volumes of new books, as per list submitted, be purchased, and that 300 cQpies of the new hand-list of books added to the library be printed. Mr. Grive moved the confirmation and was seconded by Mr. Taylor.—Mr. Bisbrowne expressed a hopeithat the work would be done in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that the law clerk's office be connected with the Runcorn Telephone and the Town Hall, at an aunual rental of £3. The committee reported that the following letter has been received from Her Majesty's Treasury :—" The Lords Commissioners of Her Majesty's Treasury have had before them your letter of the 12th ultimo, in which the Runcorn Urban District Council ask their lordships to sanction an exten- sion of the time for repayment of the balance of loan owing by the Council as representing the late Burial Board. In reply, my Llrds desire me to inform you that the repayment of these loans is regulated by Statute,and that they have no power to authorise the adoption by the Urban District Council of arrangements which would have the effect of extending the repayment of the origi- nal loans over a period inconsistent with the provisions of the Burial Acts. Mr. Unison moved the confirmation of the minutes. With reference to the recemmendation that the law clerk's office be connected with the Town Hall he ex- plained that it might appear that they were spending £l3 on telephon. communication, but in consequence of allowing the Company to erect poles for . a nominal charge, application was made to see if a special charge could not be arranged, and this application had been successful, and the minute would only entail a cost lof £1 for what was approved a month ago. Instead of paying £l3 the cost would only be £ll for the two.— Dr. Gastaldi seconded the motion which was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW ROAD TO WESTON POINT. Mr. ilisbrown said the Council would remember that the Surveyor was instructed to preitare plans with re- gard to.the alternative routes of the proposed roads to Weston Point. He wished to know whether they had been prepared and whether a reply had been received from Major Orred.— Mr. Speakman said the subject of the plans came before the Trades Council at their meet- ing on Monday, and up to that time no reply had been received. Mr. Wilding had been instructed to see to the preparation of the plans and other matters con- nected with the roads, and they were hoping the whole subject would soon be brought More the Trades Council. This concluded the business of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>FURTHER OUTBREAK OF TYPTICTS FEVER.—Another case of typhus fever was reported to have broken out in Sea-lane, Halton. Every effort has been made to pre- vent the further spread of the disease. This makes the fifth case within the last few weeks, three of which have ended fatally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 6, 1596.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 6, 1596.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Monday at the Earlestown Town Hall where there were present Mr. C. B. F. Borron (chairman), and Messrs Col- lingwood, Emmett, MacMahon, Gilmour, Wood, Stone, Simpson, Thompson, Beatty, Evans, Ormand, the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, Brierley (surveyer), Worsley (law clerk), and Shirley (clerk).</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. Mr. Wood moved the adoption of these minutes, and Mr. Gilmour seconded.—Carried.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>In this committee's minutes was the anpointment of Messrs. Borron, Wood, Thompson, and Kaye, as repre- sentatives ( f the committee on the Joint Technical In- struction Committee of the Viaduct Institute for the en- suing year.—The minutes were coklirmed on the motion of the Rev. E. W. W. Kaye, seconded by Mt. Beatty.:</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, &amp;C., COMMITTEE.-A QUESTION FROM - Dirt.</p>
        <p>MAMA TION.</p>
        <p>In the minutes of this Committee it was stated that several nuisances were reported and the committee re- solved that notices should be served for abatement.— Mr. MacMahon said with regard to these alleged nuisances one had reference to the want of a proper place for the disposal of stable manure and other refuse on premises in Bridge-st. The cccupier had been noti- fied to abate the nuisance and the owner's name was not mentioned. That was irregular. Further, both the nuisance inspector and his deputy had passed over that same nuisance until now, and it had existed for five years 50 per cent worse than it was at the present time. Mr. Brierley had been with him in the self- same yard where the nuisance was and never noticed it. Ho wanted an explanation why it was reported on the last day of March. It was better now that it was reported as a nuisance, than it was five years ago.—Mr. Ormand : I gather that a proper place has been provided for the disposal of the manure.—Mr. MacMahon said the place had been in existence for five years but the tenant had not kept it in the condition that it was now kept. He moved that Mr. Gregory, the deputy inspector be asked the question why it was reported now instead of 5 years ago?—Mr.Collingwood : It is a question entirely for the coriamittee.—Mr. Mac- Mahon : , I gave notice of it at the last meeting of the committee.—Mr. Coiling wood : You did not.—Mr. MacMahon : I ask the Clerk whether I did or not.—The Clerk : You asked the question how it was that the owner of the property was not named on the list instead of the occupier.—The Chairman : I think the Clerk's explanation is correct.—Mr. Gilmour said that from what Mr. MacMahon told the committee he understood that the same kind of nuisance that Mr. MacMahon was summoned for had been pointed out to the Inspector the same day. He wanted the public to know about it. It had been brought forward for election purposes.—The Chairman: I cannot hear you further. You must n)t bring in personalities.—Mr. Brierley said that in the case under discussion the tenant was called upon to abate the nuisance.—Mr. Gilmour: Did you notify it</p>
        <p>the same day you saw Mr. MacMahon's nuisance?— There was no answer.—Mr. Emmett asked the Law Clerk whether it was the duty of the Nuisance</p>
        <p>- Inspector to summon the occupier or owner.—The Law Clerk said it depended very much upon what the offence was. The offence in question was clearly an occupier's nuisance.—Mr. Collingwood moved the adoption of the minutes, together with those if the meeting of the com- mittee held that day.—Mr. Simpson seconded,and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday in reply to Sir W. Priestley, Mr. CHAPLIN said the adoption generally of the practice !of calf-to-arm vaccination would involve an entirely new organisation and a very large expenditure of public money. He doubted the expediency of making any change in the law pending the receipt of the report of the Royal Commission. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, in reply to Mr. WKENNA, said that from official ieformation in his possession he had no ' doubt that Mr. Rhodes was not assuming control over civil and military affairs in Matabeleland. Sir M. WHITE RIDLEY informed Dr. Clark that Mrs. May brick was detained on conviction for murder, for which she was sentenced to death. He had most care- fully considered the case, and could see no reason, any more that could his predecessors, for advising any fur- ther exercise of the clemency of the Crown. Mr. J. E. Barlow, the new member for the Frome division, took his seat, and was healthy cheered by the Opposition. The Light Railways Bill as amended was afterwards considered, and a number of other amendments were disposed of before the debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SUICIDE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE AT EA.RLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>A SINGULAR LETTER.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening Moses Tees, living at 17, Bradlegh Road, Newton, when walking along the canal towing path in company with Charles Allandale, found a man's jacket and cap lying on the canal bank near Bridge's Farm. He saw nobody about, and examining the pockets found a pocket-book. He conveyed the articles to the Earlestown police Station, and told the police where he had found them. P.C. Bullen accom• pulled Tees to the canal, and, with the assistance of the lock-keeper,tbey succeeded in recovering from the water the dead body of a man, which turned out to be that of Owen Lewis, of 163 Gas-street, Newton. It appears from enquiries made that Lewis,who was a Bottle-maker and a collector for tbe Bottle-maker's Society, was in some monetary difficulties, and as the monthly reckon- ing day drew nigh he became depressed,owing to his in- ability to make up the deficiency,and it is supposed that this has so preyed upon his mind that he was ied to commit the rash act.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held at the Old Crow Inn, Newton, on Monday by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner. Mr. W. Mills was foreman of the jury. Robert Lewis said he was a labourer, and resided with his father at 63, Gas-street, Newton. The deceased was his brother, and was 26 years of age. Ile was a bottle maker and a collector for the Bottle Maker's Society. He last saw his brother alive about 8.15 a.m. on Saturday. Deceased asked him for 103. and witness told him he had not got it. He also said to him " How is it you are wearing my jacket ?" Deceased said "1 will pull it off," but witness said "Never mind it to-day." The jacket produced was the one his brother was wearing on Satur- ciav, and the pocket book was his, as also was the hand- writing. The deceased had been drinking rather freely of late, and had threatened to take his life two or three times, and had been very strange. He had nbt been well for the past month, and last week he was very weak.</p>
        <p>John Broster,ls years of age, residing at 41, Laurence- street, Earlestown, said he was well acquainted with the deceased, and he used to work with him at the bottle house. Aboyt 5 o'clock on Saturday he was lying on the grass by the side of the canal, near the nine arches when the deceased passed him walking along the canal bank, about half a mile from where he was afterwards found. He did not notice him after he had gone past a little way. He had a coat on,and his vest and shirt front were open. He was walking slowly and looked strange,as if he had been drinking. WitneQs was present when he was taken out of the canal, and he was then without a coat. This would be at 6.45.</p>
        <p>Moses Tees said he was a grinder, and resided at 17, Bradlegh-road, Newton. About 5.30 on Saturday, he was walking along the canal bank with Charles Allan- dale, when he found the coat and cap produced on the canal bank. He saw no one about, and felt in the poc- kets and found the pocket book produced. He took them to the Eariestown police station, and went with the police to the spot, and was present when the de- ceased was taken out of the canal close to where the coat and cap were fcund. P.C. Builen said he received the jacket, cap, and pocket book produced from the last witness. He went with the witness to the canal, and with the assistance of the lock-keeper, recovered the body and had it con- veyed h4me. He stripped, and examined it but there were no marks of violence upon it. He found inside deceased's shirt, next his body, £3 03. 11d., tied up in a handkerchief.</p>
        <p>The Coroner then read the following letter, which w found in deceased's pocket book. "Dear father and brothers, very sorry, I am, as lam going to take the action of taking my life, but the temptation is so strong upon me that I cannot resist it. I would not face my fellow-workmen, being deficient, as little as it is. AU I can advise my brothers to do is to take a different kind of life to what I have been doing, and to keep away from drink, which has been my ruin. lam now going to meet my God, who I doubt, or He would not let men suffer on this earth as He does." A verdict of " Suicide whilst temporarily insane was returned."</p>
        <p>VIEMNO BODIES.</p>
        <p>The foreman of the jury (Mr. Mills) referred to the question of viewing dead bodies in similar cases to the one under notice. He said if the Coroner could exert himself to do away with the viewing of bodies of an un- pleasant character,it would be a great boon to the jury- men. Several of the jury concurred.—Mr. Carlin said he did not think the ends of justice would be met if they did not see the body.—The Coroner said his views on the viewing of bodies had been so often published that he thought they were getting stale. He thought the viewing of bodies except in exceptional cases-, did no practical good. He thought when a case was reported to the Coroner he ought to have a discretion given him to order a viewing of the body if he thought it was necessary. If it was a case of murder, or the body had wounds upon ir,he thought it was necessary to view the body, so as to follow the medical evidence,and the char- acter of the wounds would help the jury. If a child fell into a pan of water and got scalded,he did not see what good the viewing of the body wculd do, but it was only by legislation that the custom could be altered. They had his full sympathy in the matter, and if there was anything he could do to alter it he would do it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND CORPORATION AT CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Mayor ol Warrington, issued last week, the members of the Town Council and Cor- poration officials attended divine service at Holy Trinity Church on Sunday morning. An assembly was made in the Council Chamber at ten o'clock, and from there the procession headed by the Borough Brass Band walked to the Church in Sankey-st. Behind the band es as the police force under the supervision of the Chief Constable (Mr. L. Talbot) and the firemen unaer the direction of Superinteadent Turner. Both bodies were in full uniform, and presented a smart and effisient appearance which was much commented upon. The members of the Council came next as fellows : The Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough), Aldermen Francomb, and Wright; and Councillors H. Roberts, Robert Heaton, Joseph Shaw Green, James Hepherd, James Smethurst, W. James Shaw, A. Bennett, J. Shill. cock, John Baird, John Evans, Pierpoint, John Lee, William Rogers, and W. Furness; the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), Mr. Stanley Wilson (assistant town clerk), the Borough Treasurer (Mr. John Fair- hurst), the Borough Surveyor (Mr. Longdin), Mr. John Sutcliffe (assistant surveyor). Mr. George F. F. White (deputy magistrates' clerk), Mr. James Deas (manager of the depots, &amp;;.), Mr. Robert Young (cemetery registrar), Mr. W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), Mr. Fred Taylor (secretary), Mr. W. S. Howard (cashier), Mr. Charles Madeley (librarian, curator, &amp;n.) ; and Messrs. J. W. Hay ward, J.P., S. Wright, J.P., William Hickman, Alfred Jolley, Samuel Hunt, James Bolton (one of the borough auditors). Harry Toft, Charles Worthington, and W. W. Westwell. When all were seated in the church, there was a large congregation, prayers were read by the Rev. L. B. Rad- ford, curate, and the lessons by Mr. S. J. Westbrook. The communion service was taken by the Vicar and Mr. Raiford. The sermon was preached by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton (Mayor's chaplain), who took for his text the words "The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom and the knowledge of the holy is understanding" (Pro- verbs ix. 10). He said he had to ask their aid that morning towards the maintenance of their day and Sun- day schools. It was a cause that should, he thought, ensure the sympathy of all enlightened people, the train- ing of the young, who were to be the future men and women of this land, and on whom would depend the well being of the country in the time to come After referring at some length to the education and training of the y-ung, the preacher said he had endea- voured to put the case for religious teaching in their schools on its merits apart from any considesrtions of a political character: and in view of the additional re- spensibilities which contemplated legislation proposed to impose upon Town Councils, he thought it could only help towards a fair treatment of the subject if on an oc- casion like the present the thoughts that were felt strongly by some should find respenQe.They felt strongly upon that subject,because they regarded religious teach- ing not only as a Christian but also as a parental obliga- tion. Commercial success was all very well, and s, cial success was all very well, but man's happiness depended far less on these things than they generally supposed. He wanted above all things peace of mind, the rest of spirit that was resigned and contented, a clear con- science, the assurance of forgiveness, and the joy which he found in a future bright with hope. At the conclusicn of the service the procession was reformed, the members of the Council, officials, and others returning to the Town Hall, where they se?a- rated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Th. Gas Manager reported that during the past month the illuminating power of the gas had been kept up to an average of 20 candles. The consumption of water at the Viadue, Newton Bridge, and Parkside meters had been 4.944,000 gall- ns, a decrease of 200,000 gallons on the same month of last year.—Mr. Gilmour, referring to a resolution that all existing sub-committees, (gas and water), be re-appointed, said he could not act on the subcommittee for fire appliances any longer unless a meeting was called. There had only been one meeting altogether and he did not like being a dummy committee man.—The Chairman intimated that he would call a meeting, and the minutes of the committee were then adopted on the motion of himself seconded by Mr. Beattie.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMIITTEE. This committee's minutes were confirmed on Mr. Or- mand's motion, seconded by Mr. MacMahon.</p>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. 1 The seal of the Council was affixed to the following agreements :—An agreement between Lord Newton and the Council for the deposit of nightsoil and other refusel on land belonging, to Lord Newton; an agree- ment between the Council and Mr. William Gilbert, farmer,Newton-le-Willows,for the deposit and treatment of nightsoil and other refuse on land in his occupation ; an agreement between the Council and Mr. Thomas Wil- liams, farmer, Newton-le-Willows, for the deposit and treatment of nightsoil and other refuse on land in his occupation : an agreement between the Lancashire County Council and the Council, for the repair and maintenance of main roads within the district for one year ending March 25th, 1897. An application by Mr. J. Bridge for the supply of water was passed ever to the committee. The Chairman said he had a paper before him signed by a number of ratepayers, calling attention to the pit at the top of Market-st. and Crow-lane,and asking for it to he filled up,as it was a source of anxiety and danger. —Mr. Gilmour said the matter had been up before, and a resolution passed that the owner of the property be com- municated with.—Mr.Brierley said the agent of the pro- perty had been notified to fill the pit up. Nothing was done, and the Law Clerk wrote to him,and sines then he had done some four or five yards' filling up. He may have thought it sufficient.—Mr. MacMahon said the agent had filled up the part of the pit which was not dangerous.—The minutes were then confirmed on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Simpson. This concluded the busineaa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NOTES BY THE \VAT AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE \VAT AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>At the monthly of the District Council this week, there was nothing of any moment trans- pired, and it is evident the members are rapidly becoming acclimatised and settling down to a bust- Less-like condition. Mr. Smith,l regret to observe, is still an enforced absentee, but I learn that he is gradually recovering from the serious illness with which he has been afflicted, and I hope to see him in his accustomed place shortly.</p>
        <p>One important question which absorbed the attention of the Council for a short space, was a petition from a number of householders on Newton Common respecting an alleged dangerous pit at the top of Market-street. In the petition the signa- tures asked for the pit to be filled up or fenced in, alleging that its presence is a standing danger to children. In this I can fully bear out the state- ments of the petitioners, for it is not very long since I saw three or four youngsters playing on the banks of the pit, and one of them fell in. Fortun- ately at that point it was not very deep, and with the assistance of his companions he managed to reach terra firma, escaping with a drenching. This, I believe, is only one case out of many, and the anxieties of many parents would be relieved if the useless pit was filled up.</p>
        <p>The burglar scare which has alarmed the in- habitants of Earlestown has, I am glad to find,been somewhat allayed, and people are beginning to feel a trifle more secure. Many rumours floated about during the past week, :but when properly sifted, they turned out to be purely mythical.</p>
        <p>I am glad to notice that a writer in a local print has taken up my remarks anent the formation of a cycle club, and asks whether I have been asleep or wilfully ignored certain facts. The same facts be- ing that there is already a social cycling club in the town whose headquarters are at the New Market Inn. I still adhere tolwhat I previously stated, and emphatically repeat that there is not, a cycling club in the town of the kind to which I allude. lam not going to impeach the respectability of the so- called " social " cycling club, but I will state with - out fear of contradiction that the existing " is not conducted on club lines. How many pro- perly organized fixed runs has the club held, and how many members reached their destination at every run. My idea is to have formed, a club with properly appointed officers, and with a properly drawn card of fixtures, and rules which the men will adhere to, the said club to be open for the ad- mission of both sexes. If the writer will say:that such a club is in existence, then I throw up the sponge.</p>
        <p>The same writer also touches upon my suggestion for a cycle lantern parade, but his only remark upon this is " Rats, and plenty of 'em." What he means by such an expression I am at a loss to know and I should like a little enlightenment. Perhaps lie does not understand what a lantern parade is, but if he will take a trip to Liverpool this month I think he will find the hundreds of cyclists who take part do not take such a narrow view rof it as "Rats" appears to do. We have a Medical Aid Society in the town, admittedly doing incalculable good to sufferers, and my idea is that such a society deserves the help and sympathy of everybody in the town. It requires funds to carry on its good work, and a cycle parade would do much to assist it.</p>
        <p>" CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD MIXTURE "is a guaran teed cure for. all Blood and Skin Diseases. It is the most searching blood cleanser ever discovered, and it will free the system from all impurities from whatever cause arising For Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs, Pimples and Sores of all kinds, its effects are marvellous. Thousands of Testi-</p>
        <p>monials. gold everywhere, at 2s. 9d. per bottle. Beware ol worthless imitations and substitutes.—Advt. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL ATHLETE,</p>
        <p>At the annual athletic sports of the Bolton Harriers at Bolton on Saturday afternoon, John Rylance, of Newton, a member of the Salford Harriers won the half mile flat race handicap by 8 yards, with 58 yards start. HAYDOCK PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>In aid of the building debt and repair fund, special services were commenced in the above church on Wednesday evening, and continued on Thursday evening. They were conducted by the Rev. J. L. Walton, of St. Luke's Southport, who dis- coursed on each occasion in an eloquent manner to fairly large congregations. EARLESTOWN DISTRICT DAY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The annual special services in aid of these schools were held in St. John's Church, Earlestown, on Sunday. The sermon at the morning service was preached by the Rev. W. Jones, curate of Golborne, and in the evening by the Rev. F. Ireland, vicar of Haydock. At the evening service the choir sang very efficiently the anthem " Oh ! taste and see." The collections amounted to £4 10s. 4d. which was £1 in excess of last year. FALL OF A CHILD IN A LIME PIT.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening last week, a little boy 2.3- years old, named Stevenson, living with his parents at Newton Common, whilst playing near a lime pit threw a stick amongst the slaked lime, and in endeavouring to recover it, climbed the planking which was round and fell head first in. Luckily a young man named Pickup saw the child fall in, and with commendable promptitude rescued it from its dangerous position. It was taken to a doctor, who after examining it, found it to be badly burnt about the face, with injury to the eves. GOOD 'rEMPLARY (EBENEZER. LODGE). The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session on Tuesday evening in the Co-operative Hail. Bro. T. Dean. C.T., presided. One candidate was admitted to membership. Bro. J. Taylor,secretary, gave report of District Lodge Session held at Orms- kirk on Saturday. The business committee reported on mission work, and a committee was appointed to make arrangements for a waggonette drive to Frodsham. The Secretary was instructed to invite Leigh Alpha Lodge to pay a visit during the gum ter. Refreshments were then handed round, the single -members being responsible. Thanks were tendered to them for their services.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH DAY AND SUNDAY SCHOOLS,</p>
        <p>NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The annual treat to the scholars attending the above schools took place on Thursday afternoon. The day was not propitious, as during the after- noon the pleasure of the children was somewhat marred by a heavy downpour of rain. The scholars mustered at the schools at two o'clock and after- wards proceeded to the church where a short ser- vice was held. They then returned to the schools, and sat down to a substantial tea, the remainder of the day being spent in a &amp;Ad near the school where various outdoor games were indulged in. _ SONS OF TEMPERANCE. _</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting Of the Samuel Morley Secton of Cadets of Temperance was held on Wednesday evening in the Co-operative Hall. Bro. J.Judson Vir.P.presided and after opening, three members were initiated. The Worthy Patron re- ported thin the annual cadets' picnic will take place on Saturday June 20th, at Micklehead Green. At 8 o'clock the Britains Need Divi- sion met in the same room. Bro. S. Eaton W. A. presided; one member was ad- mitted from the Cadets. Bros. S. Foden and 0. Pickett were appointed to attend a united meeting of the various Temperance Organisations to take in consideration the desirability of inviting Mr. Tenny- s3n Smith to hold a mission in Earlestown during the coming winter.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp; The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke pre- sided, and .there was a good attendance of mem- bers, the visitors including Bro. J. Taylor, C.C.T., and Bro. Sergeant Roberts, P.C.C.T., of Warrington. An invitation was read from Bolton Claremont lodge to visit them on July 9th. It was decided not to make a lodge visit, but any members desir- ous of so doing, could go. A letter was read from the Rechabites lodge re a projected Mission to be conducted by Bro. E. Tennyson Smith. Bros. G. E. Warburton and J. Hatton, were appointed to represent the lodge. It was decided to hold the Lodge pic-nic on July 28. at Frodsham, and Bros. John Warburton and S. Parker were appointed to make arrangements. It was arranged to receive a visit from the Ashton lodge next summer, when the meeting will be an open one. The programme of the evening was "How we spent the Whitsuntide," many of the members taking part. Bros. J. Taylor and Sergeant Roberts gave short addresses. Bro. G. Clarke gave a report of the District Lodge Session. The Session was a very pleasant one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>INITIATION OF MIT. A. H. CROSFIELD</head>
        <p>INITIATION OF MIT. A. H. CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>AS AN ODDFELLOW.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF ODDFELLOWS, M.U.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Admiral Nelson Lodge was held on Monday evening, at the offices, Academy-st., Warrington, for the purpose of initating Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield. of Walton Lei. There were present—Bro. T. Barnwell, N.G., in the chair; Bro. Walbrook, as V.G. in the vice-chair ; who was supported by Bro. S. Turner, of Bury. the Grand Master of the , Order; Bro. F. G. Whittle, Grand Master of the district; Bro. S. Ryan, Deputy Grand Master of the district; P.P.G.M. Wads- worth, treasurer; P.P.G.M. John Emson, correspondinfr secretary; P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle, P.P.G.M. Thomas Rigby, P.P.G.M. Norbury, and a large assembly of the members of the district. After the loyal toasts had been honoured, Bro. Boyle was called upon to propose "The Unity." In departing from the programme, the N.G. then asked the Grand Master to present to P.P.G.M. Alban Boyle a past officer's gold jewel.—The Grand Master, in asking Bro. Boyle to accept the jewel, said he , knew that anyone who followed the position of an officer must dc so at the Facrifice of a large amount of time. He complimented Bro. Boyle upon having merited the jewel, as a reward for valuable services rendered to his district.—Bro. Boyle briefly replied. Mr. CROSFIELD, on his entrance to the lodge room, was introduced to the Grand Master, who officiated as lecture master, and delivered the charge in a very im- pressive manner, and at the conclusion of the ceremony Mr. Crosfield was given a hearty Odd fellows' welcome The GRAND MASTER then replied to "The Unity." He was delighted to he with them ,and to be associated with the society which knew no class. They were all on one footing in this great Society of 0 idfellows, which was founded some 86 years ago in Manchester, and had grown so rapidly. Connected with it were all ranks of society, and nothing gave him greater pleasure than to have been the instrument by which a gentleman of a name so well known to all Lancashire people had been admitted to their midst. (Applause-) The Grand Master spoke at length on the precepts of the order, He thought old-age pensions should be a nationallquestien—(hear, hear)—that they as a combination of men in one com- mon bond of union should do all they cnuld for them- selves, and then there would he less for other people to do for them. (Hear, hear.) He concluded by referrinr, to the financial position of the order,the total receipts of which were last year £1,600,000, the disbursements £1,200,000 in sick and funeral benefits, and the total capital was little short of £9,000,000. (Applause.) He hoped this lodge would go on prospering, ever remem- bering those words of the great commander, " England expects every man to do his duty." (Applause.) Bro. JOHN ENI9ON next proposed the health of Bro. Crosfield, and extended to him a very hearty welcome. He thought the more the leading men of their towns could be induced to associate with them and other friently societies, and the more interest they could be in- duced to take in the affairs of these great societies, the better it would be for all classes of the community:.</p>
        <p>Bro. CROSFIELD, in responding to his health, said he took an interest in these great institutions. He had listened very intently to the charge given by the Grand Master, as well as to the speech he had delivered, and admitted that such utterances were enongh to stir the hearts of even the sternest amongst the community. He thought there was nothing in the whole country that was more excellent than these great organizations. Be appreciated the value of the society, and hoped they would at least be able to solve the question of old age pensions. The moral truths which these societies called upon their members to acknowledge did a great deal to make good citizens. In conclusion, he thanked Bro. J. Emson for his invitation to become a working member, to which he replied that he would try, but he already has enough to do. Be hoped at least it would not be the last meeting he would be present at. (Applause.) P.P.G.M. THOMAS RIGBY proposed "The district," to which Prov. G.M. F. J. WHITTLE responded. Songs were well rendered by P.G. John Sherlock, P.G.F. Emson, and Brother Bate. The usual votes of thanks brought a pleasing evening to a close.</p>
        <p>The Health and Vigour of an individual depends upon the quantityancl quality of the Blood. When the tisue have been at work there are thrown into the blood wastesre ducts, and if these be not eliminated but (through any cape) letained in the blood they influence nutrition and funcsion and finally produce organic disease." " The Humanitarian." In cases of Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs, Skin and Blood Diseases, Pimples and Sores of all kinds, the effects of Clarke's Blood Mixture are marvellous. Thousands of won nerful cures have been effected by it. Clarke's Bleed Mixture sold everywhere, at 2s. 9d. per bottle.—Beware of worthless mitations and substitutes.—Advt. (5)</p>
        <p>MEETING OF LOCAL FARMERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>SALE OF LIVE STOCK.</head>
        <p>THE SALE OF LIVE STOCK.</p>
        <p>At the Norton Arms Hotel, Warrington, on Monday evening, a meeting of farmers and others interested in live stock was held under the auspices of the newly. formed Warrington and District Farmers Auction Com- pany, for the purpose of considering the best means of carrying on the practical working of the company. This company it may be stated is to have a capital of £l,OOO, and has been formed for the purpose of acquiring as a going concern the auctieneering business of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Farmers' Co-operative Association, Ltd., carried on at Warrington, and to carry on the trade of auctioneers, valuers, and salesmen in all its hranches. Amongst those present were Messrs. G. Mather, James Ashley, W. Royston, IV. Houghton, J.P., Samuel Houghton, A. Barber, J. Gould, Joseph Knowles, Philip Lowcock, Thomas Percival, John Sampson, J. Weir, R. Hoicroft, T. Swain, Edward Gibbons, James Brookes, Joseph Shakeshaft, R. Warburtm, John War- burton, C. Roylance, J. Whitlow, W. Carr, and A. J. Willett (solicitor to the company). In opening the meeting, Mr. Barber said he would like to see Lancashire and Cheshire farmers knit closer to- gether for their own interests than they had been before, especially as they were to inaugurate a farmers' auction for the sale of their own fat stock. He would like to see some Lancashire men on the directorate of the com- pany. (Hear, hear.) The company was one which none of them could take exception to, and there could be no selfish motive from any point of view. He would 'be glad if someone would move the appointment of a chairman.—Mr. Gibbon proposed, and Mr. Knowles seconded, that Mr. Mather occupy the chair. This was unanimously agreed to.—The Secretary gav9 a resume of what had already been done towards the formation of the company. He stated that over £3OO had already been subscribed for. The Lancashire and Cheshire Farmers' Co-operative Association Limited had agreed to sell their hurdles, &amp;c., to the new company for £3O, and Mr. Sampson of the Norton Arms, would let his yard for the sales of the company at a very reasonable figure.—ln answer to a question as to what was to be the exact amount to be paid for rent, the Chairman said Mr. Sampson would allow the company the yard for £25 per annum, and would give £lO back for the priz?s for Christmas fat stock. This was considered satisfactory. The CHAIRMAN said he believed it would be to the in- terest of all farmers both in Lancashire and Cheshire if they banded together in a company of that sort. If they conducted the business of the company in a straightforward manner he did not see why they could not make it pay, and be of great help to them. (Hear, hear.) _ _</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read the articles of Association which showed that eleven directors must be app•inted.—Mr. John WARBURTON thought the Company would work better if the butchers were united with the farmemThey should have some butchers on the directorate. (Hear, hear.)—The Secretary said he had written to Mr. Bow- cock (secretary to the Butcher's Association), inferming him that the farmers were aware that they required the butchers co-operation, and asked them for their assist- ance in the formation of the Company. He understood that the butchers intended to have a meeting. Mr. BARBER said the first thing that was necessary was for the farmers to make up their minds whether the Company was necessary. He thought at the present time they were convinced of its necessity. If they were they must not be backward in taking up shares. The more they had in the Company with a small num- ber of shares, the better it would be. When they had become shareholders they must stick to the Company and dispose of their fat stock by the Company's auc- tioneer. They must not do it half and half. (Hear, near.) They had nothing to lose and nothing to be afraid of. (Hear. hear.) He did not see why the far- mers should not co-operate and stand by their own in- terests, (4.pplause.) . _</p>
        <p>Mr. RoiLiiicE wished to know where was the weak Mot of the late Farmers Association.—Mr. Barber said the old association was too confined and was in the hands of just a few members.—Mr. Roylance considered that an evasive answer, and said he was afraid that the late association was not conducted on business lines. (Hear, hear.) He had been told by :a butcher that they were not treated with that civility which they should have received.—Mr. Lowcock said he could contradict that statement. The butchers were treated well.—The Chairman said one objection of Lancashire farmers to the new company was that the provisional directors were almostlexclusively Cheshire men .—Mr. Knowles said three out of every four of the directors would be willing to retire in favour of Lancashire farmers.—The Secretary pointed out that the present were only provisional direc- tors, it would be,for the members themselves to appoint directors.</p>
        <p>After some discussion it was decided that the duties of secretary and auctioneer to the company should be corn- bined.—The Chairman then invited those rresent to take shares in the company.—Mr. William Houghton, J.P., at d Mr. Royston both spoke in favour of the new corn- pany, and expressed their willingness to take shares. At the close of the meeting about £4OO had been sub- scribed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH IN BEWSEY-STREET.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a married woman, named Elizabeth Ann Lingard, twenty-seveniyears of age, who resid6d at 45, Forsbaw-st. died suddenly in Bewsey-st. It appears that she left home about 4 o'clock for Bank Quay Station,taking with her her two children in a bassinette, to witness the return of the Volunteers from camp. On returning home through Bewsey-st. she suddenly fell on the pavct- meat in a fit. She was carried into Dr. Adams's surgery,where she died in a few minutes. The facts of the case were communicated to the coroner, Mr. Brighouse,who, however,did not think it necessary to hold an inquest, as the cause of death was heart disease. IRON and BRASS BRDSTEADS.—GARLAND, Iron- monger, Warrington.—Advt. LADIES CYCLING COSTUMES, Gaiters, Divided Skirts, &amp;a. Special Department now open. WILLIAM HODGKIN- SON, 46 and 43, Bridge-st., Warrington.—Advt. ALL SAINTS' MISSION.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon the annual distribution of Sunday school prizes for regular attendance and good conduct took place at the above Mission. There were present—the Rev. H. V. Pigot (vicar), the Rev. H. Brierley (curate), Miss Westwell, Mr. Sudlow, the teachers, and a number of the parents, The prizes were given to the girls by Mrs. Gibson Smith and to the boys by Mr. Cherley. The attend- ance of the scholars has been exceedingly good,and about 150 children received prizes. The Vicar said that he saw no reason why every scholar should not gain a prize, and if the number of scholars in- crPased as rapidly as it had done during the past twelve months, they would soon have to enlarge the building. LADIES' CYCLING COSTUMES, Gaiters, Divided Skirts, /cc. Special Department now open. WILLIAM HODGKIN ON, 46 and 48, Bridge-st., Warrington.—Advt. YOUNG PEOPLE:6 SOCIETY OF CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR.</p>
        <p>The Sixth National Convention of the above was held in Bristol during the early days of Whit-week, when eight thousand delegates were present from all parts of the three kingdoms. It was reportei that during the past year 964 new societies had been organised,of which 160 are junior societies. The total number of societies in the three king- doms, was now 3,609, in connection with which there are 101,784 active members, 44,333 associates, and 13,764 honorary members,which altogether number nearly 166,000. Two thousand six hundred and forty-six societies report that 7,278 associates have become active members, and 9,052 active mem- bers have become members of the church during the year. Twenty-four members were elected upon the new National Council, including the Rev. W.H. Towers, president of the Alanchester and District Union, with which the Warrington and District Association is affiliated. LADIES' CYCLING COSTUMES, Gaiters, Divided Skirts</p>
        <p>&amp;e. Special Department now open. WILLIAM HODGKIN- sox, 46 and 48, Bridge-et., Warrington.—Advi. THE VACANCY AT PADGATE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Padgate Schools Committee was held in the Board-room at the Workhouse, Warring- ton, on Tuesday, for the purpose of considering the applications for the positions of superintendent, schoolmaster and matron of Padgate Schools. Mr. Davies presided, and there were also present :—Mrs. Clay, Revs. W. bioore, and T. R. Matthews, Messrs. J. Pilling, John Houghton, William Houghton, J.P., A. Jolley, A. J. Jolley, John Warburton, W. Hick- man, J. Billinge, T. B. Malkin, John Dignum, Thomas Halton, R. T. Lowe, John Johnson and J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), and H. M. Mines (superintendent of Padgate schools). There were. 32 applications and the committee divided itself into three sections, each section to select three. Die names of nine applicants were then read to the whole committee, and after some</p>
        <p>discussion and a first vote _being _taken, the number</p>
        <p>of applicants was reduced to —six. A second vote was subsequently taken, leaving three for the final selection 01 the Board at its meeting next Wednes-</p>
        <p>_ day. The clerk was instructed to write to the three following candidates to appear before the Board : Mr. and Mrs. Allen. of Hamstead, Birmingham : Mr. and Mre. Cann, of Orford Schools, Warrington; and Mr. and Mrs. Wright, of the Union Schools, Birkenhead. LADIES' CYCLING COSTUMES, Gaiters, Divided Skirts, &amp;c. Special Department now open. WILLIAM HODGKIN- SON, 46 and 43, Bridge-st., Warriugton.--ildvt.</p>
      </div>
